/* Vendored (compact) from https://github.com/ACoci86/daily-saints (MIT, (c) 2026 Alessio Coci).
 * api/v1/saints/MM-DD.json (name, era, patronage, legacy, summary, image path, wikipedia).
 * Images NOT vendored: hotlinked from https://acoci86.github.io/daily-saints (author invites embedding;
 * most images public domain, 113 under other licences — see IMAGE-CREDITS.md upstream).
 * Calendar note: upstream follows the traditional (pre-1970) calendar, so we match by SAINT NAME,
 * never by date. Exposes window.SaintsData = {name: {era, patron, legacy, summary, image, wiki, dates}}. */
window.SaintsData={"Abdon and Sennen":["3rd century",null,"Martyrs of unwavering faith and courage","Saints Abdon and Sennen were Persian dignitaries who secretly embraced Christianity during the reign of Emperor Decius. They were martyred for their faith after refusing to sacrifice to pagan gods, demonstrating unwavering devotion to Christ. Their remains were later discovered and honored in the catacombs, symbolizing their enduring legacy as witnesses to the faith.","/images/saints_abdon_and_sennen.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abdon_and_Sennen","07-30"],"Abraham and Mary his Niece":["4th century",null,"Solitary","and Saint Mary his niece († c. 375) Abraham was a rich nobleman of Edessa, born in the year 300. Ceding to his parents' desire, while still very young he married, but escaped to a cell near the city as soon as the feast was over.","/images/saint_abraham_and_saint_mary.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abraham_Kidunaia","03-16"],"Adrian and Eubulus":["Early 4th century",null,"Martyrs of steadfast faith and courage","Saints Adrian and Eubulus were martyrs who suffered during the persecution of Christians under Emperor Diocletian. They were tortured and ultimately killed for refusing to renounce their faith and sacrifice to idols. Their steadfastness in the face of persecution exemplifies the Christian call to bear one's cross and remain faithful to Christ.","/images/saints_adrian_and_eubulus.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adrian_of_Batanea","03-05"],"Aelred":["Medieval",["friendship","spirituality"],"Champion of love and spiritual friendship","Saint Aelred was a Cistercian abbot known for his profound teachings on love and friendship. He dedicated his life to monasticism, leading a community of monks and writing influential works on spirituality. His legacy emphasizes the importance of divine love and the transformative power of friendship in the pursuit of God.","/images/saint_aelred_or_alfred.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aelred_of_Rievaulx","01-12"],"Agapetus":["3rd century",null,"Courageous martyr and converter of pagans","Saint Agapetus was a young martyr who suffered greatly for his faith during the reign of Emperor Aurelian. His steadfastness under torture led to the conversion of many pagans, who were subsequently executed for their newfound beliefs. Ultimately, Agapetus was beheaded for refusing to sacrifice to Apollo, and he is remembered for his extraordinary courage and faith.","/images/saint_agapet.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agapitus_of_Palestrina","08-18"],"Agatha":["3rd century",["breast cancer patients","nurses","firefighters","cooks"],"Patroness of purity and protection","Saint Agatha was a Christian martyr from Sicily who is celebrated for her unwavering faith and commitment to chastity. She endured severe torture, including mutilation, for refusing to renounce her faith and remained devoted to Christ until her death. Her legacy continues as she is venerated for her purity and is invoked for protection against natural disasters, particularly in her hometown of Catania.","/images/saint_agatha.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agatha_of_Sicily","02-05"],"Agnes":["4th century",["virgins","young girls","rape victims"],"Symbol of purity and steadfast faith","Saint Agnes was a young Christian martyr who lived in Rome during the early 4th century. At just twelve years old, she refused to renounce her faith in Christ, enduring torture and ultimately death rather than submit to pagan practices. Her unwavering faith and purity have made her a beloved figure in the Catholic Church, symbolizing innocence and courage in the face of persecution.","/images/saint_agnes.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agnes_of_Rome","01-21"],"Agnes of Monte Pulciano":["14th century",null,"Virgin","Saint Agnes was born in Italy in 1274, a gentle future glory of the Order of Saint Dominic. Her father was an eminent Christian who dwelt in the village of Gracciano Vecchio, near the Lake of Perugia in central Italy. On the very day of her birth a first miracle announced to those present that this was a predestined child: mysterious burning torches appeared, shining brilliantly near her crib.","/images/saint_agnes_of_monte_pulciano.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agnes_of_Montepulciano","04-20"],"Albert the Great":["Medieval",["scientists","philosophers","students"],"Pioneer of science and theology integration","Albert the Great was a renowned theologian and philosopher of the 13th century, known for his extensive writings on natural sciences, philosophy, and theology. He was a mentor to Saint Thomas Aquinas and played a significant role in the development of scholasticism. Canonized in 1931 and declared a Doctor of the Church, he is celebrated for his contributions to both faith and reason.","/images/saint_albert_the_great.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Albert_the_Great","11-15"],"Albinus":["6th century",["bishops","monks","the enslaved"],"A model of virtue and charity","Saint Albinus was a bishop and abbot known for his deep piety and commitment to the monastic life. He dedicated his life to the spiritual nourishment of his community and worked tirelessly for the redemption of enslaved Christians during barbarian invasions. His legacy includes numerous miracles and a strong influence on ecclesiastical discipline in his region.","/images/saint_albinus.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Albinus_of_Angers","03-01"],"Alexius of Rome":["4th century",["the poor","married couples","abandoned spouses"],"A model of humility and devotion","Saint Alexius of Rome was a nobleman who renounced his wealth and family ties to live a life of poverty and devotion to God. After marrying a noblewoman, he left on their wedding night to seek solitude and serve the poor, eventually living at the gate of a church in Edessa. His life of humility and sacrifice remained unknown to his family until after his death, when they discovered his true identity and the miracles surrounding him.","/images/saint_alexius_of_rome.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexius_of_Rome","07-17"],"All Saints":[null,null,"Celebration of all holy men and women","All Saints' Day is a Christian feast day celebrated in honor of all the saints, known and unknown. It serves as a reminder of the communion of saints and the importance of intercession in the Christian faith. The day is observed on November 1st and is a holy day of obligation for Catholics.","/images/all_saints_day.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/All_Saints%27_Day","11-01"],"Aloysius Gonzaga":["16th century",["youth","students"," AIDS patients"],"Patron saint of youth and students","Saint Aloysius Gonzaga was a Jesuit seminarian known for his deep commitment to purity and penance. He dedicated his life to serving the sick during a fever outbreak in Rome, ultimately succumbing to the illness himself at a young age. His legacy is one of unwavering faith and dedication to God, inspiring many to pursue a life of holiness.","/images/saint_aloysius_gonzaga.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aloysius_Gonzaga","06-21"],"Alphonsus Liguori":["18th century",["theologians","confessors","moralists"],"Champion of the poor and moral theology","Saint Alphonsus Liguori was an Italian bishop and theologian known for his deep devotion and commitment to the pastoral care of the poor. He founded the Congregation of the Most Holy Redeemer and authored numerous theological works, including the influential 'The Glories of Mary.' Recognized as a Doctor of the Church, his teachings continue to inspire and guide the faithful in their spiritual lives.","/images/saint_alphonsus_liguori.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alphonsus_Liguori","08-02"],"Alphonsus Rodriguez":["16th century",["spirituality","Jesuits"],"A model of humility and service","Saint Alphonsus Rodriguez was a Spanish Jesuit known for his deep spirituality and commitment to humility and service. After experiencing personal tragedies, he dedicated his life to God and became a porter at a Jesuit college in Majorca, where he lived a life of penance and devotion. He is remembered for his profound love for Christ and his influence on fellow Jesuits, including Saint Peter Claver.","/images/saint_alphonsus_rodriguez.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alphonsus_Rodriguez","10-30"],"Ambrose":["4th century",["beekeepers","candle makers","learning","the city of Milan"],"Defender of the faith and Church doctor","Ambrose was the Bishop of Milan and a Doctor of the Church, known for his theological insights and pastoral care. He played a crucial role in the conversion of Saint Augustine and was a staunch defender of the faith against heresy. His legacy includes his eloquent preaching, liturgical contributions, and his influence on the development of Christian doctrine.","/images/saint_ambrose.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ambrose_of_Milan","12-07"],"Ambrose Leblanc":["20th century",null,"A life of joy and unwavering faith","Ambrose Leblanc was a Canadian Franciscan bishop and missionary known for his dedication to the formation of youth and his work in Japan. He faced imprisonment during World War II but maintained a spirit of joy and faith, encouraging fellow priests to celebrate Mass despite their hardships. His legacy includes the promotion of devotion to the Virgin of the Smile and a deep commitment to his faith until his untimely death in 1959.","/images/blessed_bishop_ambrose_leblanc.jpg",null,"02-13"],"Anacletus":["1st century",null,"Early Church leader and martyr","Saint Anacletus was the second pope and martyr, succeeding Saint Cletus around the year 83. He played a crucial role in the early Church by consecrating six bishops and establishing important ordinances for the consecration of bishops. Anacletus was known for his dedication to the faithful during a time of intense persecution under Emperor Domitian and was buried in the Vatican after his death on July 13, 96.","/images/saint_anacletus.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anacletus","07-13"],"Andrew":["1st century",["fishermen","scotland","greece"],"First-called disciple of Jesus Christ","Andrew was one of the first disciples of Jesus and the brother of Saint Peter. He is known for bringing Peter to Christ and for his missionary work in Scythia and Greece. Andrew was martyred on a diagonal cross, which he embraced with joy, preaching to those around him until his death.","/images/saint_andrew.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andrew_the_Apostle","11-30"],"Andrew Avellino":["16th century",["protection from sudden death","lawyers"],"Protector against sudden and unprovided death","Andrew Avellino was a Theatine priest known for his dedication to reforming a community of nuns in Naples. Despite facing violent opposition, he remained committed to his mission and was recognized for his holiness and humility. He is remembered for his deep spirituality and his ability to inspire others, particularly through his friendship with Saint Charles Borromeo.","/images/saint_andrew_avellino.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andrew_Avellino","11-10"],"Andrew Bobola":["17th century",["Poland","Jesuits","victims of religious persecution"],"Martyrdom for faith and evangelization","Andrew Bobola was a Polish Jesuit priest and martyr known for his dedicated missionary work in Lithuania during a time of great turmoil. He faced brutal persecution for his faith, ultimately leading to his martyrdom in 1657. His legacy is celebrated for his unwavering commitment to evangelization and his compassion for the suffering.","/images/saint_andrew_bobola.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andrew_Bobola","05-21"],"Andrew Corsini":["14th century",["Florence","Bishops","peacekeepers"],"Peacemaker and miracle worker of Florence","Andrew Corsini was born in Florence in 1301 and initially led a dissolute life before repenting and joining the Carmelite Order. He became known as a peacemaker and was consecrated Bishop of Fiesole, where he gained a reputation for his love of the poor and miraculous healings. Canonized in 1629, he is celebrated for his devotion and intervention in times of conflict.","/images/saint_andrew_corsini.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andrew_Corsini","02-04"],"Andrew Kim":["19th century",["Korea","Catholic priests","martyrs"],"First Korean priest and martyr","Andrew Kim was the first Korean priest and a martyr who played a crucial role in introducing Christianity to Korea. He faced immense dangers and persecution while working to bring missionaries to his homeland, ultimately sacrificing his life for his faith. His legacy inspires many in Korea and beyond, symbolizing courage and dedication to the Christian mission.","/images/saint_andrew_kim.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andrew_Kim_Taegon","09-16"],"Angela Merici":["Renaissance",["education","young girls","orphans"],"Pioneer of women's education and spirituality","Angela Merici was an Italian virgin and foundress born in 1474, known for her deep piety and commitment to educating young girls in the Christian faith. After experiencing a vision, she established the Ursuline Order in 1535, which focused on the education and spiritual formation of women. She was canonized in 1807 and is celebrated for her contributions to the Church and the empowerment of women through education.","/images/saint_angela_merici.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Angela_Merici","06-01"],"Angela of Foligno":["Medieval",["penitents","the sick","mystics"],"Inspiration for penitents and mystics","Angela of Foligno was a 13th-century mystic who experienced a profound conversion after leading a life of sin. Inspired by the ideals of Saint Francis of Assisi, she became a Franciscan tertiary and lived a life of penance and charity. Her visions and deep communion with God made her a significant figure in the Franciscan tradition, offering hope and inspiration to sinners seeking redemption.","/images/saint_angela_of_foligno.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Angela_of_Foligno","01-04"],"Anicetus":["2nd century",null,"Defender of faith against heresy","Saint Anicetus was the eleventh pope and martyr, serving during a time of significant theological challenges, particularly from Gnosticism. He is known for his friendship with Saint Polycarp and for addressing the differences in the celebration of Easter between the Eastern and Western churches. Anicetus established the tonsure for clergy and is honored for his steadfastness in faith, ultimately receiving martyrdom for his beliefs.","/images/saint_anicetus.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pope_Anicetus","04-17"],"Anna Maria Taigi":["19th century",["household workers","mothers","sick people"],"A model of humility and family devotion","Anna Maria Taigi was a devoted wife and mother who became a Trinitarian Tertiary, known for her deep spirituality and extraordinary mystical experiences. Despite the challenges of her tumultuous family life, she maintained a peaceful and loving home, raising her children with virtue and compassion. Beatified by Pope Benedict XV in 1920, she is remembered for her selfless service to others and her profound connection to God.","/images/blessed_anna_maria_taigi.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anna_Maria_Taigi","06-09"],"Anne":["1st century",["mothers","grandmothers","women in labor","housewives"],"Mother of the Blessed Virgin Mary","Saint Anne is revered as the mother of the Blessed Virgin Mary and the grandmother of Jesus Christ. She and her husband, Saint Joachim, were known for their piety and devotion to God, despite facing the challenge of childlessness for many years. Their legacy is celebrated in various sanctuaries, particularly in France and Canada, where they are honored for their role in the Holy Family.","/images/saint_anne.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Anne","07-26"],"Annunciation of the Blessed Virgin Mary and the Incarnation of the Word of God":[null,null,null,"This great festival takes its name from the happy tidings brought by the Archangel Gabriel to the Blessed Virgin, announcing the Incarnation of the Son of God. It commemorates the most important embassy that was ever known, an embassy sent by the King of kings, and performed by one of the chief princes of His heavenly court, and directed, not to the great ones of this earth, but to a poor, unknown virgin who, being endowed with angelic purity of soul and body, and perfect humility and submission to God, was greater in His eyes than the mightiest monarch in the world. When the Son of God became man, He could have taken our nature without the cooperation of any creature; but He was pleased to be born of a woman, the One announced in the third chapter of Genesis .","/images/annunciation_of_the_blessed_virgin_mary.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Annunciation","03-25"],"Anselm":["Medieval",["theologians","students","parish priests"],"Father of scholastic theology and devotion","Anselm of Canterbury was an Italian Benedictine monk who became the Archbishop of Canterbury in the late 11th century. He is renowned for his contributions to scholastic theology and for his defense of the Church's independence from secular authority. Anselm is also credited with establishing the Feast of the Immaculate Conception in the West and is considered a Doctor of the Church.","/images/saint_anselm.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anselm_of_Canterbury","04-21"],"Anthony Maria Zaccaria":["16th century",["physicians","sick","sick children"],"Renewed Christian life through education and service.","Anthony Maria Zaccaria was an Italian priest and founder of the Barnabite order, dedicated to the renewal of Christian life in the 16th century. He was known for his deep devotion to Saint Paul and his efforts to educate and care for the poor and abandoned children. Despite facing opposition, he established communities that emphasized prayer and service, leaving a lasting impact on the Church.","/images/saint_anthony_maria_zaccaria.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anthony_Maria_Zaccaria","07-05"],"Anthony Mary Claret":["19th century",["weavers","missionaries","the Catholic press"],"A champion of evangelization and education.","Anthony Mary Claret was a Spanish priest and founder of the Claretian Fathers, dedicated to evangelization and the promotion of the Catholic faith. He served as the Archbishop of Santiago in Cuba, where he established educational institutions and worked tirelessly for the spiritual and social welfare of his community. His legacy includes a profound commitment to missionary work and the written word, as he authored numerous texts aimed at inspiring others in their faith.","/images/saint_anthony-mary_claret.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anthony_Mary_Claret","10-23"],"Anthony of Padua":["Medieval",["lost items","travelers","the poor"],"Miracle-worker and beloved preacher","Anthony of Padua was a Portuguese Franciscan friar and preacher known for his powerful sermons and miracles. He became a Doctor of the Church and is often depicted holding the Child Jesus, a symbol of his deep faith and devotion. His life was marked by a commitment to teaching and helping the poor, and he is widely venerated as a patron saint of lost items and seekers.","/images/saint_anthony_of_padua.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anthony_of_Padua","06-13"],"Anthony of the Desert":["3rd century",["monks","gravediggers","animals"],"Father of Christian monasticism","Saint Anthony of the Desert, born in Upper Egypt around 251, is recognized as the father of monasticism. He renounced his wealth to live a life of solitude and prayer in the desert, where he faced numerous temptations and spiritual battles. His legacy includes the establishment of monastic communities and a profound influence on Christian asceticism.","/images/saint_anthony_of_the_desert.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anthony_of_Egypt","01-17"],"Antoninus":["15th century",["poor","clergy","Dominican Order"],"A champion of the poor and the needy.","Saint Antoninus was an Archbishop of Florence known for his deep commitment to the poor and his theological writings. He joined the Dominican Order at a young age and became renowned for his prudence and wisdom, earning the title 'Father of the Poor.' His life was marked by acts of charity, including giving away his possessions to those in need.","/images/saint_antoninus.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antoninus_of_Florence","05-10"],"Apollinaris":["2nd century",null,"Defender of the faith in early Christianity","Apollinaris was a Bishop of Hierapolis in Phrygia and a notable figure in the early Church. He is best known for his apology to Emperor Marcus Aurelius, advocating for the protection of Christians during a time of persecution. Although much of his work has been lost, he was highly regarded by early Church historians for his defense of the faith against heresies.","/images/saint_apollinaris_the_apologist.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollinaris_of_Hierapolis","01-08"],"Apollinaris of Ravenna":["1st century",["Ravenna","martyrs","sick"],"First bishop and martyr of Ravenna","Apollinaris of Ravenna was the first bishop of Ravenna and a martyr who dedicated his life to spreading Christianity. He performed numerous miracles, including healing the blind son of a soldier and curing a military tribune's wife, which helped establish a Christian community in Ravenna. Despite facing severe persecution, including torture and exile, he remained steadfast in his faith until his death in 79 AD.","/images/saint_apollinaris_of_ravenna.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollinaris_of_Ravenna","07-23"],"Apollonia":["3rd century",["dentists","toothaches","martyrs"],"Courageous witness of faith and martyrdom","Saint Apollonia was a Christian martyr from Alexandria, known for her unwavering faith during a brutal persecution of Christians in 249 AD. She was subjected to horrific torture, including having her teeth beaten out, before being led to a fire where she chose to die rather than deny Christ. Her courage and steadfastness in the face of death have made her a symbol of faith and martyrdom in the Christian tradition.","/images/saint_apollonia_virgin_and_the_martyrs_of_alexandr.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Apollonia","02-09"],"Apollonius":["2nd century",null,"Martyr","The Roman Emperor Marcus Aurelius had persecuted the Christians, but his son Commodus, who in 180 succeeded to the throne, was favorable to them, out of regard to his Empress Marcia, an admirer of the Faith. During this calm the number of the faithful greatly increased, and many persons of first rank, among them Apollonius, a Roman senator, enlisted themselves under the banner of the cross. He was already very well versed both in philosophy and Holy Scripture, as we learn from Saint Jerome, who had read and admired his discourse in the Senate of Rome on behalf of the Christian religion.","/images/saint_apollonius.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apollonius_the_Apologist","04-18"],"Arcadius":["3rd century",null,"Courageous martyr of the early Church","Saint Arcadius was a Christian martyr from Africa who suffered during a fierce persecution in the 3rd century. He courageously refused to sacrifice to pagan gods, enduring brutal torture instead. His steadfast faith and ultimate sacrifice inspired many Christians to remain faithful to God in the face of adversity.","/images/saint_arcadius.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Arcadius","01-12"],"Athanasius":["4th century",["theologians","faithfulness"],"Defender of the divinity of Christ","Saint Athanasius was a prominent bishop of Alexandria and a key defender of the Christian faith against the Arian heresy, which denied the divinity of Christ. He faced significant opposition, including multiple exiles and confrontations with four Roman emperors, yet remained steadfast in his beliefs and pastoral duties. Recognized as a Doctor of the Church, his writings and teachings have had a lasting impact on Christian theology.","/images/saint_athanasius.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Athanasius_of_Alexandria","05-02"],"Augustine":["Early Church",["theologians","printers","brewers"],"Father of Western Christianity and philosophy","Saint Augustine was a prominent theologian and philosopher who played a crucial role in the development of Western Christianity. Born in 354 in North Africa, he led a tumultuous early life before experiencing a profound conversion to Christianity at the age of 32. As the Bishop of Hippo, he became a key figure in the fight against heresy and left behind a rich legacy of writings that continue to influence Christian thought today.","/images/saint_augustine.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Augustine_of_Hippo","08-28"],"Augustine of Canterbury":["6th century",["England","missionaries"],"Apostle of England and missionary pioneer.","Augustine of Canterbury was a Benedictine monk who played a pivotal role in the Christianization of England. Appointed by Pope Gregory the Great, he led a mission to convert the Anglo-Saxons and established the Church in England. His efforts laid the foundation for the English Church, which became known for its learning and devotion to the papacy.","/images/saint_augustine_of_canterbury.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Augustine_of_Canterbury","05-28"],"Avitus":["6th century",["monks","the poor","miracles"],"Miracle worker and devoted abbot","Saint Avitus was a monk and abbot known for his deep commitment to God and service to the poor. He became the abbot of a monastery in Orleans and later lived in solitude, where he performed miracles, including restoring speech to a mute herdsman. His legacy includes the establishment of a monastery that bore his name and his reputation for intercessory prayer and miraculous deeds.","/images/saint_avitus.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Avitus","06-17"],"Bademus":["4th century",null,"Martyrdom and unwavering faith in Christ","Bademus was a wealthy citizen of Bethlapeta in Persia who renounced his possessions to follow Christ and founded a monastery. He was martyred during the persecution of Christians under King Sapor, enduring severe torture with remarkable patience. His legacy is one of unwavering faith and charity, inspiring many through his example of suffering for the glory of God.","/images/saint_bademus.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bademus","04-10"],"Barbara":["3rd century",["artillery","miners","firefighters","against sudden death"],"Protector against sudden and unprovided death","Saint Barbara was a Christian martyr who lived during the early 3rd century. She was imprisoned and tortured by her father, Dioscorus, for her faith and ultimately beheaded. She is venerated as a protector against sudden death and is especially revered in Spanish-speaking countries and the region of Metz in France.","/images/saint_barbara.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barbara_of_Nicomedia","12-04"],"Barbatus":["7th century",null,"Champion against idolatry and superstition","Saint Barbatus was a bishop of Benevento in Italy during the 7th century. Known for his piety and dedication, he worked tirelessly to eradicate idolatry and superstitions among his parishioners. His leadership and prophetic insights during a siege of the city solidified his legacy as a revered saint in the Catholic Church.","/images/saint_barbatus.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barbatus_of_Benevento","02-19"],"Barnabas":["1st century",["Cyprus","missionaries","comforters"],"Apostle of the Gentiles and martyr","Barnabas was an early Christian disciple and apostle known for his role in the spread of the Gospel. He is recognized for his partnership with Saint Paul in missionary journeys and for his efforts in establishing the Church in Antioch. Barnabas ultimately became the first bishop of Milan and was martyred for his faith around the year 61.","/images/saint_barnabas.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barnabas","06-11"],"Bartholomew":["1st century",["butchers","tanners","painters","geographers"],"Apostle known for his missionary zeal","Bartholomew, also known as Nathanael, was one of the twelve Apostles chosen by Jesus Christ. He is celebrated for his missionary work in various regions, including India and Armenia, where he ultimately faced martyrdom. His legacy is marked by his unwavering faith and dedication to spreading the Gospel, often depicted as a symbol of innocence and sincerity.","/images/saint_bartholomew.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bartholomew_the_Apostle","08-24"],"Basil the Great":["4th century",["hospital workers","monks","brewers"],"Father of monasticism in the East","Basil the Great was a prominent bishop and theologian in the early Christian Church, known for his role in combating Arianism and promoting monasticism. He founded several monasteries and a vast hospital in Caesarea, establishing a model for Christian charity and community life. His writings and teachings have had a lasting impact on Christian spirituality and liturgy, earning him the title of Doctor of the Church.","/images/saint_basil_the_great.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basil_of_Caesarea","06-14"],"Bathilde":["7th century",["slaves","the poor","hospital workers"],"A queen turned servant of the poor","Bathilde was an Englishwoman who became Queen of France after being sold into slavery. Known for her humility and virtue, she used her royal position to care for the poor and promote the Church. After her husband's death, she entered a convent, dedicating her life to prayer and service until her passing in 680.","/images/saint_bathildes.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Bathilde","01-30"],"Bede":["8th century",["scholars","historians","students"],"Father of English history and scholarship","Bede, known as the Venerable Bede, was an English monk and historian who lived during the 7th and 8th centuries. He is best known for his work 'Ecclesiastical History of the English People,' which is a key source for early English history. Bede was a prolific scholar, contributing to various fields of study, and he is revered as a Doctor of the Church.","/images/saint_bede_the_venerable.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bede","05-27"],"Benedict":["6th century",["monks","Europe","students","farmers"],"Father of Western Monasticism","Saint Benedict is known as the Father of Western Monasticism, having established the Rule of Saint Benedict, which became the foundation for monastic life in the Western Church. He fled to the desert to escape the moral decay of Rome and lived as a hermit before founding several monasteries. His life was marked by miracles and a deep commitment to prayer and community life, influencing countless monks and the development of monasticism in Europe.","/images/saint_benedict.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benedict_of_Nursia","03-21"],"Benedict Biscop":["7th century",["libraries","artists","monks"],"Pioneer of English monastic education and art","Benedict Biscop was an Anglo-Saxon abbot and a key figure in the establishment of monasticism in England during the 7th century. He made several pilgrimages to Rome, bringing back books, art, and architectural knowledge that greatly influenced English monastic life. His dedication to education and the arts helped lay the foundation for future monastic communities in Northumbria.","/images/saint_benedict_biscop.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benedict_Biscop","01-12"],"Benedict Joseph Labre":["18th century",["the homeless","pilgrims"],"A model of humility and pilgrimage","Benedict Joseph Labre was a French mendicant and pilgrim known for his deep piety and devotion to the Blessed Sacrament. After unsuccessful attempts to join various religious orders, he embraced a life of pilgrimage, visiting many Christian shrines across Europe while living in extreme poverty. He is remembered for his humility, dedication to prayer, and his service to the poor until his death in 1783.","/images/saint_benedict_joseph_labre.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benedict_Joseph_Labre","04-16"],"Benedict of Anian":["8th century",["monks","spirituality","monastic life"],"Restorer of monastic discipline and unity","Benedict of Anian was a prominent abbot and reformer of monastic life in the 8th century. He is known for restoring monastic discipline and establishing a unified rule for monks in the West, based on the Rule of Saint Benedict. His efforts significantly influenced the development of monasticism in France and Germany, leading to a revival of spiritual fervor among monks.","/images/saint_benedict_of_anian.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benedict_of_Aniane","02-12"],"Benedict of Palermo":["16th century",null,"Lay Brother","Saint Benedict the Moor, born in Sicily around 1526, belonged to the African race. His parents were Christians, living in an ancient town of Sicily today named San Fratello (Holy Little Brother), in memory of our Saint. By their virtues they possessed the liberty which belongs to all the true children of God, in whatever condition they may be.","/images/saint_benedict_of_palermo.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benedict_the_Moor","04-04"],"Benen":["5th century",null,"Patrick's beloved disciple and singer","Saint Benen, also known as Benignus, was a close disciple of Saint Patrick and played a significant role in the early Christian church in Ireland. He is celebrated for his sweet singing voice, which he used to attract many to Christianity. After serving as coadjutor bishop of Armagh, he continued to evangelize and contribute to the church until his death in 467.","/images/saint_benen.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Benen","11-09"],"Benezet":["12th century",["bridge builders","travelers"],"Patron of bridge builders and travelers","Saint Benezet was a young boy who, inspired by God, undertook the monumental task of building a bridge over the Rhone River at Avignon to prevent drowning deaths. He began the construction in 1177 and directed the work until his death in 1184, during which many miracles were reported. His body was buried on the bridge, which became a site of veneration and miracles for centuries.","/images/saint_benezet.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/B%C3%A9n%C3%A9zet","04-14"],"Benjamin":["5th century",null,"Martyrdom for faith and courage","Saint Benjamin was a deacon and martyr who lived during the early 5th century in Persia. He courageously preached the Christian faith despite severe persecution under King Isdegerdes, who sought to eradicate Christianity in his kingdom. After enduring brutal torture for his unwavering commitment to Christ, he ultimately died for his faith around the year 424.","/images/saint_benjamin.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benjamin_the_Deacon_and_Martyr","03-31"],"Bernadette Soubirous":["19th century",["sick people","poor","bodily ills"],"Visionary of Lourdes and healer of the sick","Bernadette Soubirous was a French virgin and mystic known for her visions of the Blessed Virgin Mary in Lourdes. At the age of fourteen, she experienced eighteen apparitions, during which she was instructed to reveal the healing powers of a spring that emerged at the site. She later joined the Sisters of Charity and lived a life of humility and suffering until her death at the age of 36.","/images/saint_bernadette_soubirous.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bernadette_Soubirous","02-18"],"Bernard of Clairvaux":["12th century",["beekeepers","candlemakers","the Cistercian Order"],"Father of the Cistercian Order","Bernard of Clairvaux was a prominent 12th-century abbot and theologian who founded the Cistercian monastery at Clairvaux. Renowned for his eloquence and deep spirituality, he played a significant role in the promotion of the Second Crusade and was influential in the Church during his time. His writings and teachings earned him the title of Doctor of the Church and he is remembered as a key figure in medieval monasticism.","/images/saint_bernard.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bernard_of_Clairvaux","08-20"],"Bernardine of Siena":["15th century",["advertisers","public relations","hospitality"],"A champion of the Holy Name of Jesus","Saint Bernardine of Siena was a Franciscan missionary and preacher known for his powerful oratory and devotion to the Holy Name of Jesus. Despite facing challenges, including a speech defect and accusations of heresy, he successfully reformed much of Italy through his sermons and miraculous works. He was canonized in 1450, and his legacy continues through the Feast of the Holy Name of Jesus, which he promoted.","/images/saint_bernardine_of_siena.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bernardino_of_Siena","05-20"],"Bertha":["7th century",null,"Foundress of monasteries and holy mother","Saint Bertha was a French widow and abbess born in 644. After the death of her husband, she founded a monastery in Blangy and became known for her piety and dedication to the Christian faith. She is remembered for her role in establishing religious communities and her legacy of holiness through her daughters, who were also canonized saints.","/images/saint_bertha.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bertha_of_Blangy","07-04"],"Bertilla":["7th century",null,"Exemplary leader and devoted abbess","Saint Bertilla was a 7th-century abbess known for her deep commitment to monastic life and her exemplary leadership at the Abbey of Chelles. She was initially encouraged in her vocation by Saint Owen and became known for her humility, charity, and administrative skills. Bertilla governed her community for forty-six years, attracting many to her spiritual guidance until her death in 692.","/images/saint_bertilla.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Bertille","11-05"],"Bibiana":["4th century",["virgins","martyrs"],"A symbol of unwavering faith and courage","Saint Bibiana was a Roman virgin and martyr who lived in the fourth century. She, along with her sister Demetria and their mother Dafrosa, suffered persecution for their Christian faith during the reign of Emperor Julian the Apostate. Bibiana endured severe torture and ultimately died for her beliefs, becoming a symbol of steadfast faith and courage in the face of adversity.","/images/saint_bibiana.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Bibiana","12-02"],"Blaise":["4th century",["throat diseases","physicians","animals"],"Protector against throat ailments and diseases.","Saint Blaise was a bishop and martyr known for his healing abilities and deep spirituality. After becoming a bishop in Sebaste, Armenia, he dedicated his life to serving God and helping those in need, often performing miracles. He was ultimately martyred during a persecution, and is especially invoked for protection against throat ailments.","/images/saint_blaise.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blaise_of_Sebaste","02-03"],"Bonaventure":["Medieval",["Franciscans","Bonaventure University","theologians"],"A profound theologian and spiritual leader.","Bonaventure was a 13th-century Franciscan theologian and Cardinal-Bishop, known for his deep spirituality and scholarly contributions to the Church. He served as the General of the Franciscan Order and played a significant role in the Council of Lyons, advocating for the union of the Greek and Roman Churches. His writings reflect a fervent love for God, earning him the title of the Seraphic Doctor.","/images/saint_bonaventure.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bonaventure","07-14"],"Boniface":["8th century",["Germany","missionaries","brewers"],"A pioneer of Christianity in Germany","Saint Boniface was an English missionary and bishop who played a crucial role in the Christianization of Germany during the 8th century. He faced numerous challenges, including violence and poverty, yet remained steadfast in his mission to convert pagan tribes. Ultimately, he was martyred while administering Confirmation, exemplifying his dedication to his faith and his flock.","/images/saint_boniface.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boniface","06-05"],"Brendan":["6th century",null,"Abbot of Clonfert","Saint Brendan was born in County Kerry, Ireland, in the year 484. His education was confided to Saint Ita, called the Bridget of Munster , and later to Saint Erc; the latter ordained him a priest in 512. Between the years 512 and 530 he built monastic cells at Ardfert and Shanakeel, at the foot of Brandon Hill.","/images/saint_brendan_the_navigator.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brendan_the_Navigator","05-16"],"Bridget of Sweden":["14th century",["widows","difficult marriages","Europe"],"Founder of the Order of the Holy Saviour","Bridget of Sweden was born into a royal family in 1302 and became a prominent mystic and founder of the Order of the Holy Saviour. After the death of her husband, she dedicated her life to serving the poor and received numerous revelations, including the Fifteen Prayers of Saint Bridget. She is known for her deep devotion to the Passion of Christ and her significant contributions to the spiritual life of her time.","/images/saint_bridget_of_sweden.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bridget_of_Sweden","10-08"],"Brigid of Ireland":["5th century",["Ireland","brewers","poets","children","farmers"],"Patroness of Ireland and charity","Brigid of Ireland, also known as Brigid of Kildare, was a 5th-century abbess and founder of several monasteries in Ireland. She is revered for her deep spirituality, acts of charity, and the establishment of the first convent in Ireland at Kildare. Her legacy continues to inspire many as a symbol of compassion and dedication to God.","/images/saint_bridgid.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brigid_of_Kildare","02-01"],"Brother André":["20th century",null,"Lay Brother, Miracle-Worker","Little Alfred Bessette, born at St. Grégoire, Quebec on August 9, 1845, had his roots in our own soil. The future Saint Brother Andrew of Mount Royal was a son of this land and of the French Canadian family.","/images/saint_brother_andrew_ODM.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andr%C3%A9_Bessette","03-20"],"Bruno":["Medieval",["monks","students","the Carthusian Order"],"Founder of the Carthusian Order","Saint Bruno was the founder of the Carthusian Order, known for his commitment to a life of contemplation and solitude. Born in Cologne around 1030, he became a canon and resisted the corruption of the Church before retreating to the wilderness with companions to pursue a monastic life. He died in Calabria in 1101, leaving a legacy of deep spiritual devotion and the establishment of a unique monastic community.","/images/saint_bruno.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bruno_of_Cologne","10-06"],"Cajetan of Thiena":["Renaissance",["job seekers","the unemployed","divine providence"],"Founder of the Theatine Order and reformer","Saint Cajetan of Thiena was an Italian priest and the founder of the Theatines, a religious order dedicated to reforming the clergy and promoting the sacraments. He is known for his deep devotion to the Blessed Virgin Mary and for introducing the practice of Forty Hours' Adoration of the Blessed Sacrament. Cajetan dedicated his life to serving the poor and the sick, leaving a lasting legacy of charity and spiritual renewal in the Church.","/images/saint_cajetan_of_thiena.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cajetan_of_Thiene","08-07"],"Callistus I":["3rd century",["cemeteries","martyrs"],"Pope, martyr, and protector of the faithful","Callistus I was a Pope and martyr in the early third century who played a significant role in the administration of the Church and the care of Christian cemeteries in Rome. He is known for instituting the Ember Days fast and overseeing the construction of churches during a time of persecution. His leadership led to many conversions, but he ultimately faced martyrdom himself, being killed for his faith.","/images/saint_callistus_i.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Callistus_I","10-14"],"Camillus of Lellis":["16th century",["sick","nurses","hospital workers"],"Compassionate servant of the sick","Saint Camillus of Lellis was the founder of the Servants of the Sick, a religious order dedicated to caring for the ill and needy. After a tumultuous youth marked by gambling and military service, he experienced a profound conversion that led him to dedicate his life to serving the sick. He is remembered for his compassion and tireless efforts in hospitals, especially during times of famine and plague.","/images/saint_camillus_of_lellis.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Camillus_de_Lellis","07-18"],"Canute":["11th century",["Denmark","rulers","mariners"],"A martyr king devoted to justice and faith.","Canute, King of Denmark, was a pious and just ruler who reigned in the 11th century. He is known for his military successes and efforts to spread Christianity in his kingdom. Ultimately, he was martyred during a rebellion, demonstrating his unwavering faith and commitment to his people.","/images/saint_canutus.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canute_the_Great","01-19"],"Casimir":["15th century",["Poland","young people","virgins","sick","students"],"Model of purity and devotion to God","Casimir was a Polish prince known for his deep piety and commitment to a life of virtue. He chose to remain a virgin and devoted himself to prayer and charity, even in the face of illness. After his death, he was venerated for the miracles attributed to him, including healing the sick and raising the dead.","/images/saint_casimir.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Casimir","03-04"],"Catherine Labouré":["19th century",["the Miraculous Medal","sick people","sisters of charity"],"Visionary of the Miraculous Medal","Catherine Labouré was a French nun of the Daughters of Charity, known for her visions of the Virgin Mary. In 1830, she received a vision that inspired the creation of the Miraculous Medal, which has since become a powerful symbol of faith and devotion. Her life and work emphasize the importance of prayer and the intercession of Mary in the lives of the faithful.","/images/the_miraculous_medal.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catherine_Labour%C3%A9","11-27,11-28"],"Catherine of Alexandria":["4th century",["students","young girls","philosophers","apologists"],"Patroness of students and young girls","Catherine of Alexandria was a noble virgin and martyr from the fourth century who is celebrated for her intelligence and eloquence in defending the Christian faith against pagan philosophers. She famously debated the emperor Maximinus II and converted many of his followers before enduring torture and execution for her steadfastness. Her legacy endures as a symbol of faith and courage, and she is venerated as the patroness of students and young girls.","/images/saint_catherine_of_alexandria.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catherine_of_Alexandria","11-25"],"Catherine of Genoa":["Renaissance",["sick people","hospital workers","the dying"],"Devotion to the suffering souls in purgatory","Catherine of Genoa was a noblewoman who dedicated her life to God after enduring a difficult marriage. She is known for her deep spirituality, particularly her devotion to the souls in purgatory, and her rigorous penance, which included living on Holy Communion alone for many years. Catherine also served the sick and poor in her community, eventually managing a large hospital in Genoa.","/images/saint_catherine_of_genoa.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catherine_of_Genoa","09-15"],"Catherine of Ricci":["16th century",["Holy Souls","sick people","against bodily ills"],"Mystic devoted to the Passion of Christ","Catherine of Ricci was a 16th-century Italian virgin and mystic known for her deep devotion to the Passion of Christ. She experienced numerous mystical visions and received the stigmata, enduring intense suffering for the sake of the Holy Souls in purgatory. Her life was marked by extraordinary charity and intercession, leading many to seek her prayers after death.","/images/saint_catherine_of_ricci.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catherine_de%27_Ricci","02-13"],"Catherine of Siena":["14th century",["Italy","nurses","against fire","people ridiculed for their faith"],"Mystic and reformer of the Church","Catherine of Siena was a 14th-century mystic and a prominent figure in the Catholic Church, known for her deep spirituality and commitment to reforming the Church. She played a crucial role in bringing Pope Gregory XI back to Rome from Avignon and was a counselor to several popes during a tumultuous period in Church history. Her life was marked by intense prayer, visions, and a dedication to serving God and the Church, ultimately leading to her canonization and recognition as a Doctor of the Church.","/images/saint_catherine_of_siena.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catherine_of_Siena","04-30"],"Catherine of St. Augustine":["17th century",null,"Virgin","A young future missionary to New France, Catherine de Longpré, in religion Sister Marie-Catherine of Saint Augustine, was a nursing nun in the community of the Hospitaler Sisters of Saint Augustine in Evreux. Born in France in 1632, she went to Quebec at the age of sixteen. Having offered her life for the sick and the sanctification of souls, she found in Quebec City a newly-established and very poor hospital, where she would labor for twenty years with unfailing devotion and courage.","/images/blessed_catherine_of_st_augustine.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catherine_of_St._Augustine","04-12"],"Catherine of Sweden":["14th century",["widows","pilgrims","against temptations"],"Devoted life of pilgrimage and penance","Catherine of Sweden was the daughter of Saint Bridget and a devoted religious figure known for her piety and commitment to God. After the death of her husband, she dedicated her life to pilgrimage, charity, and severe penance, often visiting the tombs of martyrs. She entered a monastery and lived a life of devotion until her death in 1381.","/images/saint_catherine_of_sweden.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catherine_of_Vadstena","03-22"],"Cecilia":["2nd century",["musicians","poets","church music","composers"],"Patroness of musicians and martyrs.","Saint Cecilia is revered as a virgin martyr who lived during the early Christian era. She is known for her unwavering faith and commitment to her vow of virginity, even in the face of persecution. Her legacy endures as the patroness of musicians, reflecting her association with music and the arts.","/images/saint_cecilia.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cecilia_of_Rome","11-22"],"Celestine":["Early 5th century",null,"Defender of faith and mercy","Saint Celestine I was a Pope from 422 to 432, known for his strong stance against heresy. He condemned the teachings of Nestorius and sent missionaries to Britain and Ireland to combat the spread of Pelagianism. His papacy emphasized the importance of repentance and the mercy of God towards sinners.","/images/saint_celestine_i.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Celestine_I","04-06"],"Charbel Makhlouf":["19th century",["healers","the sick","Lebanon"],"A model of prayer and healing","Charbel Makhlouf was a Maronite priest and hermit from Lebanon known for his deep spirituality and miraculous healings. He lived a life of extreme asceticism, dedicating himself to prayer and solitude in a hermitage for over two decades. After his death, numerous miracles attributed to him have drawn many pilgrims to his tomb, solidifying his legacy as a powerful intercessor.","/images/saint_charbel_makhlouf.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charbel_Makhlouf","12-24"],"Charles Borromeo":["16th century",["catechists","spiritual leaders","seminarians"],"Champion of church reform and education","Saint Charles Borromeo was an influential Archbishop of Milan during the Counter-Reformation, known for his commitment to church reform and education. He played a significant role in the Council of Trent and worked tirelessly to implement its decrees, establishing numerous schools and seminaries. His dedication to his flock during times of plague and personal sacrifice exemplified his deep pastoral care and holiness.","/images/saint_charles_borromeo.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_Borromeo","11-04"],"Christina":["3rd century",["martyrs","difficult situations"],"Defender of faith against persecution","Saint Christina was a young virgin and martyr who defied her pagan father by destroying his idols and distributing their pieces to the poor. She endured severe tortures for her faith, including being thrown into a lake with a heavy stone, from which she was miraculously rescued by an angel. Ultimately, she was martyred by arrows, and her relics are venerated in Palermo, Sicily.","/images/saint_christina.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Christina_of_Bolsena","07-24"],"Christopher":["3rd century",["travelers","drivers","children"],"Protector of travelers and children","Saint Christopher is a revered martyr known for his extraordinary faith and courage in the face of persecution. He is traditionally believed to have converted thousands to Christianity through his preaching and miraculous acts. His martyrdom is celebrated on July 25, commemorating his steadfastness and the miracles attributed to him, including healing and protection.","/images/saint_christopher.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Christopher","07-25"],"Chrysanthus":["3rd century",["martyrs","converts"],"Martyrdom and faith in adversity","Chrysanthus was a young Roman martyr who, along with his wife Daria, converted many to Christianity through their faith and teachings. Despite facing severe persecution from his father and the Roman authorities, he remained steadfast in his beliefs. Ultimately, both were martyred by being buried alive for their refusal to renounce their faith.","/images/saint_chrysanthus_and_saint_daria.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chrysanthus_and_Daria","10-25"],"Clare of Assisi":["13th century",["television","eye disease","goldsmiths"],"Pioneer of female monastic life","Clare of Assisi was a 13th-century Italian saint and the founder of the Order of Poor Ladies, known today as the Poor Clares. Inspired by Saint Francis, she dedicated her life to poverty and prayer, establishing a monastic community that emphasized simplicity and devotion to God. Despite her frail health, she is remembered for her miraculous defense of her convent against an invading army through her faith and the Blessed Sacrament.","/images/saint_clare_of_assisi.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clare_of_Assisi","08-12"],"Clare of Montefalco":["Medieval",["heart disease","the sick","nuns"],"A model of devotion and compassion","Clare of Montefalco was an Italian virgin and abbess known for her deep spirituality and dedication to the Passion of Christ. She led a community of nuns and was recognized for her wisdom, miraculous healings, and prophetic gifts. Her heart was said to bear the image of the Crucified Savior, symbolizing her profound connection to Christ's suffering.","/images/saint_clare_of_montefalco.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clare_of_Montefalco","08-18"],"Claude":["7th century",null,"Model of monastic and pastoral dedication","Saint Claude was an influential archbishop and abbot in the early medieval period, known for his deep spirituality and dedication to the monastic life. He played a significant role in the formation of clergy and was recognized for his powerful sermons that inspired moral transformation. His legacy includes a commitment to prayer, humility, and the nurturing of a devout community, which continued to flourish long after his death.","/images/saint_claude.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Claude_of_Besan%C3%A7on","06-07"],"Clement Mary Hofbauer":["18th century",["Vienna","bakers","the Redemptorists"],"Champion of education and pastoral care","Clement Mary Hofbauer was a Redemptorist priest known for his dedication to education and pastoral care in Austria and Poland. He founded an orphanage and a school for boys in Warsaw, significantly contributing to the spiritual and educational landscape of the region. His efforts in promoting the Catholic faith and supporting the youth earned him recognition as a key figure in the Church during his time.","/images/saint_clement_mary_hofbauer.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clement_Mary_Hofbauer","03-15"],"Clement of Rome":["1st century",["mariners","sick children","falsely accused"],"Early Church leader and martyr","Clement of Rome was a prominent early Christian leader and the fourth pope, succeeding Saint Linus. He is known for his efforts to restore peace and order within the Church, particularly through his influential Epistle to the Corinthians. His leadership during a time of persecution and internal strife solidified his legacy as a martyr and a key figure in the early Church.","/images/saint_clement_i_of_rome.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clement_of_Rome","11-23"],"Cletus and Marcellinus":["4th century",null,"Popes and Martyrs","Saint Cletus was the third Bishop of Rome, succeeding to Saint Linus, which circumstance alone commends his eminent virtue among the first disciples of Saint Peter in the West. A church and a hospital founded by him, though ruined and rebuilt several times, survived until the 18 th century, and the memory of his charity was so well conserved by the Romans, that the Crucifers who then were still serving in his hospice, considered him their founder. Saint Cletus was martyred after the peaceful reign of the Emperor Titus, when Domitian replaced him; the date was April 26 th of the year 89.","/images/saint_clet.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pope_Anacletus","04-26"],"Clotilda":["6th century",["queens","converts","France"],"Spiritual mother of Catholic France","Saint Clotilda was the queen consort of Clovis I, the first King of the Franks, and played a crucial role in his conversion to Christianity. Her piety and dedication to her faith inspired her husband and contributed to the establishment of Christianity in France. After Clovis's death, she devoted her life to prayer and charity, becoming a significant figure in the early Church.","/images/saint_clotilda.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clotilde","06-03"],"Cloud":["6th century",["hermits","monks"],"A model of monastic devotion and charity.","Saint Cloud, also known as Clodoald, was a Merovingian prince who renounced his royal heritage to live a life of piety and service to God. After witnessing the tragic deaths of his brothers, he dedicated himself to monastic life and became a revered spiritual leader. He founded a monastery in Nogent, where he guided many in their faith until his death in 560.","/images/saint_cloud_or_clodoald.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clodoald","09-07"],"Colette":["15th century",["poor people","women","sick children"],"Reformer of the Poor Clares and mystic","Saint Colette was a 15th-century virgin and reformer of the Poor Clares, known for her austere lifestyle and dedication to prayer. After receiving a vision from Saint Francis, she established a significant reform of her order, founding seventeen convents across Europe. Colette played a crucial role in healing the schism within the Church and was known for her intense spiritual battles against demonic forces.","/images/saint_colette.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colette_of_Corbie","03-06"],"Columba":["6th century",["difficult births","poets","navigators"],"Apostle of the Picts and founder of Iona","Saint Columba, born in 521 in Ireland, is renowned as the apostle of the Picts in Scotland. He founded numerous monasteries, most notably on the island of Iona, where he established a significant center for Christian learning and missionary work. His life was marked by a deep commitment to prayer, charity, and the conversion of souls, earning him widespread veneration and respect.","/images/saint_columba_or_columkille.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Columba","06-09"],"Columban":["6th century",["monks","missionaries","motorcyclists"],"Pioneer of monasticism in Europe","Columban was an Irish abbot and missionary who played a significant role in spreading Christianity across Europe during the early Middle Ages. He founded several monasteries, most notably Luxeuil in France and Bobbio in Italy, which became centers of learning and spirituality. Known for his strong defense of the Faith and moral integrity, he faced opposition from powerful nobles but remained steadfast in his mission until his death in 615.","/images/saint_columbanus.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Columbanus","11-21"],"Conrad of Piacenza":["14th century",["hernias","penitents"],"A life of penance and repentance","Saint Conrad of Piacenza was a nobleman who transformed his life after a tragic incident caused by his hunting activities. Stricken with remorse for the wrongful condemnation of an innocent man, he sold all his possessions to repay those affected and dedicated himself to a life of penance. He became a hermit, living in strict austerity and was known for his prophetic gifts and miracles until his death in 1351.","/images/saint_conrad_of_piacenza.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conrad_of_Piacenza","02-19"],"Cosmas and Damian":["3rd century",["physicians","surgeons","barbers","against plague"],"Patrons of physicians and medical workers","Saints Cosmas and Damian were twin brothers and physicians who lived in the 3rd century. They are known for their unwavering Christian faith and their practice of medicine without accepting payment, earning them great respect among the people. They were martyred during the persecution of Diocletian for their refusal to renounce their faith and for continuing to heal in the name of Christ.","/images/saint_cosmas_and_saint_damian.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cosmas_and_Damian","09-27"],"Crispin and Crispinian":["3rd century",["shoemakers","leather workers","bravery"],"Martyrs of faith and perseverance","Crispin and Crispinian were brothers and martyrs who preached Christianity in Gaul during the 3rd century. They were known for their shoemaking skills and their ability to convert many through their affable demeanor and profound faith. Ultimately, they suffered severe tortures and were executed for their beliefs under the orders of Emperor Diocletian around the year 286.","/images/saints_crispin_and_crispinian.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crispin_and_Crispinian","10-25"],"Cunegundes":["10th century",["difficult marriages","widows","the poor"],"Symbol of faith and devotion in marriage","Saint Cunegund was a 10th-century princess and the wife of Holy Roman Emperor Henry II. She is known for her piety and dedication to the Church, often engaging in charitable works and supporting the poor. Cunegund was canonized in 1200, and her legacy continues as a symbol of faith and devotion.","/images/saint_cunegundes.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cunigunde_of_Luxembourg","03-03"],"Cyprian":["3rd century",["North Africa","clergy","martyrs"],"A pillar of faith and martyrdom.","Saint Cyprian was a prominent Bishop of Carthage and a martyr who lived during the 3rd century. He was initially a pagan but converted to Christianity and became a significant leader during a time of persecution. His steadfastness in faith and leadership during crises, including a plague and the Decian persecution, left a lasting impact on the early Church.","/images/saint_cyprian.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyprian_of_Carthage","09-16,09-26"],"Cyriacus":["Early 4th century",["exorcists","healers","the possessed"],"Patron of exorcists and healers","Saint Cyriacus was a deacon in Rome who dedicated his life to helping the poor and imprisoned Christians during the reign of Emperor Diocletian. He is known for performing miraculous healings, including exorcising demons from the emperor's daughter and the daughter of the king of Persia, leading to many conversions. Ultimately, he was martyred for his faith along with his companions, refusing to profit from the gifts of God he shared with others.","/images/saint_cyriacus_and_his_companions.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyriacus","08-08"],"Cyril":[null,null,"Child Martyr","while still a boy at Caesarea in Cappadocia, suffered during the persecutions of the third century. He had been secretly instructed in Christianity and would repeat the name of Christ at all times, confessing that the mere utterance of this Name was beneficial. For this, his pagan father, plunged in the superstitions of paganism, made him suffer all kinds of bad treatment.","/images/saint_cyril_boy-martyr.jpg",null,"05-29"],"Cyril and Methodius":["9th century",["Slavic peoples","linguists","educators"],"Pioneers of Slavic literacy and Christianity","Saints Cyril and Methodius were brothers from Thessalonica who became known as the Apostles to the Slavic nations. They created the Glagolitic alphabet, which allowed for the translation of the Bible and other texts into the Slavic languages. Their missionary work significantly contributed to the spread of Christianity in Eastern Europe during the 9th century.","/images/saints_cyril_and_methodius.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyril_and_Methodius","07-07"],"Cyril of Alexandria":["5th century",["theologians","students","Egypt"],"Defender of the Incarnation and Theotokos","Cyril of Alexandria was a prominent theologian and the Patriarch of Alexandria in the early 5th century. He played a crucial role in the Christological controversies of his time, particularly opposing Nestorianism and advocating for the title Theotokos for Mary. His writings and theological insights significantly shaped Christian doctrine and earned him the title of Doctor of the Church.","/images/saint_cyril_of_alexandria.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyril_of_Alexandria","02-09"],"Cyril of Jerusalem":["4th century",null,"Defender of faith and catechesis","Cyril of Jerusalem was a prominent bishop and theologian in the early Church, known for his catechetical lectures that laid the groundwork for Christian instruction on the Creed and Sacraments. He faced persecution during his episcopate but remained steadfast in his faith, famously opposing the attempts to rebuild the Jewish temple under Emperor Julian. Cyril's legacy endures through his writings and his role in the defense of orthodoxy against heresy.","/images/saint_cyril_of_jerusalem.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyril_of_Jerusalem","03-18"],"Damasus":["4th century",null,"Promoter of the Vulgate and Church unity","Saint Damasus I served as Pope from 366 to 384 and is known for his efforts to combat Arianism and Apollinarianism. He commissioned Saint Jerome to produce the Vulgate, a significant Latin translation of the Bible. Damasus also worked to beautify the Church and honor the martyrs, leaving a lasting legacy in the history of the Catholic Church.","/images/saint_damasus.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Damasus_I","12-11"],"David":["6th century",["Wales","vegetarians","dairy workers"],"Patron saint of Wales and monastic life","Saint David was an influential archbishop and the patron saint of Wales, known for his piety and dedication to God. He founded several monasteries, including the principal one at Glyn Rhosyn (now St Davids), where he led a strict monastic life. His teachings and miracles helped combat heresy in Britain during the early sixth century, and he is celebrated for his humility and spiritual leadership.","/images/saint_david_of_wales.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_of_Wales","03-01"],"Delphinus":["5th century",null,"Defender of truth and orthodoxy","Saint Delphinus was a Bishop of Bordeaux known for his strong defense of orthodox Christian teachings during the early Church. He played a significant role in condemning heretical movements, particularly at the Council of Saragossa in 380. His influence extended to notable figures such as Saint Paulinus, whom he baptized and inspired towards a life of holiness.","/images/saint_delphinus.jpg",null,"12-24"],"Didace Pelletier":["17th century",["lay brothers","carpenters"],"First Canadian-born Lay Brother of Recollets","Didace Pelletier, known as Blessed Brother Didace, was the first Canadian-born Lay Brother of the Recollets in New France. He was a skilled carpenter who contributed significantly to the construction of various missions and churches in Canada. His life of piety and dedication to his work left a lasting legacy of sanctity in Canadian history.","/images/blessed_brother_didace_pelletier.jpg",null,"02-21"],"Didacus":["15th century",["hospitals","the sick","Canary Islands"],"A model of humility and service","Saint Didacus, born in Andalusia in the early 15th century, was a Franciscan confessor known for his deep spirituality and commitment to serving others. He dedicated his life to prayer, fasting, and caring for the sick, often performing miracles through his intercession. He died in 1463, and his incorrupt body became a source of veneration, leading to his canonization in 1588.","/images/saint_didacus_or_diego.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Didacus","11-13"],"Dionysius the Areopagite":["1st century",["France","philosophers","theologians"],"Pioneer of Christianity in Gaul","Dionysius the Areopagite was a prominent early Christian missionary and the first bishop of Athens, converted by Saint Paul. He played a significant role in spreading Christianity in Gaul, particularly in Paris, where he established several churches. He is remembered as a martyr, having been executed for his faith around the year 117.","/images/saint_dionysius_the_areopagite.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dionysius_the_Areopagite","10-09"],"Dominic":["Medieval",["astronomers","the Dominican Republic","philosophers"],"Founder of the Dominican Order and the Rosary","Saint Dominic de Guzman was a Spanish priest and the founder of the Order of Preachers, commonly known as the Dominicans. He dedicated his life to combating heresy and promoting the Catholic faith, famously receiving the Rosary from the Virgin Mary. His legacy includes the establishment of a religious order that emphasized preaching, education, and the importance of prayer.","/images/saint_dominic_de_guzman.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dominic_de_Guzman","08-04"],"Dominic Savio":["19th century",[" choirboys"," juvenile delinquents"," students"],"Inspiring youth through faith and joy","Dominic Savio was a young Italian student and a member of the Salesian Society, known for his deep commitment to his faith and his desire for holiness from a young age. He was a close associate of Saint John Bosco and founded the Immaculate Conception Sodality to promote faith among his peers. Despite his short life, he is remembered for his cheerful disposition and dedication to helping others grow in their faith.","/images/saint_dominic_savio.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dominic_Savio","05-06"],"Dominic of Silos":["11th century",["prisoners","captives","the Order of Our Lady of Ransom"],"Restorer of the monastic life","Saint Dominic of Silos was an 11th-century Benedictine abbot known for his deep spirituality and commitment to restoring his monastery. He was renowned for his miraculous healings and charitable acts, particularly towards those suffering and the captives held by the Moors. His legacy includes the inspiration for the Order of Our Lady of Ransom, which was founded to aid Christians in captivity.","/images/saint_dominic_of_silos.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dominic_of_Silos","12-20"],"Donatian and Rogatian":["3rd century",null,"Martyrs of faith and brotherhood","Donatian and Rogatian were noble brothers from Nantes who lived during the late 3rd century. They were martyred for their Christian faith, with Donatian actively converting others and Rogatian expressing a desire for baptism. Both brothers faced brutal torture and execution, ultimately dying for their unwavering commitment to Christ.","/images/saints_donatian_and_rogatian.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donatian_and_Rogatian","05-24"],"Dorothy":["Early 4th century",["gardeners","florists","virgins"],"Martyrdom and miraculous gifts of flowers","Saint Dorothy was a young virgin and martyr from Caesarea of Cappadocia, known for her unwavering faith during the Diocletian persecution. She faced torture and execution with joy, inspiring others to return to Christianity through her steadfastness. Her legacy includes the miraculous delivery of apples and roses to her persecutor, symbolizing her connection to the divine and her role as a patron of gardeners and florists.","/images/saint_dorothy.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dorothea_of_Caesarea","02-06"],"Edmund":["Medieval",["archbishops","teachers","students"],"Defender of Church rights and charity","Saint Edmund of Canterbury was an English archbishop known for his piety and dedication to the Church. He defended the rights of the Church against the greed of King Henry III and faced persecution for his steadfastness. Canonized shortly after his death, he is remembered for his miracles and his commitment to a life of prayer and charity.","/images/saint_edmund.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edmund_of_East_Anglia","11-16"],"Edward the Confessor":["11th century",["difficult marriages","peace","England"],"A holy king known for peace and charity.","Edward the Confessor was King of England from 1042 until his death in 1066. Known for his piety and commitment to the Church, he ruled with a focus on peace and charity, earning the affection of his subjects. His reign is marked by the rebuilding of churches, including Westminster Abbey, and he was canonized in 1161 due to the miracles attributed to him after his death.","/images/saint_edward_iii.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edward_the_Confessor","10-13"],"Eleutherius":["6th century",null,"A model of humility and prayer","Saint Eleutherius was a sixth-century abbot known for his simplicity and deep spirit of compunction. He presided over Saint Mark's monastery near Spoleto and was favored with the gift of miracles, including the healing of a child from demonic possession. After resigning his abbacy, he died in Rome, leaving a legacy of humility and devotion to God.","/images/saint_eleutherius.jpg",null,"09-06"],"Eligius":["7th century",["goldsmiths","metalworkers","farmers","poor","jewelers"],"Patron of goldsmiths and the poor","Eligius was a skilled metalworker and goldsmith who became known for his piety and charity. Appointed as the royal goldsmith by King Clotaire II, he dedicated his life to serving the poor and creating beautiful reliquaries for saints. Later, he became the Bishop of Noyon, where he continued his good works and was known for performing miracles.","/images/saint_eligius_or_eloy.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Eligius","12-01"],"Elizabeth of Hungary":["Medieval",["poor","hospital workers","nursing","bakers"],"A model of charity and humility","Elizabeth of Hungary was a princess known for her deep compassion and dedication to the poor. Despite her royal status, she chose a life of service, building hospitals and caring for the sick. After the death of her husband, she faced great adversity but continued to live a life of humility and charity until her death at a young age.","/images/saint_elizabeth_of_hungary.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elizabeth_of_Hungary","11-19"],"Elizabeth of Portugal":["Medieval",["peace","charitable works","difficult marriages"],"A model of charity and peace-making","Elizabeth of Portugal was born in 1271 and became the queen consort of King Dennis of Portugal. Known for her deep faith and charitable works, she dedicated her life to serving the poor and mediating peace within her family. After her husband's death, she embraced a life of austerity and devotion, ultimately being recognized as a saint for her virtuous life.","/images/saint_elizabeth_of_portugal.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elizabeth_of_Portugal","07-08"],"Elphege":["11th century",null,"Archbishop of Canterbury","Saint Elphege was born in the year 954, of a noble Saxon family. He became a monk in the monastery of Deerhurst, near Tewkesbury, England, and afterwards lived as a hermit near Bath, where he founded a community under the rule of Saint Benedict and became its first abbot. At thirty years of age he was chosen Bishop of Winchester, and twenty-two years later became Archbishop of Canterbury.","/images/saint_elphege.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%86lfheah_of_Canterbury","04-19"],"Emily de Vialar":["19th century",["sick","educators","women"],"Pioneer of charity and education for all.","Emily de Vialar was a French foundress who established the Congregation of the Sisters of Saint Joseph of the Apparition in the 19th century. She dedicated her life to the education of children and the care of the sick, particularly in response to the devastation caused by the French Revolution. Canonized in 1951, her legacy includes numerous foundations of her order across the globe, emphasizing charity and service.","/images/saint_emily_de_vialar.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emily_de_Vialar","08-24"],"Engratia and her Companions":["4th century",null,"Martyrs","The church celebrates on this day the triumph of Saint Engratia, a virgin martyr who was a native of Portugal. Her father had promised her in marriage to a man of quality in Rousillon, Gaul, and to accompany her there, he sent as her escort for the marriage her uncle Lupercius and a brilliant suite of sixteen other noblemen, as well as a servant named Julie. When they arrived at Saragossa, she learned of the horrible massacre of Christians being carried on at that time, and of the torments they were enduring at the hand of Dacian, who governed that region in the name of the emperors Diocletian and Maximian.","/images/saint_engratia.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Engratia","04-16"],"Ephrem":["4th century",["deacons","spiritual directors","theology"],"A revered Doctor of the Church","Saint Ephrem was a prominent early Christian theologian and poet, known for his deep humility and profound spirituality. He served as a deacon in Edessa, where he preached and wrote extensively, earning the title 'Harp of the Holy Spirit' for his eloquent expressions of faith. Declared a Doctor of the Church, his works continue to inspire Christians with their insights into Scripture and the nature of God.","/images/saint_ephrem.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ephrem_the_Syrian","06-18"],"Epiphanius":["4th century",["theologians","monks","Cyprus"],"Champion of monastic life and charity","Saint Epiphanius was born around 315 in Palestine and became a prominent Christian leader and theologian. He served as the Archbishop of Salamis in Cyprus and was known for his deep commitment to monastic life, charity, and the study of Scripture. His legacy includes a strong emphasis on the importance of labor in faith and a reputation for sanctity that protected him from persecution during his lifetime.","/images/saint_epiphanius.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Epiphanius_of_Salamis","05-12"],"Etheldreda":["7th century",["difficult marriages","abbesses","Ely"],"A model of devotion and perseverance","Saint Etheldreda, also known as Audrey, was a 7th-century queen and abbess who dedicated her life to God. After enduring two marriages against her will, she founded a double monastery at Ely, where she served as its first abbess. Her life was marked by asceticism and devotion, and she is remembered for her incorrupt relics and the miracles attributed to her after death.","/images/saint_etheldreda.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Etheldreda","06-23"],"Eucherius":["8th century",null,"Defender of the Church's rights","Eucherius was a Bishop of Orleans known for his deep commitment to God and his virtuous life. After spending years in a monastery, he was reluctantly elected to lead the diocese, where he became a staunch defender of the Church's rights against secular encroachments. His legacy is marked by his dedication to prayer and contemplation, even in the face of adversity.","/images/saint_eucherius.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eucherius_of_Orl%C3%A9ans","02-20"],"Eugenius":["5th century",null,"A steadfast leader during persecution","Eugenius was a bishop of Carthage who served during a time of intense persecution against Catholics under the Vandal kings. He was known for his piety, learning, and commitment to the poor, often sacrificing his own comforts to aid those in need. After enduring exile and hardship, he died in a monastery in France, where many miracles were reported at his tomb.","/images/saint_eugenius.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eugenius_of_Carthage","07-13"],"Eulalia":["Early 4th century",["Merida","young people","martyrs"],"Courageous martyr and patroness of Merida","Saint Eulalia was a young Christian martyr from Merida, Spain, who bravely faced persecution during the reign of Emperor Diocletian. At just twelve years old, she refused to renounce her faith, enduring horrific tortures before ultimately succumbing to her injuries. Her legacy as a symbol of courage and faith continues to inspire many, and she is venerated as the patroness of Merida.","/images/saint_eulalia.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eulalia_of_M%C3%A9rida","12-10"],"Eulogius of Alexandria":["6th century",null,"Defender of orthodoxy and faith","Saint Eulogius of Alexandria was a Syrian monk who became the Patriarch of Alexandria in the late 6th century. He was known for his commitment to orthodoxy and his efforts against heresies, particularly the Eutychian heresy that troubled the Church at the time. Eulogius maintained a close friendship with Saint Gregory the Great and composed many works defending the faith before his death in 606.","/images/saint_eulogius_of_alexandria.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eulogius_of_Alexandria","09-13"],"Eulogius of Cordoba":["9th century",["martyrs","Christian converts"],"Defender of faith and Christian martyr","Eulogius of Cordoba was a 9th-century Christian martyr and priest known for his staunch defense of the faith during the period of Muslim rule in Spain. He was executed for his outspoken criticism of Islam and his efforts to convert Muslims to Christianity. His writings and martyrdom have made him a significant figure in the history of Christian resistance in the Iberian Peninsula.","/images/saint_eulogius.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eulogius_of_C%C3%B3rdoba","03-11"],"Euphrasia":["Early 5th century",null,"Model of humility and charity","Saint Euphrasia was a virgin and nun born in Constantinople, known for her deep commitment to a life of prayer and charity. After refusing an arranged marriage, she dedicated her wealth to the poor and became a model of humility and obedience. She was recognized for her miraculous healings and her power over demons, leaving a legacy of faith and devotion.","/images/saint_euphrasia.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euphrasia_of_Constantinople","03-13"],"Eusebius of Rome":["3rd century",null,"Courage and fidelity in faith","Eusebius of Rome was a priest and martyr who lived during the time of Christian persecution in the Roman Empire. He is remembered for his unwavering faith and courage in the face of torture and execution. His martyrdom exemplifies the strength and fidelity of early Christians, inspiring many to endure suffering for their beliefs.","/images/saint_eusebius_of_rome.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eusebius_of_Rome","08-14"],"Eusebius of Vercelli":["4th century",null,"Defender of the Nicene faith and clergy","Eusebius of Vercelli was a bishop known for his staunch opposition to Arianism and his efforts to promote a zealous clergy. He was exiled multiple times for his beliefs but continued to advocate for the Nicene Creed and the teachings of the Gospel. His legacy includes the integration of monastic life with clerical duties, influencing the structure of the Church in the West.","/images/saint_eusebius_of_vercelli.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eusebius_of_Vercelli","12-16"],"Eustace":["2nd century",["hunters","against hesitancy","family"],"A symbol of faith and family unity","Saint Eustace, originally named Placidus, was a Roman officer who converted to Christianity after a miraculous vision involving a deer and a cross. He endured significant trials, including the loss of his family and possessions, yet remained steadfast in his faith. Ultimately, he and his family were martyred for refusing to sacrifice to pagan gods, and their story inspired many to believe in Christ.","/images/saint_eustachius_and_his_family.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Eustace","09-20"],"Evaristus":["1st century",null,"Pioneering leader of the early Church","Saint Evaristus was a Pope and martyr who served as the Bishop of Rome during the early years of the Christian Church. He is known for organizing the Church's structure by appointing seven deacons and promoting various clergy amidst the persecutions of the time. Evaristus ultimately gave his life for his faith, being martyred and buried near Saint Peter in the Vatican.","/images/saint_evaristus.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pope_Evaristus","10-26"],"Fabian":["3rd century",null,"Martyrdom and leadership in the early Church","Saint Fabian was a Pope and martyr who led the Church during a tumultuous period in the 3rd century. He was elected pope in 236 AD, reportedly chosen by a miraculous sign when a dove landed on his head. Fabian died a martyr during the persecution of Christians under Emperor Decius in 250 AD, remembered for his holiness and leadership.","/images/saint_fabian.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Fabian","01-20"],"Faustinus and Jovita":["2nd century",["Brescia","martyrs"],"Courageous martyrs of the Christian faith","Faustinus and Jovita were brothers and martyrs from Brescia, Italy, who boldly preached Christianity during the persecution under Emperor Adrian. They faced severe trials, including being thrown to lions and enduring flames, yet remained unharmed through divine intervention. Ultimately, they were decapitated for their faith, and their legacy endures as the patrons of Brescia.","/images/saints_faustinus_and_jovita.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Faustinus_and_Jovita","02-15"],"Felicitas and Perpetua":["Early Christian",["mothers","expectant mothers","martyrs"],"Symbols of faith and courage in martyrdom","Saints Perpetua and Felicitas were early Christian martyrs who were executed in Carthage around 203 AD. Their story is famously recounted in Perpetua's diary, which provides a vivid account of their faith and courage in the face of persecution. They are celebrated for their unwavering commitment to Christianity and are considered symbols of strength and fidelity in the faith.","/images/saints_felicitas_perpetua_and_companions.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Perpetua_and_Felicity","03-06"],"Felicity":["2nd century",["mothers","martyrs","difficult childbirth"],"Courageous faith in the face of persecution.","Saint Felicity was a noble Roman matron known for her unwavering faith and virtue. She raised her seven sons in the Christian faith, encouraging them to remain steadfast in their beliefs even in the face of persecution. Ultimately, she and her sons were martyred for refusing to renounce their faith, exemplifying courage and devotion to God.","/images/saint_felicity_and_her_seven_sons.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Felicitas_of_Rome","07-10"],"Felix I":["3rd century",null,"Defender of faith against heresy","Felix I was a Roman Pope and martyr who served from 269 to 274 AD. He is known for his letters that addressed ecclesiastical justice and refuted various heresies, including those of Nestorius and Eutyches. Felix I was martyred during a persecution under Emperor Aurelian, and his legacy includes the practice of enclosing saints' relics in altars.","/images/saint_felix_i_pope.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pope_Felix_I","05-30"],"Felix of Cantalice":["16th century",["poor","beggars","Capuchin friars"],"A model of humility and devotion","Saint Felix of Cantalice was a Capuchin friar known for his deep devotion to prayer and service to the poor in Rome. He spent over forty years begging for his community and was renowned for his gentleness and kindness, often mediating conflicts among others. Canonized in 1712, he is remembered for his unwavering faith and love for the Virgin Mary.","/images/saint_felix_of_cantalice.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Felix_of_Cantalice","05-18"],"Felix of Nola":["3rd century",["nuns","orators","those seeking to overcome adversity"],"A model of faith and charity","Saint Felix of Nola was a priest known for his deep faith and dedication to serving others during a time of persecution in the early Church. He endured imprisonment and torture but remained steadfast in his pastoral duties, ultimately aiding his bishop, Maximus, during a time of great distress. After the persecution ended, he lived a life of poverty and charity, becoming a beloved figure in his community.","/images/saint_felix_of_nola.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Felix_of_Nola","01-14"],"Felix of Valois":["Medieval",["captives","prisoners","the Trinitarian Order"],"Co-founder of the Trinitarian Order","Saint Felix of Valois was a co-founder of the Trinitarian Order, dedicated to the redemption of Christian captives. Born into nobility, he led a life of austerity and virtue, ultimately establishing a monastic community in France. His legacy is marked by the rapid growth of the Trinitarian Order, which became instrumental in the liberation of captives during the Middle Ages.","/images/saint_felix_of_valois.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Felix_of_Valois","11-20"],"Fiaker":["7th century",["gardeners"],"Patron saint of gardeners and solitude","Saint Fiaker was a Scottish prince who renounced his royal heritage to live a life of solitude and prayer in France. He was known for his austere lifestyle, cultivating a garden and building an oratory in honor of the Blessed Virgin. Fiaker became a spiritual guide for many and is remembered as the patron saint of gardeners.","/images/saint_fiaker.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Fiacre","08-30"],"Fidelis of Sigmaringen":["17th century",["lawyers","the sick","martyrs"],"Martyr for the Catholic faith and the poor","Fidelis of Sigmaringen was a Capuchin friar and martyr known for his dedication to the poor and his fervent preaching against Calvinism. He was born in 1577 in Sigmaringen, Germany, and after a successful legal career, he embraced a life of austerity and service. Fidelis was martyred in Switzerland while defending the Catholic faith, becoming the first martyr of the Congregation of the Propaganda.","/images/saint_fidelis_of_sigmaringen.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fidelis_of_Sigmaringen","04-24"],"Finbarr":["6th century",["Cork","students","education"],"Founder of Cork and its cathedral","Finbarr was a sixth-century bishop and the founder of the city of Cork in Ireland. He established a monastery at Lough Eire, attracting many disciples and transforming the area into a thriving community. His legacy continues through the cathedral and church that bear his name, where his relics are kept.","/images/saint_finbarr.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Finbar_of_Cork","09-25"],"Finnian":["6th century",["educators","students","Ireland"],"Father of Irish monastic education","Saint Finnian was a prominent bishop and teacher in early Ireland, known for his role in establishing monastic schools. He founded the famous monastery of Clonard, which became a center of learning and produced many notable Irish saints. His dedication to his flock and commitment to spiritual education greatly influenced the development of Christianity in Ireland.","/images/saint_finnian_or_finan.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Finnian_of_Clonard","12-12"],"Firmin":["2nd century",["Amiens","diocesan clergy"],"First Bishop of Amiens and martyr","Saint Firmin was the first Bishop of Amiens and a martyr in the early second century. He is known for his missionary work in Gaul, where he preached the Gospel and converted many pagans to Christianity. Ultimately, he was martyred for his faith, fulfilling his desire for martyrdom and leaving a lasting legacy in the city of Amiens.","/images/saint_firmin.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Firmin_of_Amiens","09-25"],"Flavian":["5th century",null,"Defender of the faith and martyr","Flavian was the Patriarch of Constantinople from 447 until his martyrdom in 449. He is known for his staunch defense of the Catholic faith against the heresy of Eutyches, which denied the dual nature of Christ. Flavian's unwavering commitment to the teachings of the Church ultimately led to his persecution and death, and he was later recognized as a martyr by the Council of Chalcedon.","/images/saint_flavian.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flavian_of_Constantinople","02-17"],"Forty Martyrs of Sebaste":["4th century",null,"Martyrdom and unwavering faith in Christ","The Forty Martyrs of Sebaste were soldiers who refused to renounce their Christian faith during the early 4th century. They endured a brutal execution by being left to freeze on a pond, steadfast in their belief and filled with joy at the prospect of martyrdom. Their unwavering commitment inspired others, including a guard who joined them in their faith just before their deaths.","/images/the_forty_martyrs_of_sebaste.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forty_Martyrs_of_Sebaste","03-10"],"Frances Xavier Cabrini":["Modern",["immigrants","hospitality","orphanages"],"Champion of immigrants and education","Frances Xavier Cabrini was an Italian-American nun and the founder of the Missionary Sisters of the Sacred Heart. She dedicated her life to serving immigrants in the United States and established numerous schools, hospitals, and charitable institutions. Canonized in 1946, she is recognized as the first naturalized citizen of the United States to be canonized as a saint.","/images/saint_frances_xavier_cabrini.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frances_Xavier_Cabrini","12-22"],"Frances of Rome":["15th century",["widows","domestic workers","against temptations"],"A model of faith in domestic life","Frances of Rome was a noblewoman born in Rome in 1384 who, despite her parents' wishes, married Lorenzo Ponziano and dedicated her life to God through her domestic duties and prayer. After enduring personal tragedies, including the loss of two children, she founded a community of Benedictine Oblate nuns following her husband's death. Known for her deep spirituality and visions, she is remembered for her unwavering faith and commitment to serving God in both her family and religious life.","/images/saint_frances_of_rome.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frances_of_Rome","03-09"],"Francis Borgia":["16th century",["Jesuits","noblemen","the dying"],"A nobleman turned servant of God","Francis Borgia was a Spanish nobleman who became a Jesuit and served as the General of the Society of Jesus. After witnessing the death of Empress Isabella, he renounced his worldly ambitions and dedicated his life to serving God and the poor. He was instrumental in establishing Jesuit colleges and houses across Spain and Portugal, and he died in service to the Church in 1572.","/images/saint_francis_borgia.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Francis_Borgia","10-10"],"Francis Caracciolo":["16th century",["sick people","sailors"],"Founder of the Order of Regular Minor Clerics","Francis Caracciolo was born in 1563 in Naples and became a prominent figure in the Catholic Church as the founder of the Order of Regular Minor Clerics. After overcoming a severe illness, he dedicated his life to serving the poor and promoting devotion to the Blessed Sacrament. He is remembered for his deep spirituality and commitment to prayer, particularly in the presence of the Eucharist, until his death in 1608.","/images/saint_francis_caracciolo.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Francis_Caracciolo","06-04"],"Francis Regis Clet":["18th century",["missionaries","martyrs"],"A martyr for faith and mission","Francis Regis Clet was a French Lazarist missionary who dedicated his life to evangelizing in China. He faced severe persecution and ultimately became a martyr for his faith after being betrayed and tortured. His unwavering commitment to his mission and his silent endurance in the face of suffering exemplify his deep faith and dedication to God.","/images/saint_francis_regis_clet.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Francis_Regis_Clet","02-17"],"Francis Solano":["16th century",["missionaries","indigenous peoples","the sick"],"A devoted missionary and peacemaker.","Francis Solano was a Spanish Franciscan missionary known for his fervent dedication to spreading the Gospel in South America, particularly among the indigenous peoples of Peru. He is celebrated for his humility, compassion, and miraculous ability to connect with both people and nature, leading to the conversion of thousands. His legacy is marked by his unwavering faith and commitment to serving the sick and marginalized, even in the face of danger and adversity.","/images/saint_francis_solano.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Francis_Solanus","07-24"],"Francis Xavier":["16th century",["missionaries","foreign missions","India","Japan"],"Pioneer of Christian missions in Asia","Francis Xavier was a Spanish Jesuit missionary who played a crucial role in the spread of Christianity in Asia during the 16th century. He is best known for his extensive missionary work in India and Japan, where he baptized thousands and established numerous churches. His dedication and miraculous deeds earned him the title of Apostle of the Indies and Japan.","/images/saint_francis_xavier.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Francis_Xavier","12-03"],"Francis de Sales":["17th century",["journalists","writers","the deaf","salespeople"],"Champion of gentleness and spiritual guidance","Saint Francis de Sales was a 17th-century bishop known for his gentle approach to spirituality and his efforts to convert Calvinists in the Chablais region. He co-founded the Order of the Visitation with Saint Jane Frances de Chantal and emphasized the importance of meekness and humility. His writings, particularly 'Introduction to the Devout Life,' have had a lasting impact on Catholic spirituality.","/images/saint_francis_de_sales.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Francis_de_Sales","01-29"],"Francis of Assisi":["Medieval",["animals","environment","merchants","ecology"],"Patron saint of animals and ecology.","Francis of Assisi was a 13th-century Italian saint known for his profound love of nature and commitment to poverty. He founded the Franciscan Order, emphasizing a life of simplicity and service to the poor. His legacy includes the promotion of peace and the care for creation, making him a beloved figure in Christianity and beyond.","/images/saint_francis_of_assisi.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Francis_of_Assisi","10-04,12-25"],"Francis of Paula":["16th century",null,"Thaumaturge, Founder","At the age of fifteen, Saint Francis left his poor home at Paula in Calabria, Italy, to live as a hermit in a cave on the seacoast. In time disciples gathered around him, and with them, in 1436, he founded the Order of the Minims. He chose this name that they might always consider themselves the least of monastic Orders.","/images/saint_francis_of_paula.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Francis_of_Paola","04-02"],"François de Laval":["17th century",["Canada","clergy","evangelization"],"Pioneer of the Church in Canada","François de Laval was the first Bishop of Quebec and played a crucial role in the establishment of the Catholic Church in Canada. He was known for his charity, piety, and dedication to the formation of clergy, founding the Seminary of Quebec to support this mission. Despite facing significant challenges, including opposition from secular authorities and the struggle against the liquor trade, he remained steadfast in his commitment to evangelization and the welfare of his community.","/images/blessed_francois_de_montmorency-laval.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fran%C3%A7ois_de_Laval","07-21"],"Frederick Janssoone":["Modern",["pilgrims","missionaries","the sick"],"A devoted missionary and miracle worker","Frederick Janssoone was a Franciscan missionary known for his dedication to serving in the Holy Land and Canada. He founded numerous fraternities of the Third Order of Saint Francis and was recognized for his humility and charity, which fostered harmony among diverse groups in Jerusalem. His legacy includes the establishment of pilgrimage sites and the veneration of relics that were believed to perform miracles.","/images/blessed_father_frederick_janssoone_of_ghyvelde.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fr%C3%A9d%C3%A9ric_Janssoone","08-04"],"Frumentius":["4th century",["Ethiopia","missionaries"],"Apostle of Ethiopia and early Christian leader","Saint Frumentius was a child taken to Ethiopia with his brother Edesius after their ship was attacked. He became a trusted advisor to the Ethiopian king and later was consecrated as the first bishop of Ethiopia by Saint Athanasius. Through his efforts, he successfully spread Christianity throughout the nation, fulfilling a prophecy from Isaiah.","/images/saint_frumentius.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frumentius","10-27"],"Fulgentius":["6th century",["theologians","exiled clergy"],"Defender of faith and the exiled.","Fulgentius was a North African bishop and theologian known for his staunch defense of Catholic doctrine against Arianism and Pelagianism. After enduring persecution and exile, he returned to his diocese to restore order and discipline among the faithful. His writings continue to influence Christian thought, and he is remembered for his deep spirituality and commitment to the poor.","/images/saint_fulgentius.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fulgentius_of_Ruspe","01-01"],"Gabriel of Our Lady of Sorrows":["19th century",["youth","young religious"],"A model of youthful devotion and piety.","Saint Gabriel of Our Lady of Sorrows was a Passionist priest known for his deep devotion to the Virgin Mary and the Passion of Christ. He lived a life of contemplation and self-denial, dedicating himself to prayer and union with God. Despite his short life, he was canonized for his heroic sanctity and is recognized as a patron of youth and young religious.","/images/saint_gabriel_of_our_lady_of_sorrows.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gabriel_of_Our_Lady_of_Sorrows","02-27"],"Gabriel the Archangel":[null,null,"Archangel","The day before the great feast of the Annunciation, the Church celebrates the feast of the Archangel who brought to earth the glad tidings that Mary was chosen to be the Mother of the Incarnate God. This angelic Messenger appears several times in the history of God's chosen people. He came to Daniel the prophet after he had a vision of the future Persian and Greek empires, to explain the vision to him, as Daniel narrates in the eighth chapter of his book.","/images/saint_gabriel_archangel.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gabriel","03-24"],"Gal":["6th century",["against fevers"],"A model of humility and patience","Saint Gal was a bishop of Clermont who dedicated his life to God after renouncing worldly pursuits. Known for his humility, charity, and remarkable patience, he became a respected leader in the Church. He participated in significant councils and is remembered for the miracles that occurred at his tomb after his death in 553.","/images/saint_gal.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gal_I_of_Clermont","07-01"],"Gall":["7th century",["Switzerland","monks","students"],"Pioneer of Christianity in Switzerland","Saint Gall was an Irish abbot and missionary who played a significant role in the spread of Christianity in Switzerland. He accompanied Saint Columban to France and later settled near Lake Constance, where he founded a monastery that became a center for Christian teaching. Known for his humility and dedication, he converted many idolaters and is remembered for his miraculous deeds and peaceful coexistence with nature.","/images/saint_gall.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Gall","10-16"],"Gatian":["1st century",null,"Apostle of the Gauls and bishop","Saint Gatian was the first bishop of Tours and a disciple of the Apostles. He dedicated his life to preaching the Gospel and converting the pagan Gauls, enduring persecution and hardships in the process. His efforts led to the establishment of Christian communities and the abandonment of pagan worship in the region.","/images/saint_gatian.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gatianus_of_Tours","12-18"],"Gemma Galgani":["Modern",["students","sick people","loss of parents"],"A model of suffering and devotion","Saint Gemma Galgani was an Italian virgin known for her deep spirituality and extraordinary mystical experiences. Despite a life filled with suffering and physical ailments, she remained devoted to Christ and sought to glorify Him through her trials. She is remembered for her stigmata and her profound love for Jesus, which inspired many during her life and continues to do so today.","/images/saint_gemma_galgani.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gemma_Galgani","04-11"],"Genevieve":["5th century",["Paris","difficult marriages","sick children"],"Patroness of Paris and protector of the faithful.","Saint Genevieve was a 5th-century virgin and the patroness of Paris. Known for her piety and miraculous interventions, she played a crucial role in protecting the city during sieges, particularly against Attila the Hun. Her legacy endures as a symbol of faith and resilience in the face of adversity.","/images/saint_genevieve.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Genevieve_of_Paris","01-03"],"George":["3rd century",["soldiers","scouts","farmers","geese","England"],"Symbol of courage and Christian fortitude","Saint George was a Christian martyr and soldier born in Palestine towards the end of the third century. He famously defied the Roman Emperor Diocletian by openly rebuking him for the persecution of Christians, which ultimately led to his execution. His legacy as a symbol of courage and faith has made him a beloved patron saint across various cultures and regions.","/images/saint_george.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_of_Lydda","04-23"],"Gerard":["10th century",null,"Pioneer of Benedictine monasticism in Belgium","Gerard was born into a noble family in the county of Namur, Belgium, towards the end of the ninth century. After a period of military service, he dedicated himself to monastic life, founding the Abbey of Brogne and establishing strict monastic discipline in multiple abbeys. He is remembered for his piety, leadership, and commitment to the Benedictine way of life until his death in 959.","/images/saint_gerard.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gerard_of_Brogne","10-03"],"Gerard Majella":["18th century",["expectant mothers","children","the falsely accused"],"Miracle worker and devoted Redemptorist","Gerard Majella was an Italian Redemptorist brother known for his miraculous works and deep devotion to God. He exhibited extraordinary virtues and was renowned for his gift of bilocation, often seen assisting the sick during a plague. He was canonized in 1904 and is celebrated for his profound charity and commitment to his religious life.","/images/saint_gerard_majella.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gerard_Majella","10-16"],"Germaine Cousin":["Early Modern",["sick people","shepherds","abused children"],"A symbol of faith and resilience","Germaine Cousin was a French virgin saint born in 1579, known for her deep faith despite enduring severe hardships and illness throughout her life. She worked as a shepherdess and was often mistreated by her stepmother, yet she remained devoted to God and inspired those around her with her kindness. After her death in 1601, her body was found incorrupt, leading to her canonization in 1867 and making her a beloved figure among pilgrims in Pibrac, France.","/images/saint_germaine_cousin.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Germaine_Cousin","06-15"],"Germanus of Auxerre":["5th century",["England","against heresy","teachers"],"Defender of faith and educator in Britain","Germanus of Auxerre was a 5th-century bishop known for his strong opposition to the Pelagian heresy in Britain. After being consecrated bishop, he dedicated his life to penance and performing miracles, attracting many to seek his help. He also established schools in Britain, contributing significantly to the education and spiritual formation of the people.","/images/saint_germanus_of_auxerre.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Germanus_of_Auxerre","07-30"],"Germanus of Paris":["6th century",["Paris","the poor","prisoners"],"Champion of the poor and the afflicted","Saint Germanus of Paris was a sixth-century bishop known for his fervent devotion and miraculous gifts. He dedicated his life to the poor and the afflicted, often sharing his resources and advocating for the imprisoned. His influence led to the conversion of King Childebert and the eradication of idolatry in France.","/images/saint_germanus_of_paris.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Germanus_of_Paris","05-28"],"Gertrude":["Medieval",["souls in purgatory","the sick","the mentally ill"],"Mystic and advocate for souls","Saint Gertrude of Eisleben, also known as Gertrude the Great, was a 14th-century mystic and abbess known for her deep spiritual insights and devotion to the Passion of Christ. Born into a noble family, she dedicated her life to prayer and contemplation, receiving numerous mystical experiences and revelations. Her writings, particularly the book of her Revelations, continue to inspire devotion and reflection on the love of God and the salvation of souls.","/images/saint_gertrude.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gertrude_the_Great","11-16"],"Giles":["7th century",["beggars","poor","disabled","brewers"],"Patron of the poor and disabled","Saint Giles was a 7th-century hermit known for his deep compassion and miraculous deeds. Born in Athens to a noble family, he dedicated his life to poverty and solitude, performing numerous miracles that drew people to him. His legacy includes the establishment of a monastery in France that became a center of the Benedictine Order.","/images/saint_giles.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giles_of_Provence","09-01"],"Goar":["6th century",["hospitality","travelers"],"Patron of hospitality and travelers","Saint Goar was a priest and hermit known for his piety and fervent preaching in the 6th century. He dedicated his life to serving God in solitude, while also converting many through his example and miracles. Renowned for his hospitality, he became a revered figure in the region near Trier, Germany, where he established a hermitage and church.","/images/saint_goar.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goar_of_Aquitaine","07-06"],"Godfrey":["Medieval",["hospitals","the sick","pilgrims"],"Compassionate leader and miracle worker","Saint Godfrey was a bishop of Amiens known for his deep compassion for the poor and sick. He became the Abbot of Nogent at a young age and revitalized the monastery, demonstrating strong leadership and dedication to his community. His life was marked by miracles and a commitment to serving those in need, ultimately leading to his canonization after his death in 1115.","/images/saint_godfrey_or_geoffroy.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Godfrey_of_Amiens","11-08"],"Gontran":["6th century",["kings","the sick","peace"],"A compassionate king and protector of peace.","Saint Gontran was the King of Burgundy and Orleans, known for his piety and dedication to his subjects. He ruled during a time of division among his brothers and sought to maintain peace and justice in his realm. His legacy includes the establishment of churches and monasteries, as well as a reputation for mercy and compassion towards the oppressed.","/images/saint_gontran.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gontran","03-28"],"Gregory Nazianzen":["4th century",["theologians","students","orators"],"Champion of Christian orthodoxy and eloquence.","Gregory Nazianzen was an influential early Christian theologian and Archbishop of Constantinople. Known for his eloquent sermons and writings, he played a crucial role in the development of Christian doctrine during the Arian controversy. His legacy as a Doctor of the Church and a champion of orthodoxy continues to be celebrated in the Christian tradition.","/images/saint_gregory_nazianzen.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gregory_Nazianzen","05-09"],"Gregory Thaumaturge":["3rd century",["theologians","students","converts"],"Miracle worker and defender of the faith","Saint Gregory Thaumaturge was a bishop and confessor known for his miraculous works and dedication to spreading Christianity in a predominantly pagan region. He was a student of Origen and became a pivotal figure in combating heresies, particularly Arianism, through his teachings and the formulation of a creed. His legacy includes a strong devotion to the Virgin Mary and a commitment to prayer and solitude, which inspired many to embrace the Christian faith.","/images/saint_gregory_thaumaturge.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gregory_Thaumaturgus","11-17"],"Gregory VII":["11th century",null,"Champion of Church reform and independence","Gregory VII, originally named Hildebrand, was a pivotal Pope known for his reformative efforts within the Catholic Church during the 11th century. He staunchly opposed simony and the interference of secular rulers in ecclesiastical matters, famously excommunicating Emperor Henry IV. His papacy was marked by significant struggles for the independence of the Church, ultimately leading to his exile and death in 1085.","/images/saint_gregory_vii.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pope_Gregory_VII","05-25"],"Gregory of Langres":["6th century",null,"A model of humility and pastoral care.","Gregory of Langres was a Christian administrator and bishop known for his virtuous life and dedication to his pastoral duties. After resigning from his position as Count of Autun, he became a bishop and was noted for his humility, prayer, and efforts in converting many to Christianity. He served as bishop for thirty-three years, founding monasteries and leaving a lasting impact on his diocese before his death in 539.","/images/saint_gregory_of_langres.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gregory_of_Langres","01-04"],"Gregory the Great":["6th century",["teachers","musicians","students","popes"],"Pioneer of medieval Church reform","Gregory the Great was a significant Pope and Doctor of the Church who played a crucial role in the early medieval Church. He is known for his contributions to the liturgy, the promotion of monasticism, and the evangelization of England. His papacy marked a period of reform and consolidation within the Church, and he is remembered for his extensive writings and sermons that emphasized the importance of Scripture.","/images/saint_gregory_the_great.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gregory_the_Great","03-12"],"Guy of Anderlecht":["10th century",["pilgrims","the poor"],"Devotion to prayer and service to the poor","Saint Guy of Anderlecht was a 10th-century hermit and pilgrim known for his deep devotion to prayer and service to the poor. After a brief period of inconstancy in his life, he dedicated himself to a life of penance and pilgrimage, visiting significant religious sites. He is remembered for his humility and commitment to the Church, ultimately passing away in 1012, with a light reportedly shining around him at his death.","/images/saint_guy_of_anderlecht.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guy_of_Anderlecht","09-12"],"Hedwig":["Medieval",["Silesia","widows","the poor"],"A model of charity and devotion","Saint Hedwig was a Polish duchess known for her piety and charitable works. After the death of her husband, she dedicated her life to the Cistercian convent of Trebnitz, where she served under her daughter, the abbess. She is remembered for her devotion to the Blessed Sacrament and her care for the poor and marginalized.","/images/saint_hedwig.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hedwig_of_Silesia","10-16"],"Hegesippus":["2nd century",null,"Early Church historian and defender of tradition","Saint Hegesippus was a Jewish convert to Christianity and an early Church historian. He is known for his work 'Memoirs', which documented the history of the Church from the Passion of Christ to his time, emphasizing the importance of apostolic tradition. His writings were highly regarded by early Church Fathers such as Saint Jerome and Eusebius, although most of his work has unfortunately been lost.","/images/saint_hegesippus.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Hegesippus","04-07"],"Helen":["4th century",["converts","archaeologists","difficult marriages"],"Discoverer of the True Cross","Saint Helen was a British princess and the mother of Constantine the Great, the first Christian emperor. She is renowned for her pilgrimage to Jerusalem, where she discovered the True Cross and built churches at significant Christian sites. Her influence on her son and her contributions to Christianity are celebrated, particularly her role in promoting the faith during the early years of the Church.","/images/saint_helen.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Helen_of_Constantinople","08-18"],"Heliodorus":["4th century",null,"Defender of the Catholic faith and piety","Saint Heliodorus was a bishop of Altino known for his piety and dedication to the Catholic faith. He was a disciple of Saint Jerome and played a significant role in combating the Arian heresy during his time. His life exemplified a balance between active ministry and contemplative spirituality until his death around the year 390.","/images/saint_heliodorus.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heliodorus_of_Altino","07-03"],"Henry":["Medieval",["Germany","the childless","difficult marriages"],"A model of piety and leadership","Henry II, also known as Saint Henry, was the King of Germany and Emperor of the Holy Roman Empire. He is remembered for his piety, humility, and dedication to the Christian faith, as well as for his efforts to spread Christianity in Eastern Europe. His reign was marked by military victories and the establishment of numerous churches and monasteries, reflecting his commitment to the service of God.","/images/saint_henry.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henry_II,_Holy_Roman_Emperor","07-15"],"Henry Suzo":["14th century",null,"Mystic and writer of profound spirituality","Henry Suso was a German Dominican friar and mystic, known for his deep spiritual writings and devotion to the Passion of Christ. He played a significant role in the development of German mysticism during the 14th century. His works, particularly 'The Exemplar', emphasize the importance of love and union with God.","/images/blessed_henry_suzo.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henry_Suso","03-02"],"Herman Joseph of Steinfeld":["13th century",null,"Priest","Blessed Herman from his earliest years, was a devoted client of the Mother of God. As a little child he used to spend all his playtime in the church at Cologne before a statue of Mary, where he received many favors. One bitter winter day, as little Herman was coming barefooted into church, his heavenly Mother, appearing to him, asked him lovingly why his feet were bare in such cold weather.","/images/blessed_herman_joseph_of_steinfeld.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hermann_Joseph_von_Steinfeld","04-07"],"Hermenegild":["6th century",["martyrs","conversions"],"Martyrdom inspiring faith and conversion","Saint Hermenegild was a Visigothic prince and martyr who was executed by his father, King Leovigild, for converting to Christianity. Despite facing severe torture and temptation to renounce his faith, he remained steadfast and chose to die rather than betray his beliefs. His martyrdom inspired many, including his brother Recared, who later converted the Visigoths to Christianity.","/images/saint_hermenegild.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hermenegild","04-13"],"Hilarion":["4th century",["monks","healers","the sick"],"Founder of monastic life in Palestine","Saint Hilarion was a prominent early Christian monk and the founder of monastic life in Palestine. Known for his severe asceticism and miraculous works, he attracted many followers and was revered for his healing abilities. He died in 372, leaving a legacy of devotion and monasticism that influenced the Christian community.","/images/saint_hilarion.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hilarion","10-22"],"Hilary of Poitiers":["4th century",["theologians","exiled people"],"Defender of orthodoxy against Arianism","Hilary of Poitiers was a bishop and Doctor of the Church known for his staunch defense of orthodox Christianity against Arianism. After converting to Christianity in middle age, he became a prominent theologian and wrote extensively on the Trinity. His efforts in promoting the faith led to his exile, during which he continued to compose influential works and ultimately returned to his diocese to continue his ministry until his death.","/images/saint_hilary_of_poitiers.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hilary_of_Poitiers","01-14"],"Holy Innocents":["1st century",["children","innocence","victims of violence"],"Martyrs of innocence and faith","The Holy Innocents are the children who were martyred by King Herod in his attempt to kill the newborn Jesus. They are recognized as the first martyrs of Christianity, having died for their faith without ever knowing it. Their feast day is celebrated in honor of their sacrifice and innocence.","/images/the_holy_innocents.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Holy_Innocents","12-28"],"Holy Martyrs of Japan":["16th century",["Japan","missionaries","martyrs"],"Courageous witnesses of faith and sacrifice.","The Holy Martyrs of Japan were a group of 26 Christians, including six Franciscans, three Jesuits, and seventeen laypeople, who were martyred in 1597 during a period of intense persecution. Their steadfast faith and willingness to die for Christ inspired many and contributed to the growth of Christianity in Japan. Canonized by Pope Pius IX in 1862, they are remembered for their courage and devotion to their beliefs.","/images/the_holy_martyrs_of_japan.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Holy_Martyrs_of_Jasenovac","02-05"],"Honoratus":["5th century",["monks","hermits"],"Founder of the monastery of Lerins","Saint Honoratus was a prominent figure in early Christianity, known for founding the famous monastery of Lerins around the year 400. He initially lived as a hermit before being compelled to serve as Archbishop of Arles in 426. His life was marked by austerity, devotion, and a commitment to leading others in the Christian faith.","/images/saint_honoratus.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Honoratus_of_Arles","01-16"],"Hospitius":["6th century",null,"A model of asceticism and prophecy","Saint Hospitius was a recluse who lived in a tower near Nice in Provence during the 6th century. He practiced extreme asceticism, surviving on minimal food and dedicating his life to prayer and prophecy. Known for his miraculous gifts, he foretold the invasion of the Lombards and converted a soldier who attempted to harm him.","/images/saint_hospitius.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hospitius","05-21"],"Hubert":["Early Medieval",["hunters","dogs","forest workers","mathematicians"],"Patron saint of hunters and the forest.","Saint Hubert was a bishop known for his deep faith and commitment to the Christian life after a transformative encounter during a hunting expedition. He became a zealous apostle, dedicating his life to serving the poor and spreading the Gospel. His legacy includes the establishment of the diocese of Liège and a reputation for performing miracles.","/images/saint_hubert.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Hubert_of_Liege","11-03"],"Hugh of Cluny":["Medieval",["monks","abandoned children","hospitals"],"Promoter of monastic reform and spirituality","Hugh of Cluny was a prominent abbot who led the Cluniac reform movement in the 11th century. He became the abbot of Cluny at a young age and was instrumental in expanding the influence of the abbey, which became a center of monastic life and spirituality. His legacy includes the establishment of numerous monasteries and the promotion of a renewed commitment to monastic discipline and prayer.","/images/saint_hugh_of_cluny.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hugh_of_Cluny","04-29"],"Hugh of Grenoble":["11th century",["clergy","poor","reformers"],"Champion of reform and charity in the Church.","Hugh of Grenoble was a 11th-century bishop known for his deep piety and commitment to reforming the Church. He initially resigned his bishopric to live a monastic life but was called back to serve his diocese, where he became renowned for his charitable works and powerful preaching. His legacy includes the founding of the Carthusian Order alongside Saint Bruno and his dedication to the poor.","/images/saint_hugh_of_grenoble.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hugh_of_Grenoble","04-01"],"Hyacinth":["13th century",["missionaries","Poland","merchants"],"Apostle of the North and miracle worker","Saint Hyacinth was a Polish missionary and one of the first members of the Order of Preachers, founded by Saint Dominic. He traveled extensively across Europe and Asia, evangelizing and converting many to Christianity, and is credited with baptizing over 120,000 individuals. Known for his deep devotion to the Virgin Mary, he performed numerous miracles and is remembered for his unwavering faith and dedication to spreading the Gospel.","/images/saint_hyacinth.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hyacinth_of_Poland","08-17"],"Hyacinth Castañeda":["18th century",["missionaries","martyrs"],"A martyr for faith in Asia.","Hyacinth Castañeda was a Dominican priest and missionary born in Spain who dedicated his life to spreading Christianity in Asia. He faced severe persecution during his missions in China and Vietnam, ultimately being martyred for his faith. He was canonized in 1988, recognized for his unwavering commitment to his mission and the sacrifices he made for the Gospel.","/images/saint_hyacinth_castaneda.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hyacinth_Casteneda","11-07"],"Hyacintha of Mariscotti":["17th century",["sick","nurses","the poor"],"From luxury to a life of service","Hyacintha of Mariscotti was a 17th-century Italian religious figure known for her profound transformation from a life of luxury to one of deep piety and service. After experiencing a spiritual awakening, she dedicated herself to nursing the sick and established confraternities to aid the poor and elderly. Her life was marked by miracles and a strong devotion to the Blessed Virgin Mary, leading to her canonization in 1807.","/images/saint_hyacintha_of_mariscotti.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hyacintha_of_Mariscotti","01-30"],"Hyginus":["2nd century",null,"Defender of faith against early heresies","Hyginus served as Pope from around 139 to 142 AD during a time of relative peace for the Church under Emperor Antoninus Pius. He faced challenges from heretics such as Cerdo and Valentine, whom he sought to correct with pastoral care. Although not officially martyred, he is recognized as a martyr due to the persecutions he endured as a leader of the early Church.","/images/saint_hyginus.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pope_Hyginus","01-11"],"Ignatius of Antioch":["1st century",["church leaders","martyrs","the sick"],"A champion of Christian unity and faith","Ignatius of Antioch was an early Christian bishop and martyr who played a significant role in the development of the Church. He is known for his letters, which emphasized the importance of unity and the Eucharist, and for his steadfastness in the face of persecution. Ignatius was martyred in Rome around the year 107, famously expressing his desire to be 'the wheat of Christ' ground by the teeth of wild beasts.","/images/saint_ignatius_of_antioch.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ignatius_of_Antioch","02-01"],"Ignatius of Loyola":["16th century",["soldiers","spiritual retreats","education"],"Founder of the Jesuits and spiritual guide","Saint Ignatius of Loyola was a Spanish priest and theologian who founded the Society of Jesus, also known as the Jesuits. After a profound spiritual conversion following a severe injury, he dedicated his life to serving God and helping others through education and spiritual exercises. His legacy includes the Spiritual Exercises, a guide for spiritual growth that has influenced countless individuals and communities.","/images/saint_ignatius_of_loyola.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ignatius_of_Loyola","07-31"],"Ildephonsus":["7th century",["theologians","virgins","Toledo"],"Defender of Mary's perpetual virginity","Ildephonsus was a 7th-century Bishop of Toledo known for his defense of the Virgin Mary's perpetual virginity. He was a prominent theologian and wrote extensively on Marian doctrine, earning the title 'Doctor of the Virginity of the Mother of God.' His legacy includes numerous miracles attributed to him and the establishment of a monastery for virgins in honor of Mary.","/images/saint_ildephonsus.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ildephonsus_of_Toledo","01-23"],"Imelda":["14th century",null,"Virgin","Saint Imelda was born at Bologna in Italy, in the early 14 th century. Still a child, she arranged a little oratory in her house, where she often would pray. She resolved to enter a monastery and make the vows of religion, and to give herself entirely to her Saviour.","/images/saint_imelda.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Imelda_Lambertini","05-12"],"Irenaeus":["2nd century",["apologists","against heresies"],"Defender of the faith against heresies","Saint Irenaeus was a prominent early Christian theologian and bishop of Lyons, known for his role in combating heresies in the 2nd century. He is best remembered for his work 'Adversus Haereses,' which defended the Christian faith against Gnostic teachings. Irenaeus was martyred around the year 202 under the Roman Emperor Septimus Severus, leaving a lasting legacy as a Doctor of the Church.","/images/saint_irenaeus.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Irenaeus","06-28"],"Ischyrion":["3rd century",null,"Martyrdom for unwavering faith","Saint Ischyrion was a martyr in Alexandria who refused to sacrifice to idols under the command of his magistrate. His steadfastness in faith led to his brutal execution by being impaled. He is remembered for his patient constancy and commitment to Christian virtue despite facing severe persecution.","/images/saint_ischyrion.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Ischyrion","12-22"],"Isidore":["7th century",null,"Doctor of the Church","despaired over his poor success in study, and ran away from school. Resting in his flight at a roadside spring, he observed a stone, which had been hollowed out by the slow but constant dripping of water. This lesson decided him to return, and then, by determined application, he succeeded.","/images/saint_isidore.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isidore_of_Seville","04-04"],"Isidore of Madrid":["12th century",null,"Confessor","Saint Isidore the Farmer, a perennially popular Saint in Spain, was born near Madrid of very poor but very Christian parents, who early inspired in him love for God and horror of sin. His education was accomplished entirely by the Holy Spirit who taught him, without books, the science of salvation. He married a wife rich in virtue, Maria Torribia, and God blessed them with a son whom they brought up in the sentiments of their own piety.","/images/saint_isidore_of_madrid.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isidore_the_Laborer","05-10"],"James of Nisibis":["4th century",["Nisibis","prophecy","miracles"],"Protector of Nisibis and miracle worker","James of Nisibis was a prominent bishop and doctor of the Syrian Church known for his deep spirituality and commitment to prayer. He is celebrated for his miraculous interventions, including protecting his city from a Persian siege through divine prayer. His legacy endures as a symbol of faith and resilience in the face of adversity.","/images/saint_james_of_nisibis.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_of_Nisibis","07-11"],"James of the March of Ancona":["15th century",["sick people","preachers"],"A devoted preacher and miracle worker.","James of the March of Ancona was a Franciscan priest known for his deep spirituality and commitment to prayer. He dedicated his life to preaching and performed numerous miracles, converting many hearts to God throughout Europe. Despite facing persecution and illness, he remained steadfast in his faith until his death in 1476.","/images/saint_james_of_the_march_of_ancona.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_of_the_Marches","11-28"],"James the Greater":["1st century",["Spain","pilgrims","apothecaries"],"First Apostle to be martyred","James the Greater was one of the twelve Apostles and a close companion of Jesus, known for his fervent faith and outspoken nature. He is traditionally believed to have preached in Spain after Christ's resurrection and was the first Apostle to be martyred, executed by King Herod Agrippa around the year 44. His legacy is commemorated at the Basilica of Saint James of Compostello, a major pilgrimage site in Spain.","/images/saint_james_the_greater.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_the_Great","07-25"],"Jane Frances de Chantal":["17th century",["widows","difficult marriages","the loss of parents"],"Foundress of the Visitation Order","Jane Frances de Chantal was a French noblewoman who founded the Order of the Visitation of the Blessed Virgin Mary. After enduring personal tragedies, including the loss of her husband and children, she dedicated her life to God and the service of others. Her legacy includes the establishment of numerous convents that welcomed widows and women of fragile health.","/images/saint_jane_frances_de_chantal.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jane_Frances_de_Chantal","08-21"],"Jane of Valois":["Renaissance",["women","difficult marriages","the sick"],"A devoted queen and founder of a religious order.","Jane of Valois was a French queen who faced significant personal trials, including neglect and betrayal by her husband, Louis XII. Despite her hardships, she dedicated her life to God and founded the Order of the Annunciation, aimed at honoring the virtues of the Virgin Mary. Her legacy includes her commitment to prayer and service, which inspired many during her lifetime and beyond.","/images/saint_jane_of_valois.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jane_de_Valois","02-04"],"Januarius":["3rd century",["blood donors","volcanoes","Naples"],"Miraculous blood and steadfast faith","Saint Januarius was a bishop of Beneventum and a martyr who died during the persecution of Christians under Emperor Diocletian around the year 305. He is renowned for the miraculous liquefaction of his blood, which occurs annually on his feast day and is believed to protect the city of Naples from disasters. His relics are venerated in the cathedral of Naples, where many miracles have been attributed to him, leading to the conversion of thousands.","/images/saint_januarius.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Januarius","09-19"],"Jeanne Leber":["17th century",["recluses","contemplatives"],"A model of contemplative prayer and service.","Blessed Jeanne Leber was a Canadian recluse known for her deep contemplative life and dedication to prayer. She lived a life of strict poverty and service, creating vestments and altar decorations for the churches of Montreal. Jeanne's commitment to her faith and her role as a spiritual guide left a lasting legacy in the Canadian Catholic community.","/images/blessed_jeanne_leber.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jeanne_Le_Ber","10-12"],"Jerome":["4th-5th century",["students","libraries","translators"],"Father of biblical scholarship and translation","Saint Jerome was a prominent early Christian scholar and translator, best known for his Latin translation of the Bible, known as the Vulgate. He spent many years in solitude, studying and writing in the deserts of Syria and later in Bethlehem, where he produced a wealth of theological works. His contributions to biblical scholarship and his defense against heresies have made him a Doctor of the Church and a significant figure in Christian history.","/images/saint_jerome.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jerome","09-30"],"Jerome Emiliani":["Renaissance",["orphaned children","sick","hospital workers"],"Champion of orphans and the sick","Jerome Emiliani was a Venetian soldier who transformed his life after being imprisoned, dedicating himself to the service of orphans and the sick. He founded the Congregation of Regular Clerics, known as the Somascans, to care for abandoned children and promote Christian education. His legacy is marked by his deep compassion and commitment to charity, particularly during times of famine and epidemic.","/images/saint_jerome_emiliani.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jerome_Emiliani","07-20"],"Joachim":["1st century",["grandparents","fathers","families"],"Father of the Virgin Mary and faithful servant","Saint Joachim is recognized as the father of the Virgin Mary and the husband of Saint Anne. He and Anne were childless for many years until an angel announced that they would have a daughter, Mary, who would become the Mother of Jesus. Joachim is honored for his piety and devotion, exemplifying faithfulness to God in the face of adversity.","/images/saint_joachim.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joachim","08-16"],"Joan Antide Thouret":["19th century",null,"Foundress","Born in the diocese of Besançon in November of 1765, Saint Joan Antide lost her pious mother when she was 16 years old, and for several years took charge of the household and her family of younger brothers and sisters. After many hesitations, her father permitted her to enter the Congregation of the Daughters of Charity of Saint Vincent de Paul in Paris in 1787. She worked in various hospitals caring for the sick, until the Revolution in France brought about the dispersion of the Congregations.","/images/saint_joan_antide_thouret.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jeanne-Antide_Thouret","05-23"],"Joan of Arc":["15th century",["France","soldiers","martyrs","women","captives"],"Symbol of faith and national pride","Joan of Arc, a peasant girl born in France, played a crucial role in the Hundred Years' War by leading French troops to several important victories. Captured by the English, she was tried for heresy and burned at the stake in 1431, becoming a martyr. Canonized in 1920, she is celebrated as a national heroine of France and a symbol of courage and faith.","/images/saint_joan_of_arc.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joan_of_Arc","05-30"],"John":["1st century",["authors","theologians","apothecaries"],"Beloved disciple and author of Revelation","Saint John the Evangelist was the last surviving Apostle and played a significant role in the early Church, particularly in Asia Minor. He was famously subjected to a miraculous escape from martyrdom when he was cast into a cauldron of boiling oil, which did not harm him. Ultimately, he was exiled to the island of Patmos, where he received the visions that would become the Book of Revelation.","/images/saint_john_before_the_latin_gate.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_the_Evangelist","05-06,12-27"],"John Baptist de LaSalle":["18th century",null,"Founder","Complete dedication to what he saw as God's will for him, dominates the life of John Baptist de LaSalle. Founder of the Brothers of the Christian Schools, or Christian Brothers, he was canonized in 1900. In 1950 Pope Pius XII named him patron of schoolteachers.","/images/saint_john_baptist_de_lasalle.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jean-Baptiste_de_La_Salle","05-15"],"John Berchmans":["17th century",["students","Jesuit novices"],"A model of piety and dedication","John Berchmans was a Belgian Jesuit seminarian known for his deep piety and dedication to education. He excelled in his studies and was beloved by children for his engaging catechism lessons. His life was marked by humility and charity, and he died at a young age, leaving a legacy of sanctity and devotion.","/images/saint_john_berchmans.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Berchmans","11-26"],"John Bosco":["19th century",["youth","students","apprentices","magicians"],"Champion of youth and education","John Bosco was an Italian priest and educator who dedicated his life to the welfare of disadvantaged youth. He founded the Salesian Society, which focuses on the education and spiritual development of young people. His legacy continues through the numerous Salesian institutions around the world that uphold his mission of love and education.","/images/saint_john_bosco.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Bosco","01-31"],"John Cantius":["15th century",["Poland","Lithuania","students","the poor"],"A model of charity and piety","John Cantius was a Polish priest and theologian known for his deep piety and commitment to the poor. He served as a professor at the University of Cracow and was renowned for his charitable acts, including providing for the needy and advocating for the marginalized. Canonized in 1767, he is remembered for his dedication to education, prayer, and service to others.","/images/saint_john_cantius.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Cantius","10-20"],"John Capistran":["15th century",["military chaplains","prisoners","the sick"],"Defender of the faith and reformer","John Capistran was an Italian Franciscan friar known for his powerful preaching and dedication to reforming the Church. He played a significant role in the defense of Christian territories during the Ottoman threat, notably leading a successful crusade at Belgrade in 1456. Canonized in 1690, he is remembered for his humility, perseverance, and the numerous miracles attributed to him after his death.","/images/saint_john_capistran.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Capistran","03-28"],"John Chrysostom":["4th-5th century",["preachers","orators","the sick","exiled people"],"Master of eloquence and pastoral care","John Chrysostom was a prominent early Church Father known for his eloquent preaching and extensive writings. He served as the Patriarch of Constantinople and was a key figure in the development of Christian liturgy. Despite facing exile and hardship due to his outspoken criticism of vice, he remained steadfast in his faith and is remembered as a Doctor of the Church.","/images/saint_john_chrysostom.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Chrysostom","01-27"],"John Climacus":["6th century",["monks","spiritual growth"],"Guide to spiritual ascent and wisdom","John Climacus was a 6th-century monk and abbot known for his spiritual writings, particularly 'The Ladder of Divine Ascent,' which outlines the path to spiritual perfection. He spent much of his life in solitude on Mount Sinai, where he became renowned for his wisdom and holiness. His teachings continue to inspire Christians seeking to deepen their spiritual lives.","/images/saint_john_climacus.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Climacus","03-30"],"John Damascene":["8th century",["theologians","icon painters","musicians"],"Defender of icons and theological thought","John Damascene was a prominent theologian and Doctor of the Church in the 8th century, known for his defense of the veneration of icons during the Iconoclast controversy. He served as a minister in the court of the Caliph of Damascus before retiring to a monastery, where he dedicated his life to writing and prayer. His works significantly influenced Christian theology, particularly in the areas of apologetics and the relationship between faith and reason.","/images/saint_john_damascene.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_of_Damascus","03-27"],"John Eudes":["17th century",["clergy","parish priests","spiritual directors"],"Pioneer of devotion to the Sacred Heart","John Eudes was a French priest and the founder of the Congregation of Jesus and Mary and the Sisters of Our Lady of Charity. He is known for his devotion to the Sacred Heart of Jesus and the Immaculate Heart of Mary, as well as for his efforts in establishing seminaries and evangelizing the poor. Eudes was canonized in 1925 and is recognized for his significant contributions to the Catholic Church during the 17th century.","/images/saint_john_eudes.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Eudes","08-19"],"John Francis Regis":["17th century",["sick people","social workers","prisoners"],"A devoted missionary and miracle worker","John Francis Regis was a Jesuit missionary known for his dedicated service to the poor and his fervent preaching in France during the 17th century. He worked tirelessly to convert heretics and assist those in need, often facing great personal danger and hardship. His legacy as a miracle worker and a model of charity continues to inspire devotion and pilgrimage to his burial site.","/images/saint_john_francis_regis.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Francis_Regis","06-16"],"John Gabriel Perboyre":["19th century",["missionaries","martyrs"],"A martyr for faith and devotion","John Gabriel Perboyre was a French Lazarist priest and martyr known for his deep devotion to the Blessed Sacrament. He dedicated his life to missionary work in China, where he faced severe persecution and ultimately gave his life for his faith. His unwavering commitment to God and his courageous response to torture have made him a revered figure in the Catholic Church.","/images/saint_john_gabriel_perboyre.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Gabriel_Perboyre","09-11"],"John Gualbert":["11th century",["against simony","parish priests"],"A champion of forgiveness and monastic life.","John Gualbert was a 11th-century Italian saint who founded the Vallombrosan Order of Benedictine monks. After a profound personal conversion marked by the forgiveness of his brother's murderer, he dedicated his life to prayer, penance, and the promotion of the Faith. His legacy includes a strong emphasis on mercy and the fight against simony within the Church.","/images/saint_john_gualbert.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Gualbert","07-12"],"John Joseph of the Cross":["18th century",null,"Model of humility and monastic fervor","John Joseph of the Cross was a Franciscan priest and confessor known for his deep spirituality and commitment to monastic life. He founded several monasteries in Italy and was recognized for his humility, discipline, and supernatural gifts, including prophecy and miracles. Canonized in 1839, he is remembered for his dedication to the principles of contemplation and penance within the Franciscan Order.","/images/saint_john_joseph_of_the_cross.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Joseph_of_the_Cross","03-05"],"John Nepomucene":["14th century",["confessors","bridges","water","silence"],"Martyr of silence and fidelity to God","John Nepomucene was a priest and martyr born in Bohemia in 1330. He is known for his unwavering silence in the face of torture and death, refusing to betray the confessions of the empress. After being thrown into the Moldau River, his body was found incorrupt, and he became a symbol of fidelity and the sanctity of the sacrament of confession.","/images/saint_john_nepomucene.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Nepomucene","05-16"],"John Vianney":["19th century",["priests","sick people","sacramental confession"],"Model of pastoral care and holiness","John Vianney, known as the Curé of Ars, was a humble priest renowned for his extraordinary pastoral care and dedication to the sacrament of confession. He transformed the parish of Ars, France, into a vibrant Christian community through his piety, preaching, and charitable works. His life of penance and devotion has made him a model for priests and a beloved saint in the Catholic Church.","/images/saint_john_vianney.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Vianney","08-09"],"John and Paul":["4th century",["martyrs","soldiers"],"Martyrs of steadfast faith and charity","Saints John and Paul were brothers and Roman army officers who served during the reign of Constantine the Great. They became martyrs after refusing to renounce their Christian faith under the rule of Julian the Apostate. Their legacy is marked by their steadfastness in faith and their charitable works, which led to their eventual martyrdom in the 4th century.","/images/saints_john_and_paul.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_and_Paul","06-26"],"John de Britto":["17th century",["missionaries","India"],"A martyr and apostle of India.","John de Britto was a Portuguese Jesuit missionary who dedicated his life to spreading Christianity in India, particularly in the region of Madura. Despite facing severe opposition, including torture and eventual martyrdom, he remained steadfast in his mission to convert and aid the local population. He was canonized in 1947, recognized for his unwavering faith and commitment to serving others.","/images/saint_john_de_britto.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_de_Britto","02-16"],"John of Egypt":["4th century",null,"A model of solitude and obedience","John of Egypt was a solitary monk known for his deep commitment to prayer and obedience. After leaving his life as a carpenter, he retreated to the desert where he developed a reputation for his spiritual gifts, including healing and prophecy. He is remembered for his teachings on humility and the dangers of vanity, as well as his unwavering faith in God until his death in 394.","/images/saint_john_of_egypt.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_of_Egypt","03-27"],"John of God":["16th century",["hospitals","the sick","firefighters"],"Patron of hospitals and the sick","John of God was a Portuguese saint known for his deep compassion and dedication to the sick and poor. After a tumultuous early life, he founded the Brothers Hospitallers, an order devoted to caring for the needy. His legacy endures through his commitment to charity and the care of the marginalized, inspiring many in the field of healthcare and social service.","/images/saint_john_of_god.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_of_God","03-08"],"John of Matha":["Medieval",["captives","prisoners","trinitarians"],"Champion of captives and the poor","Saint John of Matha was a French priest and the founder of the Order of the Holy Trinity, dedicated to the redemption of Christian captives. He devoted his life to serving the poor and rescuing those enslaved during the Crusades. His work was recognized by Pope Innocent III, who approved the establishment of his order in 1198.","/images/saint_john_of_matha.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_of_Matha","02-08"],"John of Sahagun":["15th century",["preachers","theologians","those suffering from poison"],"A model of holiness and reform","John of Sahagun was a Spanish Augustinian confessor known for his powerful preaching and moral reform in Salamanca during the 15th century. He dedicated his life to the poor and was recognized for his deep devotion to the Holy Mass, which often led him to spend extended time in prayer. His courageous denouncement of vice ultimately led to his martyr-like death, and he was canonized in 1690 due to the miracles attributed to him after his passing.","/images/saint_john_of_sahagun_or_st_fagondez.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_of_Sahag%C3%BAn","06-12"],"John of the Cross":["16th century",["mystics","poets","Spanish poets"],"A guide to mystical union with God.","John of the Cross was a Spanish mystic and a major figure of the Counter-Reformation. He co-founded the Discalced Carmelites with Saint Teresa of Ávila and is known for his profound spiritual writings, which explore the soul's journey toward God. He was declared a Doctor of the Church in 1926 for his contributions to mystical theology.","/images/saint_john_of_the_cross.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_of_the_Cross","11-24"],"John the Almsgiver":["6th century",["almsgivers","the poor","charity"],"Champion of the poor and almsgiving","John the Almsgiver was a 6th-century Patriarch of Alexandria known for his extraordinary generosity and commitment to helping the poor. After losing his family, he dedicated his life to almsgiving, feeding thousands of needy individuals daily. His legacy includes the establishment of numerous churches and a reputation for unwavering faith and charity.","/images/saint_john_the_almsgiver.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_the_Almsgiver","04-09"],"John the Baptist":["1st century",["baptism","conversions","painters","printers"],"Forerunner of Christ and martyr","John the Baptist is a significant figure in Christianity, known for his role as the forerunner to Jesus Christ. He preached repentance and baptized Jesus in the Jordan River, marking the beginning of Christ's public ministry. His life was characterized by asceticism and a deep commitment to God, ultimately leading to his martyrdom at the hands of Herod Antipas.","/images/saint_john_the_baptist.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_the_Baptist","06-24"],"John the Discalced":["14th century",null,"A life of humility and service","Blessed John the Discalced was a Franciscan friar known for his austere lifestyle and dedication to serving the poor and sick. He spent many years as a parish priest, walking barefoot to visit his parishioners and living a life of extreme mortification. He is remembered for his prophetic gifts and his compassionate service during times of plague, ultimately dying from the very scourge he sought to alleviate.","/images/blessed_john_the_discalced.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_of_the_Cross","12-15"],"John the Silent":["6th century",null,"A model of silence and prayer","John the Silent was a bishop and monk known for his deep commitment to silence and prayer. Born in Armenia, he founded a monastery and lived a life of austerity, often retreating into the wilderness. He guided many souls towards God through his example and teachings until his death around the year 558.","/images/saint_john_the_silent.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_the_Silent","05-13"],"Jonas and Barachisius":["4th century",null,"Martyrs of unwavering faith and courage","Saints Jonas and Barachisius were brothers and martyrs who lived during the reign of King Sapor of Persia. They courageously encouraged fellow Christians facing persecution and ultimately faced brutal tortures for their steadfast faith. Their unwavering commitment to Christ and their martyrdom serve as a powerful testament to the strength of Christian virtue in the face of suffering.","/images/saints_jonas_and_barachisius.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jonas_and_Barachisius","03-29"],"Josaphat":["17th century",["unity among Christians","church unity"],"Martyr for church unity and reconciliation.","Saint Josaphat was a Polish archbishop and martyr known for his efforts to unite the divided Ruthenian Church. He dedicated his life to preaching and restoring the faith among those who had separated from the Holy See. Josaphat was assassinated in 1623 due to his unwavering commitment to the Union, and he was canonized by Pope Pius IX in 1867.","/images/saint_josaphat.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Josaphat","11-14"],"Joseph":["1st century",["workers","fathers","families","universal Church"],"Model of fatherhood and family life","Saint Joseph is known as the spouse of the Virgin Mary and the earthly father of Jesus Christ. He is celebrated for his obedience to God's will, exemplified by his acceptance of his role as the guardian of the Holy Family. Revered as a model for fathers and families, he is invoked for a happy death and is the patron saint of workers and the universal Church.","/images/saint_joseph.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joseph_of_Nazareth","03-19,05-01"],"Joseph Benedict Cottolengo":["19th century",["the sick","the poor","orphaned children"],"Compassionate care for the marginalized","Joseph Benedict Cottolengo was an Italian priest known for his deep compassion for the sick and poor. He founded the Piccola Casa della Divina Provvidenza, a community dedicated to caring for those in need, which grew into a city of over 7,000 individuals. His legacy includes the establishment of fourteen religious communities focused on both corporal and spiritual works of mercy.","/images/saint_joseph_benedict_cottolengo.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giuseppe_Benedetto_Cottolengo","04-29"],"Joseph Calasanctius":["17th century",["teachers","students","the poor"],"Pioneer of free education for the poor","Joseph Calasanctius was a Spanish priest and the founder of the Piarists, an order dedicated to the education of poor children. He established schools in Rome that provided free education, emphasizing religious instruction alongside basic academics. Despite facing opposition and challenges, he remained committed to his mission until his death in 1648.","/images/saint_joseph_calasanctius.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joseph_Calasanctius","08-27"],"Joseph Chiwatenhwa":["17th century",["Hurons","martyrs","Christian converts"],"Apostle and martyr of the Hurons","Joseph Chiwatenhwa was a Huron martyr and a devoted Christian who lived in the early 17th century. He was baptized by Jesuit missionaries and became an apostle to his people, promoting Christianity and leading by example in his family and community. Tragically, he was martyred in 1640, but his legacy of faith inspired many conversions among the Hurons after his death.","/images/blessed_joseph_chiwatenhwa.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joseph_Chiwatenhwa","08-02"],"Joseph of Cupertino":["17th century",["students","aviators","astronauts"],"Patron saint of students and aviators","Joseph of Cupertino was a Franciscan priest known for his deep spirituality and mystical experiences, often described as being in ecstasy. Despite facing numerous challenges, including a lack of formal education and ridicule from others, he became a beloved figure for his humility and devotion. He is particularly remembered for his miraculous levitations during prayer and his unwavering faith until his death in 1663.","/images/saint_joseph_of_cupertino.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joseph_of_Cupertino","09-18"],"Juan Diego":["16th century",["indigenous peoples","Mexico"],"Symbol of faith for indigenous peoples","Juan Diego is the first native-born saint of the Americas, canonized in 2002. He is best known for his visions of the Virgin Mary, who appeared to him in 1531 and requested that a church be built in her honor at Tepeyac Hill, Mexico. His tilma, which bore the image of the Virgin of Guadalupe, became a symbol of faith and devotion for millions.","/images/saint_juan_diego.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juan_Diego","12-09"],"Julia":[null,null,"Virgin, Martyr","was a noble virgin of Carthage, who, when the city was taken by Genseric in 439, was sold for a slave to a pagan merchant of Syria. In the most mortifying employments of her station, by cheerfulness and patience she found a happiness and comfort which the world could not give. Whenever she was not employed in household affairs, her time was devoted to prayer and reading books of piety.","/images/saint_julia.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Julia_of_Corsica","05-23"],"Julian":["4th century",["hospitals","the sick","martyrs"],"Patron of hospitals and the sick","Saint Julian, known as the Hospitaler, was a martyr who lived in Egypt during the early 4th century. Together with his wife, Basilissa, he dedicated his life to charity, converting their home into a hospital for the poor and sick. After enduring persecution for his faith, Julian was ultimately martyred in 313, and his tomb became a site of numerous miracles.","/images/saint_julian_martyr_and_saint_basilissa.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Julian_the_Hospitaller","01-09"],"Juliana Falconieri":["Medieval",["sick people","those suffering from stomach ailments"],"Devotion and charity in service to others.","Juliana Falconieri was a 14th-century virgin and the foundress of the Mantellate, a branch of the Servite Order. Known for her deep devotion and modesty, she dedicated her life to serving others and leading a life of apostolic charity. Her death was marked by a miraculous sign, with the form of the Blessed Sacrament appearing on her heart.","/images/saint_juliana_falconieri.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juliana_Falconieri","06-19"],"Juliana of Mt. Cornillon":["13th century",null,"Virgin","Saint Juliana was born near Liege, Belgium in 1193. At the age of five she lost her parents and was placed in the convent of Mt. She made rapid progress in virtue, and read with pleasure the writings of Saint Augustine and Saint Bernard.","/images/saint_juliana_of_mont_cornillon.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juliana_of_Li%C3%A8ge","04-07"],"Julitta":["4th century",["mothers","martyrs"],"A symbol of maternal devotion and faith","Saint Julitta was a wealthy Christian martyr who, along with her young son Cyricus, suffered persecution during the reign of Emperor Diocletian. They were arrested for their faith, and despite the cruel tortures inflicted upon them, both remained steadfast in their belief in Christ. Julitta's courage and unwavering faith in the face of death have made her a symbol of maternal devotion and martyrdom in Christian tradition.","/images/saint_julitta_and_saint_cyricus.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Julitta","06-16"],"Julius I":["4th century",null,"Defender of Nicene Christianity","Julius I was a Roman Pope who served from 337 to 352 AD. He is known for his defense of the Nicene faith against Arianism and for welcoming exiled bishops to Rome. His papacy was marked by significant ecclesiastical reforms and the construction of basilicas and cemeteries in the city.","/images/saint_julius_i.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Julius_I","04-12"],"Justin of Naplouse":["2nd century",null,"Martyr","Saint Justin was born in about the year 103 of pagan parents at Neapolis or Sichem in Samaria, site of Jacob's well. He was well educated and applied himself in particular to the study of philosophy, always with one object — that he might come to know God. He sought this knowledge among the contending schools of philosophy, but in vain; and finally God appeased the thirst He Himself had created.","/images/saint_justin_martyr.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Justin_Martyr","04-14"],"Kateri Tekakwitha":["17th century",["environmentalists","Native Americans","people in exile"],"First Native American saint in the U.S.","Kateri Tekakwitha, known as the Lily of the Mohawk, was born in 1656 to an Algonquin mother and an Iroquois chieftain. After experiencing personal tragedy and a strong spiritual calling, she dedicated her life to God and became a symbol of faith among Native Americans. She was canonized in 1991 and is recognized for her deep devotion and commitment to her Christian faith.","/images/saint_kateri_tekakwitha.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kateri_Tekakwitha","04-17"],"Ladislas I":["Medieval",["Hungary","soldiers","peace","children"],"A pious king and defender of faith","Ladislas I was the King of Hungary from 1077 until his death in 1095, known for his piety, charity, and military prowess. He was a strong defender of the Christian faith and worked to restore the laws established by his predecessor, Saint Stephen. Canonized in 1199, he is remembered for his virtuous life and the miracles attributed to him after his death.","/images/saint_ladislas_i.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ladislaus_I_of_Hungary","06-27"],"Lambert":["7th century",["bishops","stone masons","fishermen"],"Martyrdom for faith and integrity","Saint Lambert was a bishop of Maestricht and a martyr who lived in the 7th century. Known for his miracles and dedication to the Gospel, he was chosen as a successor to Bishop Theodard at a young age. His strong opposition to moral corruption ultimately led to his assassination in 705.","/images/saint_lambert.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lambert_of_Maastricht","09-17"],"Laurence O'Toole":["12th century",["Ireland","archbishops","the poor"],"Champion of the poor and peacemaker","Laurence O'Toole was the Archbishop of Dublin and a prominent figure in the Church during the 12th century. Known for his deep piety and commitment to the poor, he dedicated his life to serving the needs of his community and promoting ecclesiastical discipline in Ireland. He is remembered for his miraculous healing and his efforts to reconcile conflicts, ultimately becoming a martyr for his faith.","/images/saint_laurence_o_toole.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laurence_O%27Toole","11-14"],"Lawrence":["3rd century",["deacons","poor","cooks","firefighters"],"Patron of the poor and deacons","Saint Lawrence was a deacon in Rome and is known for his unwavering faith and charity towards the poor. He was martyred in 258 AD during the persecution of Christians under Emperor Valerian, famously enduring torture with humor. His legacy as a patron of the poor and a symbol of charity continues to inspire many in the Catholic faith.","/images/saint_lawrence.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lawrence_of_Rome","08-10"],"Lawrence Justinian":["15th century",["patrons of Venice","clergy","spiritual leaders"],"First Patriarch of Venice and reformer","Lawrence Justinian was the first Patriarch of Venice, known for his deep spirituality and commitment to reforming the Church. He founded numerous monasteries and churches, emphasizing humility and the importance of frequent Communion. His legacy is marked by his dedication to the poor and his exemplary life of mortification and prayer.","/images/saint_lawrence_justinian.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lawrence_Justinian","09-05"],"Leander":["6th century",["Spain","the Visigoths","conversions"],"Champion of faith and unity in Spain","Saint Leander was an influential Archbishop of Seville in the 6th century, known for his role in converting the Visigoths from Arianism to Catholicism. He was instrumental in the conversion of his nephew, Saint Hermenegild, and later helped King Recared embrace the Catholic faith, leading to the unification of the Suevi and Visigoths under Catholicism. His legacy includes the introduction of the Nicene Creed into the liturgy of Spain, which eventually spread throughout the Church.","/images/saint_leander.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leander_of_Seville","02-27"],"Leo II":["7th century",null,"A Pope of music and charity","Leo II was a Pope in the 7th century known for his brief but impactful pontificate. He addressed significant issues between the Holy See and the emperors of Constantinople, ensuring that bishops of Ravenna were consecrated in Rome. His contributions to music and charity, along with his efforts to uphold Church doctrine, left a lasting legacy.","/images/saint_leo_ii.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Leo_II","07-03"],"Leo IX":["Medieval",null,"Reformer and defender of Church doctrine","Leo IX was Pope from 1049 to 1054 and is known for his significant reforms within the Church. He condemned simony and worked to enforce ecclesiastical discipline while also opposing the doctrines of Berengarius regarding Transubstantiation. His papacy marked the beginning of the schism between the Roman Catholic and Eastern Orthodox Churches.","/images/saint_leo_ix.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leo_IX","04-19"],"Leo the Great":["5th century",["popes","theologians","Rome"],"Defender of the faith and Church unity","Leo the Great was a Pope who played a crucial role in defining the doctrine of the Incarnation and combating heresies during a tumultuous time for the Church. He is best known for his theological writings and for successfully negotiating with Attila the Hun to spare Rome from destruction. His leadership and teachings have left a lasting impact on the Catholic Church and its understanding of Christ's nature.","/images/saint_leo_the_great.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leo_the_Great","04-11"],"Leocadia":["4th century",["Toledo","virgins","martyrs"],"Patroness of Toledo and Christian martyrs","Saint Leocadia of Toledo was a Christian virgin and martyr who lived during the early 4th century. She was arrested and tortured for her faith under the Roman governor Dacian, ultimately dying in prison. Her legacy endures as she is venerated as the principal patroness of Toledo, where her relics are honored.","/images/saint_leocadia_of_toledo.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leocadia_of_Toledo","12-09"],"Leonard":["6th century",["prisoners","women in childbirth"],"Patron saint of prisoners and freedom","Saint Leonard was a hermit and the patron saint of prisoners, known for his deep compassion and dedication to helping those in need. He gained a reputation for performing miracles, particularly in freeing prisoners from their chains through prayer. His legacy continues to inspire devotion, especially in France, where many churches and monasteries are dedicated to him.","/images/saint_leonard.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leonard_of_Noblac","11-06"],"Leonides":["2nd century",null,"Father of Origen and early martyr","Leonides was a Christian martyr and the father of the theologian Origen. He was executed during the persecution of Christians under Emperor Severus in 202 AD. His legacy is marked by his dedication to faith and the profound influence he had on his son, who became one of the most significant early Christian scholars.","/images/saint_leonides.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leonides_of_Alexandria","04-22"],"Liberatus":["5th century",null,"Martyrdom for faith and unity in Christ.","Saint Liberatus was a Christian martyr who lived during the reign of the Arian Vandal king Huneric in Africa. He, along with six companions, was imprisoned and tortured for their steadfast faith in the Trinity and Baptism. Ultimately, they were executed by being burned at sea, but their legacy of courage and faith continues to inspire Christians today.","/images/saint_liberatus_and_companions.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liberatus_of_Carthage","08-17"],"Linus":["1st century",null,"Second Pope and early Christian martyr","Saint Linus was a Pope and martyr who served as the second Pope of the Catholic Church after Saint Peter. Originally from Volterra in Tuscany, he was known for his zeal in preaching and converting idolaters, particularly in Gaul. He was martyred for his faith and is buried near Saint Peter in the Vatican.","/images/saint_linus.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pope_Linus","09-23"],"Louis Bertrand":["16th century",["missionaries","indigenous peoples"],"Champion of indigenous rights and faith","Louis Bertrand was a Spanish Dominican missionary known for his dedicated work in converting indigenous peoples in the Americas during the 16th century. He exhibited remarkable gentleness and compassion, even in the face of adversity, and was blessed with the gift of prophecy and miracles. His legacy includes the baptism of thousands and a commitment to advocating for the rights of the oppressed indigenous communities.","/images/saint_louis_bertrand.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Louis_Bertrand_(saint)","10-09"],"Louis IX":["Medieval",["France","crusaders","lepers","barbers","the dying"],"A model of Christian kingship and justice.","Louis IX, also known as Saint Louis, was the King of France from 1226 until his death in 1270. Renowned for his deep piety and commitment to justice, he led two Crusades and was a protector of the weak and oppressed. His legacy includes the construction of the Sainte Chapelle in Paris, which houses the Crown of Thorns, and he is remembered as a model of Christian kingship.","/images/saint_louis_ix.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Louis_IX_of_France","08-25"],"Louis Mary de Montfort":["18th century",null,"Doctor of Mary","One of the great Saints whose mission appears verified and on the increase as the years pass and as we find ourselves amid the latter times, Saint Louis Mary de Montfort can now be recognized as a prophet and an oracle of God for the sanctification of the Church which must resist the foretold evils of this period. Author of a Prophetic Prayer Requesting the Apostles of the Latter Times, he is also the ardent apostle of True Devotion to the Blessed Virgin and the Saint of love for the Cross of the Lord, as we see from his Letter to the Friends of the Cross and his entire life of missionary activity. Born at Montfort-la-Cane near Saint-Malo in 1673, he was the oldest of eight children.","/images/saint_louis_mary_de_montfort.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Louis_de_Montfort","04-28"],"Louis of Toulouse":["Medieval",["poor","students","Franciscans"],"A life of humility and service","Louis of Toulouse was a bishop known for his deep piety and commitment to serving God from a young age. Despite being born into nobility, he chose a life of humility and service, dedicating himself to the care of the poor and the sick. He became the Archbishop of Toulouse and is remembered for his austere lifestyle and devotion until his early death at the age of twenty-three.","/images/saint_louis_of_toulouse.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Louis_of_Toulouse","08-19"],"Luchesio":["13th century",["merchants","the poor","third order of Saint Francis"],"First member of the Franciscan Third Order","Luchesio was a wealthy merchant from Poggibonzi, Italy, who underwent a profound conversion after hearing Saint Francis of Assisi preach. He became the first member of the Third Order of Saint Francis, dedicating his life to charity and the service of the poor. Alongside his wife, Bona Dona, he established a community that provided for the needy and became known for their acts of mercy.","/images/blessed_luchesio.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Luchesius_Modestini","04-28"],"Lucian":["Early 4th century",null,"Defender of Scripture and Christian faith","Saint Lucian was a priest and martyr from Samosata in Syria, known for his dedication to studying and revising the Scriptures. He translated the Hebrew Bible into Greek, which greatly influenced later biblical translations, including the Vulgate by Saint Jerome. Lucian was imprisoned and tortured for his faith during the persecution of Maximin, ultimately dying as a martyr while affirming his Christian identity.","/images/saint_lucian_the_syrian.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lucian_of_Antioch","01-07"],"Lucy of Syracuse":["4th century",["blindness","martyrs","writers","throat diseases"],"Patroness of the blind and martyrs","Saint Lucy of Syracuse was a young Christian martyr who lived in the early 4th century. She is known for her unwavering faith and miraculous healing of her mother, which led her to dedicate her life to God and serve the poor. Ultimately, she was martyred for her beliefs during the persecution of Diocletian, becoming a symbol of purity and resilience in the face of adversity.","/images/saint_lucy_of_syracuse.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lucy_of_Syracuse","12-13"],"Ludger":["8th century",["Westphalia","Friesland","educators"],"Pioneer of Christian faith in Northern Europe","Saint Ludger was the first Bishop of Munster and played a significant role in the Christianization of the Saxons in Friesland. Educated under Saint Gregory of Utrecht and Alcuin in England, he founded several monasteries and churches during his missionary work. Known for his deep piety and miracles, he continued his pastoral duties until his death on March 26, 809.","/images/saint_ludger.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ludger","03-26"],"Luke":["1st century",["physicians","artists","butchers","bakers"],"Evangelist and companion of Saint Paul","Luke is recognized as the author of the Gospel of Luke and the Acts of the Apostles, providing a detailed account of the life of Jesus and the early Church. A physician by profession, he was a companion of Saint Paul and played a significant role in the spread of Christianity. His artistic depictions of the Virgin Mary and his meticulous writings have made him a revered figure in Christian tradition.","/images/saint_luke.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Luke_the_Evangelist","10-18"],"Lydwina of Schiedam":["15th century",null,"Virgin","Saint Lydwina was born in Holland of virtuous but poor parents, the only daughter among nine children. Her name means suffer in plenitude , and indeed her entire life was nothing but a continuous suffering. Even in the cradle a grievous illness afflicted her.","/images/saint_lydwina_of_schiedam.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lidwina","04-14"],"Macarius of Alexandria":["4th century",null,"Exemplar of hermitic spirituality and prayer","Saint Macarius of Alexandria was a hermit and a disciple of Saint Anthony the Great. He is known for his intense spiritual practices and struggles against temptation, often engaging in extreme forms of mortification. His life exemplifies the pursuit of holiness through prayer, vigilance, and self-denial.","/images/saint_macarius_of_alexandria.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Macarius_of_Alexandria","01-02"],"Magloire":["6th century",null,"Devoted abbot and miracle worker","Saint Magloire was an abbot from Brittany who dedicated his life to monastic service and prayer. He governed the monastery at Lanmeur for over fifty years before becoming the Abbot of Dol. Known for his piety and miraculous intercessions, he is remembered for his deep devotion and the establishment of a monastery on the Island of Jersey.","/images/saint_magloire.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magloire","10-24"],"Malachy":["12th century",["Ireland","priests","the diocese of Armagh"],"Prophet of future popes and reformer","Saint Malachy was a 12th-century Irish bishop known for his dedication to reforming the Church and his prophetic visions regarding future popes. He served as the Archbishop of Armagh and was instrumental in revitalizing the faith among his people, enduring significant hardships during his ministry. His legacy includes a famous prophecy listing the mottos of future popes, which has intrigued scholars and the faithful alike.","/images/saint_malachy_d_armagh.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Malachy","11-02"],"Mammertus":["5th century",null,"Archbishop of Vienne","Saint Mammertus, Archbishop of Vienne in Dauphiné during the 5 th century, was a prelate renowned for his sanctity, learning, and miracles. He instituted in his diocese the fasts and supplications called the Rogations , during the three days before the Ascension, to remedy the neglect of religion which brought down on ancient Gaul many chastisements. Almighty God, to punish the sins of the people, had visited them with wars and other public calamities and awakened the city of Vienne in particular from spiritual lethargy by the terrors of earthquakes, fires, and ravenous wild beasts, which were sometimes seen in the very market place.","/images/saint_mammertus.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mamertus","05-11"],"Marcella":["Early 5th century",["widows","noble women","spiritual growth"],"Model of Christian widowhood and piety","Marcella was a noble widow in Rome who dedicated her life to asceticism and the study of Scripture under the guidance of Saint Jerome. She became a leader among Christian women, gathering them for spiritual growth and support. Marcella died during the Gothic siege of Rome in 410, having lived a life of piety and service to the poor.","/images/saint_marcella.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Marcella","01-31"],"Marcellinus":["4th century",null,"Bishop of Embrun","Saint Marcellinus was born in Africa of a noble family; with two other Christians desiring to evangelize Gaul, Vincent and Domninus, he went to Pope Saint Damasus. The young men were sent to Eusebius, bishop of Verceil, who encouraged them for their mission and announced to them that they would have much to suffer. The three were ordained, and Marcellinus was consecrated bishop of Embrun by Eusebius and the bishop of Valencia.","/images/saint_marcellinus_of_embrun.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marcellinus_of_Gaul","04-20"],"Marcellus":["3rd century",null,"Papal leadership during early Christian persecution","Saint Marcellus was the Pope during a tumultuous time for the early Church, facing severe persecution under Emperor Diocletian. He reorganized the Church's structure, establishing parishes to better serve the faithful and address the needs of the growing Christian community. Ultimately, he was martyred for his steadfast leadership and commitment to maintaining Church discipline amidst the challenges of apostasy.","/images/saint_marcellus.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marcellus_I","01-16,10-30"],"Marcus and Marcellianus":["3rd century",null,"Martyrs of steadfast faith and courage","Saints Marcus and Marcellianus were twin brothers and martyrs who lived in Rome during the reign of Emperor Diocletian. They were imprisoned and ultimately executed for refusing to renounce their Christian faith despite the pleas of their family. Their legacy is honored particularly in Spain, where they are credited with interceding for the city's safety.","/images/saint_marcus_and_saint_marcellianus.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marcus_and_Marcellianus","06-18"],"Margaret":["Medieval",["Scotland","difficult marriages","widows"],"A queen of faith and charity","Saint Margaret of Scotland was a queen known for her deep piety and commitment to the Church. She was instrumental in the spiritual and material improvement of her realm, building churches and educating her children in the faith. Her life exemplified a balance of royal duty and personal holiness, leading her to be revered as a saint after her death in 1093.","/images/saint_margaret_queen_of_scotland.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Margaret_of_Scotland","06-10"],"Margaret Bourgeoys":["17th century",["teachers","Canada","education"],"Pioneer of education in Canada","Margaret Bourgeoys was a French nun and the founder of the Congregation of Notre Dame, dedicated to the education of children in Canada. She arrived in New France in 1653 and established the first school in Montreal, significantly contributing to the education and social welfare of the community. Her legacy includes the establishment of numerous schools and her dedication to the spiritual and practical guidance of young women and families.","/images/saint_margaret_bourgeoys.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marguerite_Bourgeoys","01-12"],"Margaret Mary Alacoque":["17th century",["devotion to the Sacred Heart","loss of parents","sick people"],"Apostle of the Sacred Heart of Jesus","Saint Margaret Mary Alacoque was a French nun and mystic known for her visions of the Sacred Heart of Jesus. She played a pivotal role in promoting the devotion to the Sacred Heart, which emphasizes God's love for humanity. Canonized in 1920, her legacy continues to inspire devotion and faith among Catholics worldwide.","/images/saint_margaret_mary_alacoque.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Margaret_Mary_Alacoque","10-17"],"Margaret d'Youville":["18th century",["the poor","hospital workers","widows"],"Pioneer of charity and healthcare in Canada","Margaret d'Youville was a Canadian foundress known for establishing the Sisters of Charity, also known as the Grey Nuns, in Montreal. After becoming a widow, she dedicated her life to serving the poor, the sick, and the marginalized in her community. Her legacy continues through the ongoing work of the Grey Nuns, who have expanded their mission across North America.","/images/saint_margaret_d_youville.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marie-Marguerite_d%27Youville","12-23"],"Margaret of Antioch":["3rd century",["pregnant women","childbirth","falsely accused"],"Courageous martyr and defender of faith","Saint Margaret of Antioch was a Christian virgin and martyr born in the third century in Antioch of Pisidia. She faced persecution for her faith, refusing the advances of a Roman prefect and enduring severe tortures, including imprisonment and beheading. Her steadfastness in faith and her eloquent defense of Christianity inspired many, leading to her martyrdom and veneration as a saint.","/images/saint_margaret_of_antioch.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Margaret_of_Antioch","07-20"],"Margaret of Cortona":["Medieval",["penitents","the homeless","single mothers"],"A beacon of hope for sinners","Margaret of Cortona was a 13th-century Franciscan tertiary who transformed her life from one of sin to one of profound holiness after the murder of her lover. She dedicated herself to serving the poor and founded a hospital and a congregation to assist those in need. Known for her visions and miraculous intercessions, she became a beloved figure in Tuscany, often referred to as the 'lily of the valley.'","/images/saint_margaret_of_cortona.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Margaret_of_Cortona","02-22"],"Maria Goretti":["20th century",["young women","purity","victims of rape"],"Patroness of purity and forgiveness","Saint Maria Goretti was an Italian virgin and martyr who died at the age of eleven after refusing to submit to an assault. Her forgiveness of her attacker before her death and her subsequent canonization highlight her profound faith and purity. She is celebrated as a symbol of chastity and forgiveness in the Catholic Church.","/images/saint_maria_goretti.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maria_Goretti","07-09"],"Mark":[null,null,"Evangelist","Saint Mark was converted to the Faith by the Prince of the Apostles, whom he later accompanied to Rome, acting there as his secretary or interpreter. When Saint Peter wrote his First Epistle to the churches of Asia, he affectionately joined to his own salutation that of his faithful companion, whom he calls my son Mark. The Roman people entreated Saint Mark to put in writing for them the substance of Saint Peter's frequent discourses on Our Lord's life.","/images/saint_mark_evangelist.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mark_the_Evangelist","04-25"],"Martha":["1st century",["cooks","servants","hospitality"],"Model of service and faith in Christ","Martha of Bethany is known for her role in the New Testament as the sister of Mary and Lazarus, and she is depicted as a devoted servant of Jesus. She is celebrated for her strong faith, particularly in the story of Lazarus's resurrection, where she expresses her belief in Jesus's power. Tradition holds that after the death of Jesus, she traveled to France, where she lived a life of austerity and holiness.","/images/saint_martha.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Martha_of_Bethany","07-29"],"Martin I":["7th century",null,"Defender of the true faith","Martin I served as Pope from 649 to 655 and is remembered for his staunch defense of the true faith against heresies, particularly the Monothelite heresy. He was exiled and suffered greatly for his beliefs, enduring imprisonment and starvation, yet he remained steadfast in his faith. His legacy is one of courage and commitment to the teachings of Christ, exemplifying the trials faced by early Church leaders.","/images/saint_martin_i.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Martin_I","11-12"],"Martin de Porres":["17th century",["social justice","mixed-race people","innocent victims","the poor"],"Compassionate healer and advocate for the poor","Martin de Porres was a Dominican lay brother born in Lima, Peru, in 1579. Known for his deep humility and extraordinary compassion, he dedicated his life to serving the poor and sick, often performing miracles of healing. Canonized in 1962, he is remembered as a powerful intercessor for those in need.","/images/saint_martin_de_porres.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Martin_de_Porres","11-03"],"Martin of Tours":["4th century",["soldiers","horses","winemakers","beggars"],"Apostle of Gaul and model of charity","Martin of Tours was a 4th-century bishop known for his missionary work in Gaul. He is famous for his act of charity where he shared his cloak with a beggar, leading to a vision of Christ. His efforts in evangelizing and converting the pagan population earned him the title of the Light and Apostle of Gaul.","/images/saint_martin_of_tours.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Martin_of_Tours","11-11"],"Martina":["3rd century",null,"Martyrdom and unwavering faith in Christ","Saint Martina was a Roman virgin and martyr who lived during the early 3rd century. After losing her parents at a young age, she dedicated her life to Christ and the poor, ultimately facing persecution under Emperor Alexander Severus. Despite enduring severe torture and miraculous events, she remained steadfast in her faith and was ultimately beheaded for refusing to renounce her beliefs.","/images/saint_martina.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Martina_of_Rome","01-30"],"Mary Assunta":["20th century",null,"Franciscan Missionary","Saint Pius X promulgated his decree Quam Singulari, recommending frequent Holy Communion for all and early First Communion for children, just two years after the death of Blessed Mary Assunta. This holy young nun, especially devoted to the Blessed Sacrament in her youth, though she could not yet benefit from the decree, is a living proof to the wisdom of his decision. Saints among children and youth, whom he foretold would multiply, have always had such devotion, and thus God shows us that it is not the number of years that counts.","/images/blessed_mary_assunta.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maria_Assunta_Pallotta","04-08"],"Mary Euphrasia Pelletier":["19th century",["women","girls","social workers"],"Champion of women and social justice","Mary Euphrasia Pelletier was a French nun and the foundress of the Congregation of Our Lady of Charity of the Good Shepherd. She dedicated her life to the care and rehabilitation of women and girls, establishing numerous homes for those in need. Canonized in 1940, she is remembered for her profound compassion and commitment to social justice.","/images/saint_mary_euphrasia_pelletier.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mary_Euphrasia_Pelletier","04-24"],"Mary Magdalen":["1st century",["penitents","women","converts"],"Symbol of repentance and devotion","Mary Magdalen is a prominent figure in the New Testament, known for her deep devotion to Jesus Christ. She was a witness to His crucifixion and the first to encounter the resurrected Christ, receiving the message of His resurrection to share with the Apostles. Her life exemplifies repentance and the transformative power of love and faith in Jesus.","/images/saint_mary_magdalen.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mary_Magdalene","07-22"],"Mary Magdalene of Pazzi":["17th century",null,"Carmelite","Saint Mary Magdalene of Pazzi was the only daughter of the illustrious Camille de Pazzi, related to the Medicis of Florence. She was born in the year 1566, and was baptized with the name of Catherine. As a child she loved to go into solitary places to enter into prayer with God, who revealed Himself to her from her tender years without the aid of teachers, as her Creator, Redeemer and Sanctifier.","/images/saint_mary_magdalene_of_pazzi.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mary_Magdalene_de'_Pazzi","05-29"],"Mary Micaela":["19th century",["young girls","education","charity"],"A pioneer in education and charity","Mary Micaela, also known as Madre Sacramento, was a Spanish foundress who dedicated her life to the education and reformation of young girls in need. She established the Institute of Religious Adorer-Slaves of the Most Blessed Sacrament and of Charity, focusing on a deep love for the Eucharist and service to others. Beatified in 1925 and canonized in 1934, her legacy continues through her religious order and charitable works.","/images/saint_mary_micaela.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maria_Micaela_Desmaisieres","08-25"],"Mary of Egypt":["5th century",["penitents","converts"],"A symbol of repentance and redemption.","Mary of Egypt was a former prostitute who underwent a profound conversion after a pilgrimage to Jerusalem. After realizing her sinful life, she retreated to the wilderness where she lived in penance for the remainder of her life. Her story serves as a powerful testament to the possibility of redemption and the grace of God.","/images/saint_mary_of_egypt.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mary_of_Egypt","04-09"],"Mary of Jesus Deluil-Martiny":["19th century",null,"Foundress of the Daughters of the Heart of Jesus","Mary of Jesus Deluil-Martiny was a French nun and the foundress of the Daughters of the Heart of Jesus, a religious congregation dedicated to the adoration of the Sacred Heart of Jesus. Born in Marseille in 1841, she displayed remarkable spiritual depth and leadership from a young age, leading her to establish her congregation in 1873. Her life was marked by personal trials and a deep commitment to prayer and sacrifice for the glory of God and the sanctification of priests.","/images/saint_mary_of_jesus_deluil-martiny.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marie_Deluil-Martiny","02-27"],"Mary of Oignies":["Medieval",null,"A model of mystical devotion and charity.","Mary of Oignies was a Belgian recluse born in 1167 who dedicated her life to prayer and contemplation. Despite being married, she and her husband chose to live in continence and devoted themselves to the poor and spiritual works. Known for her deep spirituality and mystical experiences, she became a respected figure for her intercessory prayers and was venerated after her death in 1213.","/images/saint_mary_of_oignies.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marie_of_Oignies","06-23"],"Mary of the Incarnation":["17th century",null,"Carmelite","Blessed Mary of the Incarnation was born in Paris in February 1565; both of her parents were members of the most ancient families of that great city. Before she was born, several other children had seen the light of day, but all died in their infancy. During the time her mother awaited this child, she vowed her to the Blessed Virgin and Saint Claude, promising to clothe her in white until the age of seven and to offer her in a church of the Blessed Virgin.","/images/blessed_mary_of_the_incarnation_carmelite.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marie_of_the_Incarnation_(Carmelite)","04-18,06-02"],"Mary, Queen of the World":["Early Christianity",["mothers","pregnant women","the sick"],"Mother of the Savior and Queen of Heaven","Mary, the mother of Jesus, holds a special place in Catholic devotion as the Queen of Heaven and Earth. She is revered for her maternal intercession and is seen as a powerful advocate for humanity. Her feast day, celebrated on May 31st, emphasizes her role as a source of hope and peace for the world.","/images/mary-queen-of-the-world.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mary,_Queen_of_Heaven","05-31"],"Mathilda":["10th century",null,"Empress","This princess, the greatest glory of her noble family, was the daughter of Theodoric, a powerful Saxon count, and Reinhilde, a princess of Denmark. Her parents placed her very young in the monastery of Erfort, of which her grandmother Maude had become the Abbess. The young girl became in that house an accomplished model of all virtues and domestic arts.","/images/saint_mathilda.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Matilda_of_Ringelheim","03-14"],"Matthew":["1st century",["tax collectors","accountants","bankers"],"Apostle and Evangelist of Jesus Christ","Matthew, originally known as Levi, was a tax collector called by Jesus to be one of His apostles. He is traditionally credited with writing the Gospel of Matthew, which emphasizes Jesus as the fulfillment of Old Testament prophecies. After preaching in Judea, he spent many years evangelizing in Egypt and Ethiopia, where he ultimately became a martyr for his faith.","/images/saint_matthew.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Matthew_the_Apostle","09-21"],"Matthias":["1st century",["persecuted Christians","tailors"],"Apostle chosen to replace Judas","Matthias was chosen by the apostles to replace Judas Iscariot after the Ascension of Jesus. He is known for his dedication and perseverance in preaching the Gospel, particularly in Judea, where he faced persecution. Matthias is traditionally believed to have died by stoning around the year 63 AD.","/images/saint_matthias.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Matthias_the_Apostle","02-24"],"Maturinus":["4th century",["mental illness","demon possession","pottery makers"],"Patron of mental health and exorcism","Maturinus was a priest and confessor who lived in the late 4th century and is known for his miraculous abilities and dedication to the Christian faith. He converted his family from paganism and became a prominent figure in the early Church, preaching and performing miracles, particularly in Rome. His legacy includes being a patron of those suffering from mental illness and demon possession, with a significant following in Larchant, France, where his relics were venerated.","/images/saint_mathurin_of_larchant.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maturinus","11-09"],"Maurice":["3rd century",["soldiers","martyrs"],"Martyrdom for faith and loyalty","Saint Maurice was the leader of the Theban Legion, a group of Christian soldiers who refused to sacrifice to pagan gods. In 286 AD, under the orders of the emperor, Maurice and his legion were martyred for their steadfast faith. Their courageous stand against persecution has made them symbols of loyalty and sacrifice in the Christian tradition.","/images/saint_maurice_and_the_theban_legion.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Maurice","09-22"],"Maurilius":["4th century",null,"Bishop and miracle worker of Angers","Saint Maurilius was a bishop of Angers who played a significant role in the early history of the Church in France. He was known for his zeal in converting pagans and for his deep compassion, which led him to seek the resurrection of a child who had died during a Mass. His legacy includes the establishment of a church and monastery that became a center for Christian life in the region.","/images/saint_maurilius.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maurilius_of_Angers","09-13"],"Maurus":["6th century",["monks","abbots","France"],"Model of humility and monastic dedication","Saint Maurus was a Benedictine abbot known for his deep humility and miraculous abilities. He was a disciple of Saint Benedict and played a significant role in the establishment of monastic life in France, founding the abbey of Glanfeuil. His legacy includes a strong devotion among the faithful and a lasting influence on monastic practices in the region.","/images/saint_maurus_abbot.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Maurus","01-15"],"Maximilian Kolbe":["20th century",["prisoners","journalists","families","the pro-life movement"],"Martyr of charity and faith","Maximilian Kolbe was a Polish Franciscan priest known for his deep devotion to the Virgin Mary and his commitment to spreading the faith through media and missionary work. He founded the Militia of the Immaculata, an organization dedicated to promoting the veneration of Mary and the conversion of sinners. Kolbe was martyred in Auschwitz during World War II, sacrificing his life for a fellow prisoner, and is remembered for his unwavering faith and love in the face of persecution.","/images/saint_maximilian_kolbe.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maximilian_Kolbe","08-14"],"Maximin Giraud":["19th century",null,"Visionary of the La Salette apparition","Maximin Giraud was a French shepherd who, along with Melanie Calvat, witnessed the apparition of the Virgin Mary at La Salette in 1846. The apparition conveyed a message of repentance and warned of impending chastisements if the people did not return to faith. Giraud dedicated his life to sharing the message of La Salette and remained steadfast in his faith despite facing skepticism and hardship.","/images/blessed_maximin_giraud.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maximin_Giraud","09-20"],"Maximin of Verdun":["Early Medieval",null,"Miracle worker and monastic leader","Saint Maximin of Verdun was an abbot and a prominent figure in early medieval monasticism. He was known for his miraculous works, including providing for the needy during a famine and healing the blind. His leadership attracted many young men to the religious life, and he is remembered for his piety and dedication to God.","/images/saint_maximin_or_mesmin_of_verdun.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mesmin","12-15"],"Maximus":["5th century",null,"A model of humility and virtue","Saint Maximus was a bishop of Riez in the 5th century, known for his deep piety and commitment to virtue. He initially resisted becoming a bishop, preferring a life of humility and service in his monastery. His leadership was marked by gentleness and kindness, earning him the affection of his clergy and the people he served until his death in 460.","/images/saint_maximus.jpg",null,"11-27"],"Mechtildis of Hackeborn":["Medieval",null,"Influential mystic and spiritual writer","Mechtildis of Hackeborn was a 13th-century abbess known for her deep spirituality and contributions to mystical theology. She was the younger sister of Saint Gertrude the Great and served as choir mistress in her monastery. Her writings, particularly 'The Book of Special Grace,' have had a lasting impact on Christian mysticism and literature.","/images/saint_mechtildis_of_hackeborn.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mechthild_of_Hackeborn","02-26"],"Medard":["6th century",["farmers","weather","poor","against bad weather"],"Compassionate bishop and miracle worker","Saint Medard was a bishop of Noyon and Tournai in the 6th century, known for his deep compassion for the poor and his miraculous deeds. He played a significant role in the conversion of the Franks and was instrumental in the establishment of Christianity in his region. His legacy is marked by his dedication to charity and his unwavering commitment to his pastoral duties despite facing numerous challenges.","/images/saint_medard.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Medard","06-08"],"Melanie Calvat":["19th century",null,"Visionary of Our Lady of La Salette","Melanie Calvat was a French visionary and seer known for her role in the apparition of Our Lady of La Salette in 1846. She faced significant challenges throughout her life, including exile and opposition from church authorities, yet remained devoted to her mission. Her revelations emphasized the call for prayer and repentance in a time of moral decline, and she is remembered for her deep faith and commitment to the message of the Virgin Mary.","/images/blessed_melanie_calvat.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Melanie_Calvat","12-14"],"Mello of Cardiff":["Early 4th century",null,"Apostle of Rouen and miracle worker","Saint Mello of Cardiff was an early Christian bishop who played a significant role in spreading the Gospel in Neustria, now Normandy. After converting from idolatry, he was ordained by Pope Saint Steven and performed numerous miracles, including healings and even a resurrection. He is remembered for his long episcopate in Rouen, where he established a church and baptized many converts, leaving a lasting legacy in the region.","/images/saint_mello_of_cardiff.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mellonius","10-22"],"Michael":["Early Christian",["military","police","sick","who is like God?"],"Protector of the Church and faithful souls","Saint Michael is one of the principal archangels in Christian tradition, known for his role as a protector and leader of the heavenly armies. He is often depicted as a warrior who defends the faithful against the forces of evil, particularly during the final battle against Satan. His apparitions and the establishment of shrines in his honor, such as Mont-Saint-Michel, highlight his significance in the Church's history and devotion.","/images/apparition_of_saint_michael_the_archangel.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Michael_the_Archangel","05-08"],"Michael Garicoits":["19th century",null,"Founder of the Priests of the Sacred Heart","Michael Garicoits was a French priest and founder of the Priests of the Sacred Heart of Betharram. He dedicated his life to fostering devotion to the Sacred Heart and establishing a religious community focused on poverty and the interior life. His legacy continues through the spread of his congregation in various countries and their commitment to education and spiritual growth.","/images/saint_michael_garicoits.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Michel_Garico%C3%AFts","05-14"],"Michael de los Santos":["17th century",null,"Devotion through asceticism and miracles","Michael de los Santos was a Spanish Trinitarian priest known for his deep piety and commitment to a life of chastity and mortification. He joined the Trinitarians at a young age and dedicated himself to the reform of the Order while practicing extreme asceticism. Canonized in 1862, he is remembered for his miracles and the devotion he inspired in others.","/images/saint_michael_de_los_santos.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Michael_de_Sanctis","04-10"],"Michael the Archangel":["Biblical",["military personnel","police officers","paramedics","sick people"],"Protector and leader of heavenly hosts","Michael the Archangel is revered as the leader of the heavenly armies and a protector of the faithful. He is known for his role in spiritual warfare, notably defeating Lucifer and defending God's people throughout history. The Church honors him as a guardian and intercessor, especially in times of conflict and during the final judgment.","/images/saint_michael_the_archangel.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Michael_the_Archangel","09-29"],"Monica":["4th century",["mothers","wives","conversions","abused women"],"A model of maternal devotion and faith","Saint Monica was the mother of Saint Augustine, known for her deep faith and perseverance in prayer. Born in 332 in North Africa, she devoted her life to the conversion of her husband and son, enduring many trials with patience and love. Her unwavering prayers were instrumental in Augustine's eventual conversion to Christianity, making her a model of maternal devotion and faith.","/images/saint_monica.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monica_of_Hippo","05-04"],"Most Precious Blood of Jesus":[null,null,"Symbol of salvation and divine grace","The Most Precious Blood of Jesus is venerated in the Catholic Church as a source of salvation and grace. It symbolizes the sacrifice of Christ and the cleansing power it holds for humanity's sins. Devotees believe that through the Precious Blood, believers can attain eternal life and healing for their souls.","/images/most_precious_blood_of_jesus.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Feast_of_the_Most_Precious_Blood","07-01"],"Narcissus":["2nd century",null,"A devoted bishop and miracle worker","Saint Narcissus was a Bishop of Jerusalem known for his deep commitment to both religious and human disciplines. He presided over a significant council regarding the celebration of Easter and is remembered for performing miracles, including turning water into oil for church lamps. Despite facing false accusations and enemies, he remained dedicated to his flock and returned to his duties until his death in extreme old age.","/images/saint_narcissus.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Narcissus_of_Jerusalem","10-29"],"Nazarius":["1st century",null,"Martyrdom for faith and virtue","Saint Nazarius was a Christian martyr from the first century, known for his zealous preaching of the Gospel. He was baptized by Saint Linus and traveled extensively to spread Christianity, often facing persecution. Along with his disciple Celsus, he was ultimately arrested and beheaded for refusing to renounce his faith during the reign of Nero.","/images/saints_nazarius_and_celsus.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nazarius_and_Celsus","07-28"],"Nemesion of Alexandria":["3rd century",null,"Courageous martyr of early Christianity","Nemesion of Alexandria was a Christian martyr who lived during the persecution of Decius in the 3rd century. He was accused of being a Christian and, despite clearing himself of a theft charge, was subjected to torture and ultimately sentenced to death by burning. Along with him, several companions also faced martyrdom, demonstrating remarkable courage and faith in the face of persecution.","/images/saint_nemesion_of_alexandria_and_companions.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nemesion","12-19"],"Nicasius":["5th century",null,"Martyrdom for faith and leadership","Saint Nicasius was the Archbishop of Rheims in the 5th century and is remembered for his courageous leadership during the invasion of the Vandals. He urged his flock to repent and remained steadfast in his faith, ultimately sacrificing his life while praying for his people. Alongside him, his deacon Florens, lector Jocond, and sister Eutropia were martyred, and their remains became a site of veneration and miracles.","/images/saint_nicasius_and_his_companions.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nicasius_of_Rheims","12-14"],"Nicholas":["4th century",["children","sailors","travelers","prisoners"],"Protector of children and sailors","Saint Nicholas, the Archbishop of Myra, is renowned for his generosity and miraculous deeds. He dedicated his life to helping the poor and protecting the innocent, famously resurrecting three children from death. His legacy as a protector of children and sailors has made him a beloved figure in Christianity, celebrated for his compassion and kindness.","/images/saint_nicholas.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nicholas_of_Myra","12-06"],"Nicholas of Flue":["15th century",["Switzerland","hermits","peace","spirituality"],"A beacon of peace and spirituality","Nicholas of Flue was a Swiss hermit and mystic who left his family and possessions to dedicate his life to God. He became known for his deep spirituality and wisdom, attracting many visitors seeking his counsel. His efforts helped to prevent civil war in Switzerland, and he is revered for his role in promoting peace and unity.","/images/saint_nicholas_of_flue.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nicholas_of_Fl%C3%BCe","03-31"],"Nicholas of Tolentino":["Medieval",["Universal Church","souls in purgatory","the dying"],"Patron of the Universal Church and souls","Nicholas of Tolentino was a 13th-century confessor known for his deep spirituality and dedication to the service of God. He was renowned for his miracles, including resurrecting children and his profound charity towards the souls in purgatory. Canonized in 1446, he is recognized as a Patron of the Universal Church and continues to inspire devotion among the faithful.","/images/saint_nicholas_of_tolentino.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nicholas_of_Tolentino","09-10"],"Norbert":["11th century",["sick people","sacramental devotion","peace","the clergy"],"Reviver of Eucharistic devotion and reform","Saint Norbert was a nobleman who underwent a profound conversion after a life of luxury and dissipation. He founded the Premonstratensian Order, which combined the active ministry with monastic life, emphasizing the importance of the Eucharist and reforming the clergy. As Bishop of Magdeburg, he dedicated his life to preaching, reform, and combating heresy until his death at the age of fifty-three.","/images/saint_norbert.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Norbert_of_Xanten","06-06"],"Odon of Cluny":["10th century",["monks","educators","children"],"Pioneer of monastic reform and education","Odon of Cluny was a Benedictine abbot who played a pivotal role in the reform and organization of monastic life in the 10th century. He founded the Abbey of Cluny, which became a center of spiritual and educational excellence, attracting many vocations and promoting the Rule of Saint Benedict. His legacy includes a commitment to charity and the education of children, as well as the establishment of a federation of monasteries under Cluny's guidance.","/images/saint_odon_or_eudes_of_cluny.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Odo_of_Cluny","11-18"],"Olympia of Constantinople":["Early Christian",["widows","deaconesses","the poor"],"Model of charity and steadfast faith","Saint Olympia of Constantinople was a noble widow and deaconess known for her deep commitment to prayer, penance, and charity. After the death of her husband, she dedicated her life to serving the poor and supporting the Church, notably assisting Saint John Chrysostom in his charitable works. Despite facing persecution and exile, she remained steadfast in her faith and good deeds until her death in 410.","/images/saint_olympia_of_constantinople.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olympias_the_Deaconess","12-17"],"Omer":["7th century",null,"Devoted bishop and missionary leader","Saint Omer was a 7th-century bishop known for his dedication to converting pagans in the region of Therouanne, France. He entered monastic life with his father and later became a prominent church leader, revitalizing the Christian community in his diocese. Despite becoming blind in his later years, he continued to serve his flock until his death in 670.","/images/saint_omer.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Omer_(saint)","09-09"],"Onesimus":["1st century",["slaves","prisoners","bishops"],"From slave to bishop and martyr","Onesimus was a Phrygian slave who fled from his master, Philemon, and sought refuge with Saint Paul in Rome. After being baptized by Paul, he returned to Philemon, who granted him freedom and allowed him to serve as a bishop. Onesimus later became a martyr for his faith, enduring severe torture before his death around the year 95 under Emperor Domitian.","/images/saint_onesimus.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Onesimus","02-16"],"Oswald":["10th century",["England","archbishops","healers"],"Reviver of monastic life in England","Oswald was an influential Archbishop of York in the 10th century, known for his commitment to reforming the clergy and promoting monastic life. He founded seven religious houses and worked tirelessly to restore discipline among the secular canons. His legacy includes a significant monastic revival in England and a reputation for piety, culminating in his death while serving the poor.","/images/saint_oswald.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Oswald_of_Worcester","02-29"],"Our Lady of Fatima":["20th century",["peace","families","travelers"],"A call to prayer and conversion","Our Lady of Fatima refers to the apparitions of the Blessed Virgin Mary that occurred in 1917 in Fatima, Portugal. She appeared to three shepherd children, delivering messages of prayer, penance, and a warning about the spread of Communism. The final apparition on October 13, 1917, included the Miracle of the Sun, witnessed by thousands, affirming her messages and the importance of faith.","/images/our_lady_of_fatima.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Our_Lady_of_F%C3%A1tima","05-13"],"Our Lady of Good Counsel":["15th century",["counselors","mothers","the mentally ill"],"A source of guidance and miracles","Our Lady of Good Counsel is a title of the Blessed Virgin Mary associated with a miraculous image that appeared in Gennazano, Italy, in 1467. The image, believed to have been transported from Albania, has been a source of numerous miracles and spiritual graces for the faithful. The devotion to Our Lady of Good Counsel has been recognized by the Church, and her feast day is celebrated with great reverence.","/images/our_lady_of_good_counsel.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Our_Lady_of_Good_Counsel","04-26"],"Our Lady of Guadalupe":["16th century",["Latin America","the unborn","pregnant women","environment"],"Patroness of the Americas and the unborn","Our Lady of Guadalupe is a celebrated apparition of the Virgin Mary that occurred in 1531 in Mexico. She appeared to Juan Diego, a humble indigenous man, and requested the construction of a church in her honor. Her image, miraculously imprinted on Juan Diego's tilma, remains a symbol of faith and cultural identity for millions in Latin America.","/images/our_lady_of_guadalupe.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Our_Lady_of_Guadalupe","12-12"],"Our Lady of La Salette":["19th century",["reconciliation","peace"],"Call to repentance and faith renewal","Our Lady of La Salette is a title of the Blessed Virgin Mary associated with her apparition to two shepherds in France in 1846. During this apparition, she delivered a message of warning and hope, urging the faithful to return to God and live according to His teachings. The event has inspired devotion and the establishment of religious communities dedicated to her message.","/images/our_lady_of_la_salette.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Our_Lady_of_La_Salette","09-19"],"Our Lady of Loreto":["Medieval",["air travelers","builders","families","homeowners"],"Symbol of faith and pilgrimage","Our Lady of Loreto is a title of the Virgin Mary associated with the Holy House of Loreto, believed to be the home of Mary in Nazareth. According to tradition, the house was miraculously transported to Loreto, Italy, in the late 13th century. This site has become a major pilgrimage destination, symbolizing the Incarnation and the role of Mary in salvation history.","/images/translation_of_the_holy_house_of_loreto.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Our_Lady_of_Loreto","12-10"],"Our Lady of Lourdes":["Modern",["sick","hospital workers","bodily ills"],"A symbol of healing and hope","Our Lady of Lourdes refers to the apparitions of the Blessed Virgin Mary to Bernadette Soubirous in 1858 in Lourdes, France. During these apparitions, Mary identified herself as the Immaculate Conception, affirming the dogma proclaimed by Pope Pius IX in 1854. The site has since become a major pilgrimage destination, known for miraculous healings and a deep devotion to Mary as the Mother of God.","/images/our_lady_of_lourdes.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Our_Lady_of_Lourdes","02-11"],"Our Lady of Mount Carmel":["Medieval",["Carmelites","those seeking protection","seafarers"],"Patroness of the Carmelite Order","Our Lady of Mount Carmel is a title given to the Blessed Virgin Mary in her role as the patroness of the Carmelite Order. According to tradition, she appeared to Saint Simon Stock in 1251, presenting him with the brown scapular as a sign of her protection. This devotion has since spread widely, symbolizing Mary's intercession and care for her followers.","/images/our_lady_of_mount_carmel.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Our_Lady_of_Mount_Carmel","07-16"],"Our Lady of Perpetual Help":["Medieval",["mothers","the sick","travelers"],"A symbol of hope and intercession","Our Lady of Perpetual Help is a revered icon of the Virgin Mary and the Child Jesus, known for its Byzantine style. The image has been a source of devotion and miracles since its arrival in Rome from Crete in the 15th century. It is celebrated for its role in providing comfort and assistance to the faithful in times of need.","/images/our_lady_of_perpetual_help.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Our_Lady_of_Perpetual_Help","06-27"],"Our Lady of Ransom":[null,["captives","prisoners","those in need of redemption"],"Champion of captives and mercy","Peter Nolasco was a Spanish saint and the founder of the Order of Our Lady of Ransom, established to redeem Christian captives from Muslim captivity. He dedicated his life to charitable works and took a vow of chastity, committing his wealth to the Church. His efforts were recognized by King James I of Aragon, and he is remembered for his deep faith and commitment to the liberation of the oppressed.","/images/our_lady_of_ransom_or_of_mercy.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Feast_of_Our_Lady_of_Ransom","09-24"],"Our Lady of the Rosary":["Modern",["prayers","victory in battle","the Catholic Church"],"Promoter of the Rosary devotion","Our Lady of the Rosary is a title of the Blessed Virgin Mary associated with the prayer of the Rosary. This devotion emphasizes the importance of meditation on the mysteries of Christ's life through the recitation of prayers. The feast of Our Lady of the Rosary was established to commemorate the victory at the Battle of Lepanto and to promote the Rosary as a powerful tool for prayer and reflection.","/images/our_lady_of_the_rosary.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Our_Lady_of_the_Rosary","10-07"],"Our Lady, Help of Christians":["Modern",["Catholic Church","Christianity"],"Patroness of the Catholic Church's struggles","Our Lady, Help of Christians is a title of the Blessed Virgin Mary, recognized for her intercession and assistance to the Church. Instituted by Pope Pius VII in 1815, this feast commemorates her role in supporting the papacy during times of adversity, particularly during Napoleon's reign. The devotion was notably promoted by Saint John Bosco, who dedicated his Salesian Order to her and constructed a church in her honor in Turin.","/images/our_lady_help_of_christians.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Our_Lady_Help_of_Christians","05-24"],"Pachomius":["4th century",["monks","Christian monasticism"],"Founder of Christian monastic community life","Saint Pachomius was a Christian monk and the founder of cenobitic monasticism, which emphasizes communal living among monks. After converting from paganism, he established a monastery in Tabenna, Egypt, where he created a rule of life that balanced spiritual discipline with accessibility for new monks. His teachings and practices laid the foundation for many future monastic communities in Christianity.","/images/saint_pachomius.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pachomius_the_Great","05-14"],"Palladius":["5th century",["Scotland","missionaries"],"First bishop and apostle of the Scots","Saint Palladius was the first bishop and apostle of the Scots, sent by Pope Celestine in 429 to preach Christianity in North Britain. He is recognized for his efforts to combat heresy and establish the Catholic faith among the Scots and in parts of Ireland. Palladius is often regarded as a precursor to Saint Patrick in the evangelization of Ireland.","/images/saint_palladius.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palladius_(bishop_of_Ireland)","07-06"],"Pamphilus":["Early 4th century",null,"Scholarship and martyrdom in faith","Saint Pamphilus was a scholar and martyr from Berytus, known for his dedication to the study of Holy Scripture and the establishment of a school of sacred literature in Caesarea. He collected a vast library for the church and was renowned for his humility and austere lifestyle. Pamphilus was arrested during the persecution of Christians under Maximius Daia and ultimately martyred for his faith, leaving a legacy of steadfastness and devotion.","/images/saint_pamphilus.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pamphilus_of_Caesarea","06-01"],"Pantaleon":["Early 4th century",["physicians","healers"],"Compassionate healer and martyr","Saint Pantaleon was a physician and martyr from Nicomedia, known for his miraculous healing abilities. After converting to Christianity, he dedicated his life to serving the poor and healing the sick, ultimately facing persecution for his faith. He was martyred during the reign of Emperor Galerius Maximianus, and his relics are venerated in various locations, including Constantinople and France.","/images/saint_pantaleon_of_nicomedia.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pantaleon_of_Nicomedia","07-27"],"Pantænus":["2nd century",["teachers","missionaries"],"Pioneer of Christian education and missions","Pantænus was a prominent Christian teacher and theologian in the second century, known for his role as the head of the Christian school in Alexandria. He traveled to India to spread the Gospel, where he discovered early Christian teachings and the Hebrew Gospel of Matthew. His dedication to education and evangelization significantly influenced the spread of Christianity in the eastern regions.","/images/saint_pantaenus.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pantaenus","07-07"],"Paphnutius":["4th century",null,"Defender of the Catholic Faith","Saint Paphnutius was an Egyptian bishop and confessor who lived during the second half of the Fourth Century. He was known for his staunch defense of the Catholic Faith against the Arian heresy and was a prominent figure at the Council of Nicea in 325. After enduring persecution and losing an eye for his beliefs, he returned to his diocese to lead his flock with great sanctity.","/images/saint_paphnutius.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Paphnutius_the_Bishop","09-11"],"Paschal Baylon":["16th century",["Eucharistic congresses","confraternities"],"Devotion to the Eucharist and humility","Saint Paschal Baylon was a Franciscan lay brother known for his deep devotion to the Blessed Sacrament. He dedicated his life to serving God and evangelizing others, despite being unlettered and humble in his tasks. Canonized in 1690, he is recognized as the patron of Eucharistic congresses and confraternities.","/images/saint_paschal_baylon.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paschal_Bayl%C3%B3n","05-17"],"Paternus":["6th century",null,"Evangelizer and founder of monasteries","Saint Paternus was a bishop of Avranches and a prominent figure in the early Christian evangelization of France. Born in Poitiers around 482, he embraced monastic life and later became known for his austere anchoritic lifestyle and missionary work alongside Saint Scubilion. He was consecrated bishop in his later years and is remembered for his miracles and the establishment of several monasteries.","/images/saint_paternus.jpg",null,"04-15"],"Patrick":["5th century",["Ireland","engineers","parish clergy","snakes"],"Patron saint of Ireland and missionaries","Saint Patrick is known as the Apostle of Ireland, credited with converting the Irish to Christianity in the 5th century. Born in Scotland, he was kidnapped and taken to Ireland as a slave, where he later returned to spread the Gospel after escaping captivity. His missionary work led to the establishment of churches, schools, and monasteries throughout Ireland, making him a central figure in Irish Christianity.","/images/saint_patrick.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Patrick_of_Ireland","03-17"],"Paul of the Cross":["18th century",["spiritual desolation","the Passionists"],"Founder of the Passionist Order","Paul of the Cross was the founder of the Passionist Order, dedicated to the meditation on the Passion of Jesus Christ. Throughout his life, he endured significant physical and spiritual suffering, which he embraced as a means of deepening his faith and commitment to God. He is remembered for his profound spirituality and the establishment of a religious community focused on the Passion of Christ.","/images/saint_paul_of_the_cross.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paul_of_the_Cross","04-28"],"Paul the Hermit":["3rd century",["hermits","desert dwellers"],"The first hermit and model of solitude","Saint Paul the Hermit is recognized as the first hermit and is known for his life of solitude in the Egyptian desert. After fleeing persecution, he lived for nearly ninety years in prayer and penance, relying solely on God's providence for sustenance. His legacy is closely tied to Saint Anthony, who discovered him and shared in his spiritual life before Paul's death.","/images/saint_paul_hermit.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paul_of_Thebes","01-15"],"Paulinus of Nola":["Early Christian",["poets","theologians","Nola"],"A model of humility and generosity","Paulinus of Nola was a Roman senator who became a bishop and a prominent figure in the early Church. After experiencing personal tragedy, he renounced his wealth and status to live a life of poverty and service, dedicating himself to the care of his community. He is remembered for his deep humility, generosity, and commitment to the Christian faith, particularly during times of crisis.","/images/saint_paulinus_of_nola.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paulinus_of_Nola","06-22"],"Perpetuus":["5th century",null,"A devoted bishop and advocate for the poor.","Saint Perpetuus was the eighth Bishop of Tours, serving from 461 to 494. He was known for his dedication to leading souls to virtue through sermons, synods, and the enhancement of church facilities, particularly the church of Saint Martin. His legacy includes a testament of love for the poor and the establishment of a fund for sacred vessels, reflecting his deep commitment to charity and community.","/images/saint_perpetuus.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Perpetuus_of_Tours","04-08"],"Peter":["1st century",["fishermen","net makers","shipbuilders","popes"],"First Pope and leader of the apostles","Saint Peter, one of Jesus' twelve apostles, is recognized as the first Pope and a foundational figure in Christianity. He is known for his leadership in the early Church and his martyrdom in Rome under Emperor Nero. His legacy includes the establishment of the Church in Rome, which remains a central point of Catholic faith and communion.","/images/saint_peters_chair_at_rome.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter_the_Apostle","01-18,02-22,06-29,08-01,11-18"],"Peter Canisius":["16th century",["Germany","catechists","students"],"Champion of the Catholic Counter-Reformation","Peter Canisius was a Dutch Jesuit priest and theologian who played a crucial role in the Catholic Counter-Reformation. He was instrumental in the establishment of Jesuit colleges and the development of catechetical materials, notably his famous catechism. Canonized in 1621 and declared a Doctor of the Church in 1927, he is remembered for his dedication to the renewal of the Catholic faith in Germany and beyond.","/images/saint_peter_canisius.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter_Canisius","04-27"],"Peter Celestine":["13th century",["celibacy","papal elections"],"Pope and founder of the Celestines","Peter Celestine was a 13th-century Pope who founded the Celestine Order, a branch of the Benedictines. Known for his deep spirituality and desire for solitude, he resigned the papacy after just five months, feeling unfit for the role. He spent the remainder of his life in seclusion, performing miracles and fostering a close relationship with God and the angels.","/images/saint_peter_celestine.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter_Celestine","05-19"],"Peter Chanel":["19th century",["Oceania","missionaries","mariners"],"First martyr of Oceania","Peter Chanel was a French missionary and martyr who dedicated his life to spreading Christianity in Oceania. He faced persecution for his faith while working to convert the people of Futuna, where he was ultimately killed. Canonized in 1954, he is recognized as the first martyr of Oceania and is celebrated for his unwavering commitment to his mission.","/images/saint_peter_chanel.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter_Chanel","04-23"],"Peter Claver":["17th century",["Colombia","slaves","African missions"],"A champion of enslaved Africans' dignity","Peter Claver was a Spanish Jesuit missionary known for his dedicated service to enslaved Africans in Colombia during the 17th century. Over his 44 years of ministry, he baptized approximately 400,000 individuals and tirelessly worked to improve their spiritual and physical conditions. His life exemplified profound charity and commitment to the marginalized, earning him recognition as the patron saint of Colombia and enslaved people.","/images/saint_peter_claver.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter_Claver","09-09"],"Peter Damian":["11th century",["cardinals","students","theologians"],"Reformer and defender of Church integrity","Peter Damian was a prominent 11th-century cardinal and bishop known for his reformative efforts within the Church. He dedicated his life to combating simony and ecclesiastical corruption, serving as an advisor to seven popes. His commitment to prayer and asceticism, along with his leadership, left a lasting impact on the Church's moral and spiritual direction.","/images/saint_peter_damian.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter_Damian","02-23"],"Peter Favre":["16th century",["students","teachers","Jesuits"],"Pioneer of Jesuit education and spirituality.","Peter Favre was a pioneering Jesuit priest and one of the first companions of Saint Ignatius Loyola. He played a significant role in the early Jesuit mission, particularly in Germany, where he worked to strengthen the Catholic faith amidst the challenges of the Reformation. Beatified by Pope Pius IX in 1872, he is remembered for his dedication to education and spiritual guidance.","/images/blessed_peter_favre_of_villaret.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter_Favre","08-08"],"Peter Fourier":["17th century",["educators","schoolchildren","women's education"],"Pioneer of women's education in France.","Peter Fourier was a French priest and reformer known for his dedication to education and the spiritual formation of young women. He founded the Canonesses of Notre Dame, an order focused on teaching and nurturing virtue among girls. His efforts revitalized the religious fervor in his parish and established a model for Catholic education that influenced future generations.","/images/saint_peter_fourier.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter_Fourier","12-09"],"Peter Gonzales":["13th century",null,"Dominican Priest","Saint Peter Gonzales was born in Spain in 1190, of parents both rich and noble. He was brought up by his maternal uncle, a bishop in the region of Astorga, and while still young was named a canon of his cathedral. Soon he was chosen to be head of the cathedral chapter; but when he came to take possession of that office, mounted on a finely arrayed horse, the animal by a false step threw him into the mud.","/images/saint_peter_gonzales.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter_Gonz%C3%A1lez","04-15"],"Peter Julian Eymard":["19th century",["Eucharistic adoration","clergy","the Blessed Sacrament"],"Champion of Eucharistic devotion and spirituality.","Peter Julian Eymard was a French priest and the founder of the Congregation of the Blessed Sacrament. He dedicated his life to promoting devotion to the Eucharist and established communities to support this mission. Canonized in 1962, he is remembered for his deep spirituality and commitment to the sacramental life of the Church.","/images/saint_peter-julian_eymard.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter_Julian_Eymard","08-03"],"Peter Nolasco":["13th century",["captives","ransom","prisoners"],"Champion of Christian captives and freedom","Peter Nolasco was a 13th-century founder of the Order of Our Lady of Ransom, dedicated to the redemption of Christian captives held by the Moors. He devoted his life and fortune to rescuing enslaved Christians, often negotiating with their captors and even offering himself as a ransom. Canonized in 1628, his legacy continues through the ongoing work of his Order in preaching and humanitarian efforts.","/images/saint_peter_nolasco.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter_Nolasco","01-28"],"Peter of Alcantara":["16th century",["spiritual directors","penitents"],"A model of penance and reform","Peter of Alcantara was a Franciscan priest and reformer born in 1499 in Spain. He founded the Congregation of Saint Joseph of the Strict Observance to uphold the Rule of Saint Francis and was known for his extreme penance and dedication to prayer. His legacy includes guiding notable figures like Saint Teresa of Avila and promoting the Franciscan reform movement during the tumultuous 16th century.","/images/saint_peter_of_alcantara.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter_of_Alc%C3%A1ntara","10-19"],"Peter of Alexandria":["Early 4th century",null,"Defender of faith during persecution","Peter of Alexandria was a Patriarch and martyr who led the Church of Alexandria during the severe persecutions under Emperor Diocletian. He is known for his firm stance against heresy and his pastoral care for imprisoned Christians, encouraging them to remain steadfast in their faith. Ultimately, he was martyred by beheading on November 26, 310, after a life dedicated to the service of God and the Church.","/images/saint_peter_of_alexandria.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter_I_of_Alexandria","11-26"],"Peter of Luxembourg":["Medieval",["children","lost causes","diocesan priests"],"A model of youthful holiness and charity.","Peter of Luxembourg was a bishop and cardinal known for his deep piety and commitment to serving the Church. Appointed bishop of Metz at the young age of fifteen, he governed with remarkable zeal and charity, often giving away his own wealth. He died at just eighteen years old, leaving a legacy of holiness and miraculous intercession.","/images/blessed_peter_of_luxemburg.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter_of_Luxembourg","07-05"],"Peter of Verona":["Medieval",["martyrs","preachers","heretics"],"Courageous martyr and defender of faith","Peter of Verona was a Dominican priest and martyr born in 1206. He dedicated his life to preaching against heresy and converting many to the Catholic faith, often facing severe opposition. He was ultimately martyred in 1252, leaving a legacy of courage and unwavering faith.","/images/saint_peter_of_verona.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter_of_Verona","04-29"],"Peter, Andrew, Paul and Dionysia":["3rd century",null,"Martyrs","In the Decian persecution of the year 250 the blood of the Christians flowed at Lampsacus, a city of Asia Minor. A young Christian named Peter was the first to be led before the proconsul; he was told to sacrifice to the goddess Venus. His energetic condemnation of impurity and his defense of his God, the king of all ages, caused him to be condemned to die for the name of Christ, and he went joyfully to his torments.","/images/saints_peter_andrew_paul_dionysia.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter,_Andrew,_Paul,_and_Denise","05-15"],"Petronilla":["1st century",["virgins","sick children"],"A symbol of virginity and holiness","Saint Petronilla is traditionally regarded as the daughter of Saint Peter, one of the Apostles. She is celebrated for her vow of virginity and her exemplary holiness during the early Christian era. Her legacy is honored in the Basilica of Saint Peter, where her relics are kept.","/images/saint_petronilla.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Petronilla","05-31"],"Philip":["1st century",["hatmakers","lovers","deaconry"],"Apostle known for his evangelistic zeal","Philip was one of the first disciples chosen by Jesus Christ, known for his enthusiastic invitation to Nathaniel to meet the Messiah. He is remembered for his deep thirst for God, famously asking Jesus to show him the Father. Tradition holds that Philip was martyred in Hierapolis, and his relics were later moved to Rome.","/images/saint_philip.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philip_the_Apostle","05-11"],"Philip Benizi":["Medieval",["sick people","medical professionals","Servites"],"A model of humility and service","Philip Benizi was a Servite priest born in Florence in 1233. He dedicated his life to serving God and helping others, known for his humility and dedication to peace during a time of civil strife in Italy. His legacy includes his commitment to prayer and penance, as well as his role in the founding and leadership of the Servite Order.","/images/saint_philip_benizi.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philip_Benizi_de_Damiani","08-23"],"Philip Neri":["16th century",["youth","joy","clergy"],"Apostle of Rome and patron of youth","Philip Neri was a 16th-century priest known for his joyful spirit and dedication to the youth of Rome. He founded the Oratory, a society of priests focused on spiritual education and pastoral care. Renowned for his piety and instances of bilocation, he became a beloved figure in the Catholic Church, emphasizing the importance of serving God with gladness.","/images/saint_philip_neri.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philip_Neri","05-26"],"Philip of Jesus":["16th century",["Mexico","Franciscans","martyrs"],"First martyr of Japan's Christian mission","Philip of Jesus was a Franciscan friar and martyr who was born in Mexico and later traveled to Japan as a missionary. He was arrested and executed for his faith during a period of intense persecution against Christians in Japan. His legacy is honored as one of the first martyrs of Japan, and he is remembered for his unwavering commitment to his faith even unto death.","/images/saint_philip_of_jesus.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philip_of_Jesus","02-05"],"Philogonius":["3rd century",null,"Defender of the faith and integrity","Saint Philogonius was a Bishop of Antioch in the early 4th century, known for his eloquence and integrity. He played a significant role in defending the Catholic faith during the Council of Nicaea against the Arian heresy. His steadfastness in the face of persecution earned him the title of Confessor, and he is remembered for his deep commitment to the Church and its teachings.","/images/saint_philogonius.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philogonius_of_Antioch","12-20"],"Philomena":["3rd century",["children","lost causes","virgins"],"Protector of children and miracle worker","Saint Philomena is a virgin and martyr who is believed to have died in the third century under the Roman Emperor Diocletian. Her tomb was discovered in the early 19th century, leading to a surge in devotion and reports of miracles attributed to her intercession. She is particularly venerated as a protector of children and is known for her miraculous works, earning her the title of the wonder-worker of the 19th century.","/images/saint_philomena.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philomena","08-11"],"Pius I":["2nd century",null,"Defender of faith against heresy","Pius I was the ninth pope of the Catholic Church, serving from 142 to 157 AD. He is known for his efforts to combat heresy and for establishing the celebration of Easter on a Sunday. Pius I was martyred for his faith, becoming a symbol of steadfastness in the early Church.","/images/saint_pius_i.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pius_I","07-11"],"Pius V":["16th century",["papal states","liturgical reform","the Council of Trent"],"Champion of Church reform and liturgy","Pius V was a Pope known for his strong commitment to reforming the Catholic Church and enforcing the decrees of the Council of Trent. He is recognized for his role in the victory at the Battle of Lepanto, which was attributed to his prayers and leadership. His papacy was marked by a dedication to ecclesiastical discipline and the promotion of the Roman Missal.","/images/saint_pius_v.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pius_V","05-05"],"Pius X":["20th century",["clergy","sick people","sick children"],"Pope of Frequent Communion and Eucharistic devotion.","Pius X was a Pope known for his strong advocacy for frequent Communion and his efforts to combat modernism within the Church. Born Joseph Sarto in 1836, he rose through the ranks of the clergy, becoming a beloved bishop and later the Patriarch of Venice before his papacy. He was canonized in 1954, remembered for his piety, charity, and dedication to the Eucharist.","/images/saint_pius_x.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pius_X","09-03"],"Placid":["6th century",["monks","Sicily"],"First Benedictine martyr and founder","Saint Placid was a Benedictine monk born in Rome around 515. He founded a monastery in Messina, Sicily, where he was known for his humility and miraculous healings. He was martyred by Saracen pirates in 541, becoming the first Benedictine martyr.","/images/saint_placid.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Placidus_(abbot)","10-05"],"Polycarp":["2nd century",["martyrs","persecuted Christians"],"A model of faith and perseverance","Saint Polycarp was a 2nd-century bishop of Smyrna and a disciple of Saint John the Apostle. He is known for his steadfast faith and refusal to renounce Christ during persecution, ultimately leading to his martyrdom. His writings and teachings emphasized love for God and the Church, as well as a strong opposition to heresy.","/images/saint_polycarp.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polycarp_of_Smyrna","01-26"],"Porphyry":["Early 5th century",["Christian converts","the city of Gaza"],"Champion of Christian conversion in Gaza","Saint Porphyry was a bishop known for his zealous efforts to convert pagans in Gaza during the late 4th and early 5th centuries. After a life of asceticism and dedication to the Christian faith, he successfully dismantled pagan worship in his diocese and established a strong Christian community. His legacy includes the transformation of a prominent pagan temple into a church, symbolizing the triumph of Christianity over idolatry.","/images/saint_porphyry.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Porphyry_of_Gaza","02-26"],"Pothinus":["2nd century",null,"Martyrdom and faith in early Christianity","Saint Pothinus was the Bishop of Lyons during the early Christian persecutions in the Roman Empire. He was martyred around 177 AD, enduring severe torture due to his faith and age. His steadfastness and zeal for Christ inspired many, including his successor, Saint Irenaeus.","/images/sts_pothinus_blandina_and_companions.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Pothinus","06-02"],"Praxedes":["2nd century",["virgins","the poor","prisoners"],"Compassionate servant of the persecuted faithful","Saint Praxedes was a Roman virgin and daughter of a senator, known for her deep compassion and dedication to the Christian faith during a time of persecution. She devoted her life to prayer, fasting, and aiding the poor, particularly those suffering for their beliefs. Praxedes is remembered for her acts of charity, including caring for imprisoned Christians and providing them with comfort and support.","/images/saint_praxedes.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Praxedes","07-21"],"Primus and Felicianus":["3rd century",null,"Martyrs of faith and charity","Saints Primus and Felicianus were brothers and martyrs who lived in Rome during the latter part of the 3rd century. They were converted to Christianity through the influence of Pope Felix I and dedicated their lives to serving the poor and encouraging fellow Christians during times of persecution. Ultimately, they were apprehended and executed for their faith, enduring severe tortures before being beheaded on June 9, 286.","/images/saints_primus_and_felicianus.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Primus_and_Felician","06-09"],"Prisca":["3rd century",null,"Martyrdom and faith in early Christianity","Saint Prisca was a noble Roman virgin and martyr who suffered for her faith around the year 275. She is remembered for her steadfastness in the face of persecution and is honored in the church that bears her name in Rome. Her relics are preserved there, and she is often associated with the early Christian figures Aquila and Priscilla.","/images/saint_prisca.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Prisca","01-18"],"Prosper of Aquitaine":["5th century",null,"Defender of Augustine's teachings and orthodoxy","Prosper of Aquitaine was a prominent theologian and Doctor of the Church in the 5th century, known for his writings and defense against the semi-Pelagian heresy. He collaborated closely with Saint Augustine, contributing to the theological debates of his time and helping to clarify Augustine's teachings. His legacy includes a significant role in the Church's efforts to combat heresy and promote sound doctrine.","/images/saint_prosper_of_aquitaine.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prosper_of_Aquitaine","06-25"],"Quentin":["3rd century",["brewers","plumbers","painters"],"Martyrdom and miracles in Gaul","Saint Quentin was a Roman martyr who evangelized in Gaul during the 3rd century. He was known for his miraculous healings and steadfast faith, which ultimately led to his brutal martyrdom under the Roman prosecutor Rictiovarus. His legacy endures in France, where many churches and places are dedicated to him, and he is venerated as a protector of the faithful.","/images/saint_quentin.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Quentin","10-31"],"Radegund":["6th century",["difficult marriages","widows","nursing","the poor"],"A life of devotion and service","Saint Radegund was a queen of France who dedicated her life to God after her marriage to King Clotaire. She founded the Monastery of the Holy Cross in Poitiers and lived a life of extreme austerity and devotion. Known for her care of the sick and the poor, she became a significant figure in early medieval Christianity.","/images/saint_radegundes.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Radegund","08-13"],"Raphael":["Biblical",["healers","travelers","the blind"],"Angel of healing and guidance","Raphael is one of the seven archangels who stand before God and is known for his role in the Book of Tobit, where he guides Tobias on a journey to retrieve his father's money and find a wife. He is associated with healing, as he helps cure Tobias's father's blindness and is believed to have agitated the waters of the pool of Bethsaida for healing. Raphael's name means 'God heals,' and he is revered for his assistance and guidance in times of need.","/images/saint_raphael_the_archangel.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raphael_the_Archangel","10-24"],"Raymond of Pennafort":["13th century",["lawyers","captives","ransom of captives"],"Patron of lawyers and captives","Raymond of Pennafort was a Spanish Dominican priest and a prominent figure in the 13th century. He is best known for founding the Order of Our Lady of Ransom, dedicated to the redemption of Christian captives from Muslim hands. His legacy includes significant contributions to canon law and a life marked by miracles and deep devotion to the Virgin Mary.","/images/saint_raymond_of_pennafort.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raymond_of_Pe%C3%B1afort","01-23"],"Raymund Nonnatus":["Medieval",["captives","childbirth","midwives"],"Champion of captives and the oppressed","Raymund Nonnatus was a Spanish saint born in 1204 who dedicated his life to the redemption of captives. He joined the Order of Our Lady of Mercy and risked his life to free slaves, ultimately becoming a hostage himself. After enduring severe torture for his faith, he died in 1240 and was later canonized, recognized for his selfless sacrifice and devotion to the salvation of souls.","/images/saint_raymund_nonnatus.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raymond_Nonnatus","08-31"],"Remigius":["5th century",["France","baptism","merchants"],"Apostle of the Franks and France","Saint Remigius was a 5th-century bishop who played a crucial role in the conversion of the Frankish king Clovis to Christianity. He is known for baptizing Clovis and leading the Christianization of France, which earned the country the title of 'eldest daughter of the Church.' His long episcopate and miraculous deeds solidified his legacy as a significant figure in the history of the Catholic Church in France.","/images/saint_remi.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Remigius","10-01"],"Richard of Chichester":["Medieval",["bishopric","poor","students"],"Champion of the poor and Church rights","Richard of Chichester was a 13th-century bishop known for his dedication to the Church and his commitment to social justice. He faced significant opposition from the king but remained steadfast in his duties, advocating for the rights of the Church and the welfare of the poor. His legacy includes a reputation for holiness and miraculous works, making him a beloved figure in English Christianity.","/images/saint_richard_of_chichester.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richard_of_Chichester","04-03"],"Rita of Cascia":["15th century",["impossible causes","married women","widows"],"Patroness of impossible causes and reconciliation.","Saint Rita of Cascia was a 15th-century Italian widow known for her deep spirituality and miraculous life. After enduring a difficult marriage and the loss of her children, she sought to dedicate her life to God by joining a convent. Renowned for her piety and the gift of miracles, she is often invoked for difficult causes and is a beloved figure in Catholic devotion.","/images/saint_rita_of_cascia.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rita_of_Cascia","05-22"],"Robert Bellarmine":["16th-17th century",["theologians","catechists","apologetics"],"Defender of Catholic doctrine and faith","Saint Robert Bellarmine was a prominent Jesuit theologian and cardinal known for his defense of Catholic doctrine during the Counter-Reformation. He authored significant works, including his Course of Controversy and a Commentary on the Psalms, which contributed to the Church's intellectual tradition. Canonized in 1930 and declared a Doctor of the Church, he is remembered for his deep commitment to faith and scholarship.","/images/saint_robert_bellarmine.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_Bellarmine","05-13"],"Robert of Arbrissel":["11th century",["hermits","monks","reformers"],"Champion of poverty and reform in the Church","Robert of Arbrissel was a prominent 11th-century abbot known for his reforming zeal and dedication to the ideals of poverty and charity. He founded the famous monastery of Fontevrault, which became a center for both men and women living in separate communities dedicated to the Gospel. His legacy includes a commitment to healing feuds and promoting spiritual growth among his followers throughout western France.","/images/blessed_robert_of_arbrissel.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_of_Arbrissel","02-24"],"Robert of Newminster":["12th century",null,"Founder of Fountains Abbey and monastic reformer","Robert of Newminster was an English abbot and a key figure in the reform of the Benedictine order during the 12th century. He founded the monastery of Fountains Abbey and later became the abbot of Newminster, guiding his community towards holiness and strict observance of monastic life. His legacy is marked by his deep commitment to asceticism and the spiritual growth of his brethren, which inspired the establishment of several new monastic communities.","/images/saint_robert_of_newminster.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_of_Newminster","06-07"],"Roch":["Medieval",["invalids","plague victims","dogs"],"Patron of the sick and travelers","Saint Roch was born to a governor in Montpellier and dedicated his life to serving the poor and sick. He is known for his miraculous healings during a plague in Italy, where he cared for the afflicted. After suffering from the plague himself, he died in prison, recognized by the red cross on his breast, and became a popular figure in Christian devotion.","/images/saint_roch.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roch","08-16"],"Romanus":["5th century",null,"Founder of monastic communities in Jura","Saint Romanus was a 5th-century monk and abbot who, along with his brother Lupicinus, founded several monasteries in the region of Jura. He was known for his gentle leadership and patience, often welcoming back monks who had strayed from their community. Romanus dedicated his life to prayer, study, and the cultivation of a monastic life, leaving a lasting legacy of faith and community.","/images/saints_romanus_and_lupicinus.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Romanus_of_Condat","02-28,08-09"],"Romuald":["10th century",["hermits","monks","spirituality"],"Founder of the Camaldolese order","Saint Romuald was a Benedictine monk and the founder of the Camaldolese order in the 10th century. He dedicated his life to restoring the primitive rule of Saint Benedict and established numerous monasteries across Italy and France. Known for his deep spirituality and commitment to solitude, he overcame many temptations and adversities, ultimately leading a life of profound holiness until his death in 1027.","/images/saint_romuald.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Romuald","02-07"],"Rosalia":["12th century",["Sicily","plague victims"],"Patroness of Palermo and Sicily","Saint Rosalia was a 12th-century virgin and hermit from Palermo, Sicily. She is known for her deep devotion to God, having chosen a life of solitude and penance over worldly pursuits. Her intercession is credited with saving the city of Palermo from a plague in the 17th century, leading to her veneration as a patron saint of the city.","/images/saint_rosalia.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rosalia_of_Palermo","09-06"],"Rose of Lima":["17th century",["Peru","gardening","the indigenous peoples of Latin America"],"First saint of the Americas","Rose of Lima was the first canonized saint of the Americas, born in Lima, Peru, in 1586. Known for her extreme penance and devotion to God, she dedicated her life to prayer and service, often suffering for the conversion of sinners. She died at a young age in 1617, leaving a legacy of humility and deep faith.","/images/saint_rose_of_lima.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rose_of_Lima","08-30"],"Rose of Viterbo":["13th century",["illness","people ridiculed for their faith"],"A young saint known for her fervent faith.","Saint Rose of Viterbo was a young Italian virgin and mystic who dedicated her life to God from a very early age. Known for her fervent devotion and austere lifestyle, she became a prominent figure in defending the Church during a time of political turmoil. Despite her short life, she left a lasting impact through her preaching and miracles, ultimately being recognized as a saint shortly after her death at the age of eighteen.","/images/saint_rose_of_viterbo.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rose_of_Viterbo","09-04"],"Sabas":["6th century",["monks","pilgrims"],"Patriarch of Palestinian monasticism","Saint Sabas was a prominent patriarchal abbot in Palestine, known for his dedication to monastic life and spiritual leadership. He founded several monasteries and played a significant role in the religious community, advocating for the Church during times of persecution. His legacy includes the establishment of a hospice for pilgrims and a commitment to preserving the true faith amidst heretical challenges.","/images/saint_sabas.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sabbas_the_Sanctified","12-05"],"Sabinus":["4th century",null,"Martyrdom and miracles in faith","Saint Sabinus was a Bishop of Spoleto and a martyr who lived during the early 4th century. He was imprisoned for refusing to worship idols and performed miracles, including healing a blind man, which led to the conversion of many. Ultimately, he was martyred for his faith on December 7, 303, and is remembered for his steadfastness in the face of persecution.","/images/saint_sabinus_and_his_companions.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sabinus_of_Spoleto","12-30"],"Saturninus":["1st century",["Toulouse","France","farmers"],"Martyr and early Christian evangelizer","Saint Saturninus was a bishop and martyr who played a crucial role in the early spread of Christianity in southern France. He was known for his miracles and his steadfastness in the face of persecution, ultimately leading to his martyrdom. His legacy is celebrated in the Christian tradition, particularly in Toulouse, where he is venerated as a patron saint.","/images/saint_saturninus.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saturninus_of_Toulouse","11-29"],"Scholastica":["6th century",["nuns","convulsive children","storms"],"Model of monastic devotion and sisterly love.","Saint Scholastica was an abbess and the sister of Saint Benedict, the founder of Western monasticism. She established a convent near Monte Cassino and devoted her life to prayer and the spiritual formation of her community. Known for her deep faith and connection to her brother, her life exemplifies the importance of spiritual companionship and devotion to God.","/images/saint_scholastica.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scholastica","02-10"],"Sebastian":["3rd century",["soldiers","athletes","plague victims"],"Champion of faith and martyrdom","Saint Sebastian was a Roman officer and martyr who is revered for his unwavering faith in Christ. He encouraged fellow Christians during a time of persecution and was known for performing miracles, including healing the sick. Ultimately, he was martyred twice, first by arrows and then by being beaten to death, solidifying his legacy as a champion of the faith.","/images/saint_sebastian.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Sebastian","01-20"],"Seraphia":["1st century",null,"Martyrdom for unwavering faith in Christ","Saint Seraphia was a virgin and martyr from Antioch who dedicated her life to God after the death of her parents. She became a servant to Saint Sabina, a Roman noblewoman, and was eventually martyred for her Christian faith. Despite being subjected to torture, her unwavering faith led to her beheading, and she was buried with great respect by her mistress.","/images/saint_seraphia_and_saint_sabina.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Serapia","09-03"],"Serenus":["4th century",null,"Martyrdom for faith and virtue","Saint Serenus was a Greek martyr who lived during the early 4th century. He dedicated his life to God through celibacy, penance, and prayer, cultivating a garden in Sirmium, Pannonia. After being falsely accused of impropriety, he boldly professed his Christian faith and was beheaded for his refusal to sacrifice to the gods.","/images/saint_serenus.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Serenus_the_Gardener","02-23"],"Servulus of Rome":["7th century",["the disabled","beggars","sick people"],"A model of faith in suffering","Saint Servulus of Rome was a paralyzed beggar who lived in poverty yet exemplified profound faith and patience. Despite his physical limitations, he devoted his life to prayer and meditation on the sufferings of Christ, becoming a source of inspiration for many. His life of humility and charity transformed his suffering into a powerful witness of God's grace.","/images/saint_servulus_of_rome.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Servulus_of_Rome","12-23"],"Seven Holy Servite Founders":["Medieval",["servants of Mary","spiritual retreats"],"Founders of the Servite Order","The Seven Holy Servite Founders were a group of men from Florence who dedicated their lives to God in the mid-13th century. They formed the Order of the Servants of Mary after receiving a vision from the Blessed Virgin Mary, encouraging them to live a life of prayer and penance. Their commitment led to the establishment of a religious order that grew significantly throughout Europe and beyond.","/images/the_seven_holy_servite_founders.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seven_Holy_Founders_of_the_Servite_Order","02-12"],"Severianus":["5th century",null,"Courageous defender of the faith","Saint Severianus was a bishop and martyr known for his steadfast resistance against the Eutychian heresy during the reign of Emperor Marcian. He courageously opposed the tyrannical monk Theodosius, who persecuted Christians in Jerusalem. Severianus ultimately paid the ultimate price for his faith, being martyred around the year 452 or 453.","/images/saint_severianus.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Severianus,_Bishop_of_Scythopolis","02-21"],"Severinus":["Early 6th century",["healers","the sick","monks"],"Miracle worker and holy abbot","Saint Severinus was an abbot from Burgundy who dedicated his life to God in the monastery of Agaunum. He became known for his miraculous healings, including curing King Clovis of France. After his death in 507, he was venerated as a saint, and his legacy continues through the Abbey of Saint Severinus.","/images/saint_severinus.jpg",null,"02-11"],"Silverius":["6th century",null,"Martyrdom for the integrity of faith","Silverius was the Pope and martyr who served during a tumultuous period in the Church's history. Elected Pope in 536, he faced opposition from the Empress Theodora and was ultimately deposed through treachery. He died in exile on the island of Palmeria, enduring great suffering for his steadfastness to the Catholic faith.","/images/saint_silverius.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silverius","06-20"],"Simeon":["1st century",["martyrs","Jerusalem"],"Martyr and leader of the Jerusalem Church","Saint Simeon was the second bishop of Jerusalem and a martyr who led the Christian community during a tumultuous period. He was a relative of Jesus Christ, being the son of Cleophas and the cousin of the Savior. After enduring persecution and ultimately being crucified around the year 107, he is remembered for his steadfast faith and leadership.","/images/saint_simeon.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Simeon_of_Jerusalem","02-18"],"Simeon Stylites":["5th century",["ascetics","hermits","those seeking solitude"],"Pioneer of pillar asceticism and prayer","Simeon Stylites was a Christian ascetic known for his extreme form of monasticism, spending 37 years atop a pillar in Syria. He practiced severe austerities, including fasting and self-discipline, which drew many followers and admirers. His life of prayer and dedication to God inspired countless individuals and led to numerous miracles attributed to his intercession.","/images/saint_simeon_stylites.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Simeon_Stylites","01-05"],"Simon":["15th century",null,"Infant Martyr","Hail, flowers of the martyrs! the Church sings in her Office of the Holy Innocents, who were the first to die for Christ; and in every age mere children and infants have gloriously confessed His name. The Jews of the city of Trent, assuredly possessed at that moment by the demons of hell, determined in 1472 to vent their hatred for the Crucified by slaying a Christian child at the coming Passover.","/images/saint_simon_infant_martyr.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Simon_of_Trent","03-24,10-28"],"Simon Stock":["Medieval",["Carmelites","breast cancer patients","those seeking a good death"],"Promoter of the Carmelite Order's growth","Simon Stock was an English Carmelite friar and the Superior General of the Carmelite Order in the 13th century. He is best known for his deep devotion to the Virgin Mary and for receiving the Brown Scapular, a significant symbol of the Carmelite Order. His leadership helped the Order flourish in Europe, particularly during times of persecution and conflict.","/images/saint_simon_stock.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Simon_Stock","05-16"],"Simplicius":["5th century",null,"Guided the Church through turbulent times","Simplicius served as Pope from 468 to 483 during a tumultuous period marked by the fall of the Western Roman Empire. He worked tirelessly to comfort the afflicted and spread the Catholic faith among the barbarian tribes. His leadership helped guide the Church through significant challenges, including heretical movements and political instability.","/images/saint_simplicius.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pope_Simplicius","03-02"],"Soter and Caius":["3rd century",null,"Popes, Martyrs","Saint Soter was raised to the papacy upon the death of Saint Anicetus in 161. By the sweetness of his discourses he comforted all afflicted persons with the tenderness of a father, and assisted the indigent with liberal alms, especially those who suffered for the Faith. He liberally extended his charities, according to the custom of his predecessors, to remote churches.","/images/saint_soter_and_saint_caius.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pope_Soter","04-22"],"Stanislav":["11th century",["Poland","bishops","martyrs"],"Defender of the faith and the poor","Saint Stanislaus was a Bishop of Cracow and a martyr who courageously opposed King Boleslaus II for his immoral actions. He is known for his deep piety, commitment to the poor, and miraculous acts, including raising the dead to testify on his behalf. His steadfastness in faith ultimately led to his martyrdom at the altar during Mass in 1079.","/images/saint_stanislaus.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stanislaus_of_Szczepan%C3%B3w","05-07"],"Stanislaw Kostka":["16th century",["youth","students","Jesuits"],"Patron of youth and students","Saint Stanislaus Kostka was a Polish Jesuit known for his deep spirituality and commitment to God from a young age. He entered the Society of Jesus at the age of 17 and is remembered for his piety, devotion to the Virgin Mary, and miraculous experiences during his illness. He died at the young age of 18, and his incorrupt body has become a site of veneration, leading to his canonization in 1726.","/images/saint_stanislaus_kostka.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stanislaus_Kostka","11-13"],"Stephanie Quinzani":["15th century",null,"A life of suffering and service","Blessed Stephanie Quinzani was born in 1457 in Italy and dedicated her life to God from a young age. She became a member of the Third Order of Saint Dominic, enduring great physical and spiritual suffering throughout her life. Known for her mystical experiences and works of mercy, she founded a monastery in Soncino and was beatified in 1740 due to the miracles attributed to her intercession.","/images/blessed_stephanie_quinzani.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stephanie_Quinzani","01-16"],"Stephen":["3rd century",["popes","deacons","stonemasons"],"Defender of the Faith and martyr","Stephen was a Roman by birth and became the Pope and martyr in the 3rd century. He is known for his defense of the validity of baptism and his efforts to maintain the integrity of the Faith during a time of heretical disputes. Ultimately, he was martyred on August 2, 257, while steadfast in his commitment to Christ and the Church.","/images/saint_stephen_I_pope.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stephen_of_Armenia","08-02,09-02,12-26"],"Susanna":["3rd century",null,"Courageous martyr of early Christianity","Saint Susanna was a noble Roman virgin and martyr, known for her strong commitment to her vow of chastity. She refused to marry the emperor Diocletian's favorite, Maximian, and was subsequently arrested and executed for her faith. Her legacy is honored in the Christian tradition, particularly for her courage and dedication to God amidst persecution.","/images/saint_susanna.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Susanna","08-11"],"Sylvester":["Early Christian",["churches","baptism"],"Pioneer of church consecration and unity","Pope Sylvester I served as the Bishop of Rome from 314 to 335 AD, during a pivotal time in the early Church. He is best known for his role in the establishment of the Lateran Basilica, the first church built in Rome, which became a significant center of Christian worship. His papacy coincided with the Edict of Milan, which granted religious freedom to Christians in the Roman Empire.","/images/dedication_of_the_lateran_basilica.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sylvester_I","11-09,12-31"],"Symphorian":["2nd century",null,"A symbol of steadfast Christian faith","Saint Symphorian was a young Christian martyr from Autun, France, who refused to worship pagan idols during a public procession in honor of the goddess Ceres around the year 180. He boldly declared his faith before the magistrate and faced severe torture, ultimately being executed by the sword. His mother, witnessing his martyrdom, encouraged him to remain faithful to God, and both are remembered for their unwavering commitment to Christ.","/images/saint_symphorian.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symphorian_and_Timotheus","08-22"],"Tarachus":["Early 4th century",null,"Courageous martyrs of the early Church","Saint Tarachus was a martyr who, along with his companions Probus and Andronicus, suffered persecution during the reign of Emperor Diocletian in the year 304. They were known for their unwavering faith and courage in the face of brutal torture and execution. Their acts of martyrdom are recorded as significant contributions to early Christian history, showcasing their steadfast commitment to God despite immense suffering.","/images/saint_tarachus_and_his_companions.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tarachus,_Probus,_and_Andronicus","10-11"],"Tarasius":["8th century",null,"Defender of icons and Church integrity","Tarasius was the Patriarch of Constantinople from 784 to 806, known for his strong defense of the veneration of icons during the Iconoclast controversy. He was a man of great virtue and simplicity, leading a life of prayer and service while refusing to compromise his principles, even in the face of imperial pressure. His legacy includes a commitment to the integrity of the Church and the promotion of holy images, which was affirmed by the Second Council of Nicaea.","/images/saint_tarasius.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tarasios_of_Constantinople","02-25"],"Tarsilla and Emiliana":["6th century",null,"Models of virginity and spiritual perseverance","Tarsilla and Emiliana were two sisters and aunts of Saint Gregory the Great who dedicated their lives to God through virginity. They lived together in a spiritual community, encouraging each other in virtue while mourning their sister Gordiana's return to worldly ways. Their lives exemplify the importance of perseverance in faith and the call to a deeper spiritual life.","/images/saints_tarsilla_and_emiliana.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trasilla_and_Emiliana","12-24"],"Telesphorus":["2nd century",null,"Defender of the faith and liturgical reformer","Telesphorus was a Greek Pope and martyr who served as the Bishop of Rome during the early second century. He is known for his efforts to combat heresy and for instituting certain liturgical practices, including the celebration of Mass on Christmas Eve. Telesphorus was martyred under Emperor Antoninus Pius and is commemorated on January 5.","/images/saint_telesphorus_pope.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pope_Telesphorus","01-05"],"Teresa of Avila":["16th century",["sick","loss of parents","writers","Spain"],"Pioneer of contemplative prayer and reform","Teresa of Avila was a Spanish mystic and reformer of the Carmelite Order, known for her profound spiritual writings and her role in the reform of her religious community. She founded numerous convents and was dedicated to a life of prayer and service, emphasizing the importance of a personal relationship with God. Canonized in 1622, she is recognized as a Doctor of the Church for her contributions to Christian spirituality.","/images/saint_teresa_of_avila.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teresa_of_%C3%81vila","10-15"],"The Assumption of the Blessed Virgin Mary":[null,["heaven","mothers","pregnant women"],"Mother of God and Queen of Saints","The Assumption of Mary commemorates the belief that the Blessed Virgin Mary was taken up into heaven at the end of her earthly life. This event signifies her unique role as the Mother of God and her exaltation above all saints. It is celebrated as a culmination of her virtues and her participation in the divine plan of salvation.","/images/the_assumption_of_the_blessed_virgin_mary.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Assumption_of_Mary","08-15"],"The Baptism of Our Lord":[null,null,"Initiation of Christ's public ministry","The Baptism of Jesus by John the Baptist marks the beginning of His public ministry and is a significant event in Christian theology. During this event, Jesus is anointed by the Holy Spirit, symbolizing His divine mission as the Messiah. This act represents the vicarious nature of His sacrifice, as He takes on the sins of the world and opens the path to salvation for humanity.","/images/the_baptism_of_our_lord.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baptism_of_Jesus","01-13"],"The Beheading of Saint John the Baptist":[null,["baptism","conversions","painters"],"Forerunner of Christ and martyr","John the Baptist is recognized as the precursor to Jesus Christ, heralding His coming and calling for repentance. He led an austere life in the wilderness, emphasizing the need for spiritual purification through baptism. His bold condemnation of Herod's immoral actions ultimately led to his martyrdom by beheading.","/images/the_beheading_of_saint_john_the_baptist.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_the_Baptist","08-29"],"The Circumcision of Our Lord":[null,null,null,"Circumcision was a sacrament of the Old Law, and the first legal observance required of the descendants of Abraham by Almighty God. It was a sacrament of initiation in the service of God, and a promise, an engagement, to believe and act as He had revealed and directed. The law of circumcision continued in force until the death of Christ.","/images/the_circumcision_of_our_lord.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Feast_of_the_Circumcision_of_Christ","01-01"],"The Commemoration of Saint Paul":[null,["theologians","missionaries","gentlemen"],"Apostle to the Gentiles and theologian","Paul, originally known as Saul of Tarsus, was a pivotal figure in early Christianity, known for his conversion from a persecutor of Christians to an Apostle of Christ. He traveled extensively, spreading the Gospel and establishing churches, and is credited with writing fourteen Epistles that form a significant part of the New Testament. His teachings and writings have profoundly influenced Christian doctrine and spirituality throughout the centuries.","/images/the_commemoration_of_saint_paul.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paul_of_Tarsus","06-30"],"The Commemoration of the Faithful Departed":[null,["souls in purgatory","Benedictine monks"],"Champion of prayers for the departed","Saint Odilon of Cluny was a Benedictine abbot known for establishing the commemoration of all the faithful departed, which later became known as All Souls' Day. His efforts in promoting prayers for the dead emphasized the importance of intercession and charity within the Christian faith. Odilon's legacy continues to inspire the faithful to remember and pray for those who have passed away.","/images/commemoration_of_the_faithful_departed.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/All_Souls'_Day","11-02"],"The Conversion of Saint Paul":[null,["missionaries","theologians","gentlemen"],"Apostle to the Gentiles and theologian","Paul, originally known as Saul of Tarsus, was a fervent persecutor of early Christians before experiencing a profound conversion on the road to Damascus. After this transformative encounter with Jesus, he became one of the most influential apostles, spreading the message of Christianity throughout the Roman Empire. His letters to various Christian communities form a significant part of the New Testament, shaping Christian theology and practice.","/images/the_conversion_of_saint_paul.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conversion_of_Paul_the_Apostle","01-25"],"The Dedication of Saint Mary of the Snows":[null,["mothers","childbirth","snow","builders"],"Miraculous foundation of a major basilica","Mary of the Snows, also known as Our Lady of the Snows, commemorates the miraculous event in which the Virgin Mary indicated the site for a church in Rome by covering it with snow. This event led to the construction of the Basilica of Saint Mary Major, one of the oldest and most significant churches dedicated to Mary. The feast is celebrated on August 5, marking the dedication of the basilica and honoring Mary's role as the Mother of God.","/images/the_dedication_of_saint_mary_of_the_snows.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Santa_Maria_Maggiore","08-05"],"The Divine Maternity of Mary":[null,["mothers","pregnant women","the sick","lost causes"],"Mother of God and intercessor for all","Mary, the Mother of Jesus, is revered in Christianity as the Theotokos, or God-bearer, due to her role in the Incarnation. Her acceptance of God's will and her unique grace make her a central figure in the faith, symbolizing purity and maternal love. Mary is often invoked as an intercessor and is seen as a model for all believers in their relationship with God.","/images/the_divine_maternity_of_mary.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solemnity_of_Mary,_Mother_of_God","10-11"],"The Dormition of Mary":[null,["mothers","pregnant women","loss of parents"],"Mother of God and model of faith","Mary, the mother of Jesus, is venerated as a central figure in Christianity, known for her purity and maternal love. Her death is celebrated as the Dormition, reflecting her deep faith and desire to be united with her Son. Mary is regarded as a model for all believers, embodying the virtues of humility and devotion.","/images/the_dormition_of_mary.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dormition_of_the_Mother_of_God","08-13"],"The Epiphany of Our Lord":[null,null,"Celebration of Christ's divine manifestations","Epiphany, also known as Theophany, is a Christian feast day that celebrates the revelation of God incarnate as Jesus Christ. It commemorates the visit of the Magi to the infant Jesus, His baptism in the Jordan River, and His first miracle at the wedding at Cana. This feast highlights the manifestation of Christ to the world and the recognition of His divine nature by both the Gentiles and the faithful.","/images/the_epiphany_of_our_lord.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Epiphany_(holiday)","01-06"],"The Eternal Father":[null,null,"The embodiment of divine fatherhood","The Eternal Father is a title used to refer to God the Father in Christian theology. He is recognized as the creator and sustainer of the universe, embodying love and authority. The concept emphasizes the relationship between the Father and the Son, particularly in the context of the Holy Trinity.","/images/the_eternal_father.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/God_the_Father","01-01"],"The Exaltation of the Holy Cross":[null,null,"Celebration of the triumph of the Cross","The Exaltation of the Holy Cross commemorates the recovery of the True Cross by Emperor Heraclius in 628 AD after it had been taken by the Persians. This event symbolizes the triumph of Christianity over paganism and the importance of the Cross in the faith. The feast serves as a reminder of the significance of the Cross in the lives of believers and the miracles associated with it.","/images/the_exaltation_of_the_holy_cross_of_our_lord_jesus.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exaltation_of_the_Holy_Cross","09-14"],"The Feast of the Holy Redeemer":[null,null,"Redeemer of humanity through His sacrifice","Jesus Christ is the central figure of Christianity, believed by Christians to be the Son of God and the awaited Messiah (Christ) prophesied in the Old Testament. His teachings, crucifixion, and resurrection form the foundation of Christian faith, emphasizing themes of redemption, love, and forgiveness. Through His sacrifice, He offers salvation to humanity, liberating them from sin and death.","/images/feast_of_the_holy_redeemer.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Feast_of_the_Most_Holy_Redeemer","10-23"],"The Feast of the Holy Relics":[null,null,"Veneration of saints through their relics","Holy relics are the physical remains or personal effects of saints, venerated in the Catholic Church. The cult of relics has been a longstanding tradition, believed to connect the faithful with the divine through the intercession of the saints. This practice is rooted in the belief that honoring relics can lead to spiritual benefits and miracles.","/images/feast_of_the_holy_relics.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Holy_relics","11-05"],"The Finding of Saint Stephen's Relics":[null,["deacons","stonemasons","sudden death"],"First martyr and advocate of faith","Saint Stephen is recognized as the first Christian martyr, known for his unwavering faith and powerful preaching. He was stoned to death for his beliefs, as recounted in the Acts of the Apostles. His relics were discovered in 415 AD, leading to a significant festival in his honor and the healing of many afflicted individuals.","/images/the_finding_of_saint_stephens_relics.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stephen_the_Protomartyr","08-03"],"The Finding of the Holy Cross":["4th century",null,null,"When God restored peace to His Church by exalting Constantine the Great to the imperial throne, that pious prince, who had triumphed over his enemies by the miraculous power of the Cross of Christ, was very desirous of expressing his veneration for the holy places which had been honored and sanctified by the presence and sufferings of our blessed Redeemer on earth. He accordingly resolved to build a magnificent church in the city of Jerusalem. Saint Helen, the Emperor's mother, desiring to visit the holy places there, made a journey into Palestine in 326, though she was at that time near eighty years of age.","/images/the_finding_of_the_holy_cross.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/True_Cross","05-03"],"The Holy Guardian Angels":[null,["children","travelers","the sick"],"Divine protectors of every individual","Guardian Angels are believed to be celestial beings assigned to protect and guide individuals throughout their lives. According to Christian doctrine, every person is assigned a Guardian Angel at birth, who watches over them and intercedes on their behalf. The existence of Guardian Angels is a dogma of the Christian faith, emphasizing their role in safeguarding the faithful.","/images/the_holy_guardian_angels.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guardian_angel","10-02"],"The Holy Martyrs of Uganda":["19th century",["youth","catechists","African missions"],"Martyrdom for faith and youth empowerment","Charles Lwanga was a Ugandan martyr who played a pivotal role in the early Christian community in Uganda during the late 19th century. He was a leader among the pages of King Mwanga and was instrumental in teaching catechism to his peers. Lwanga was executed for his faith on June 3, 1886, along with other young Christians, and is celebrated for his courage and commitment to Christianity.","/images/the_holy_martyrs_of_uganda.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_Lwanga","06-03"],"The Holy Name of Mary":[null,null,"Celebration of Mary's intercessory power","The Feast of the Holy Name of Mary is celebrated on September 12, commemorating the intercession of the Blessed Virgin Mary during the siege of Vienna in 1683. This feast was established by Pope Innocent XI to give thanks for the deliverance of the city from Turkish invaders. It highlights the importance of Mary's role in the protection of the Church and the faithful.","/images/feast_of_the_holy_name_of_mary.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Holy_Name_of_Mary","09-12"],"The Holy North American Martyrs":["17th century",["Canada","Indigenous peoples","missionaries"],"Martyrs of faith and dedication","The North American Martyrs are eight Jesuit missionaries who were martyred in the 17th century while evangelizing among Indigenous peoples in North America. They faced extreme hardships and violence, particularly from the Iroquois, as they sought to spread Christianity. Their dedication and sacrifice have made them revered figures in the Catholic Church, symbolizing the commitment to faith in the face of adversity.","/images/the_holy_north_american_martyrs.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_American_Martyrs","09-26"],"The Immaculate Conception":[null,["Catholic Church","pregnant women","children","the United States"],"Mother of God and model of purity","The Immaculate Conception refers to the belief that Mary, the mother of Jesus, was conceived without original sin. This dogma was formally declared by Pope Pius IX in 1854, affirming her purity and special role in salvation history. The feast day is celebrated on December 8, honoring her as a model of holiness and grace.","/images/the_immaculate_conception.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Immaculate_Conception","12-08"],"The Immaculate Heart of Mary":[null,["mothers","families","the sick"],"Symbol of maternal love and intercession","The Immaculate Heart of Mary represents the pure love and compassion of the Virgin Mary for her Son, Jesus Christ, and for all humanity. This devotion emphasizes the importance of Mary's maternal role in the spiritual life of believers and her intercessory power. The message of Fatima in 1917 highlighted the call for devotion to her Immaculate Heart as a means to attain peace and salvation.","/images/the_immaculate_heart_of_mary.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Immaculate_Heart_of_Mary","08-22"],"The Nativity of Our Lord Jesus Christ":[null,null,null,"The Nativity of Our Lord O. pinxit The Nativity of Our Lord Noel! This was the cry of our fathers when the Faith prevailed, ardent and lively in the bosom of families, institutions, and all of society.","/images/the_nativity_of_our_lord.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nativity_of_Jesus","12-25"],"The Nativity of the Blessed Virgin":[null,["mothers","pregnant women","loss of parents","the sick"],"Mother of God and intercessor for all","Mary is revered as the mother of Jesus Christ and holds a central place in Christian theology. She is celebrated for her purity and obedience to God's will, being conceived without original sin. The Nativity of the Blessed Virgin Mary is commemorated as a significant event in the Christian calendar, marking her birth and the beginning of the path to salvation.","/images/the_nativity_of_the_blessed_virgin.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nativity_of_Mary","09-08"],"The Presentation of Jesus in the Temple":[null,["mothers","pregnant women","family","loss of parents"],"Model of faith and humility","Mary is revered as the mother of Jesus Christ and is a central figure in Christianity. Her role in the Presentation of Jesus in the Temple highlights her obedience to Jewish law and her deep faith. She is often honored for her humility and devotion, serving as a model for believers seeking to follow God's will.","/images/the_presentation_of_jesus_in_the_temple.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Presentation_of_Jesus","02-02"],"The Presentation of the Blessed Virgin Mary":[null,["mothers","virgins","the poor","all humanity"],"Model of faith and purity","Mary, the mother of Jesus, is venerated in Christianity as the Mother of God and a model of virtue. According to tradition, she was presented in the Temple at a young age, where she dedicated her life to prayer and service. Her unique sanctity and role in salvation history make her a central figure in Christian devotion and theology.","/images/the_presentation_of_the_blessed_virgin_mary.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Presentation_of_Mary","11-21"],"The Seven Sorrows of the Blessed Virgin Mary":[null,["mothers","sorrows","loss","family"],"Mother of God and model of faith","Mary is revered as the Mother of Jesus Christ and holds a central role in Christianity. She is often depicted as a figure of compassion and sorrow, particularly in relation to the Passion of Christ. Her life exemplifies profound faith and maternal devotion, making her a model for believers.","/images/the_seven_sorrows_of_the_blessed_virgin_mary.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Our_Lady_of_Sorrows","09-15"],"The Stigmata of Saint Francis":[null,["animals","environment","merchants","stowaways"],"Patron saint of animals and ecology","Francis of Assisi was a 13th-century Italian friar and founder of the Franciscan Order, known for his deep love of nature and commitment to poverty. He is most famously recognized for receiving the Stigmata, the wounds of Christ, which marked him as a living reflection of Jesus' suffering. His life and teachings emphasized humility, compassion, and a profound connection to all of creation.","/images/the_stigmata_of_saint_francis.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Francis_of_Assisi","09-17"],"The Visitation of the Blessed Virgin Mary":[null,["mothers","pregnant women","family","travelers"],"Model of faith and motherhood","Mary is revered as the mother of Jesus Christ and holds a central role in Christianity. The Visitation, celebrated on July 2, commemorates her visit to her cousin Elizabeth, during which Elizabeth recognized Mary as the mother of her Lord. This event highlights Mary's humility and her role as an intercessor, as she brings divine grace to those around her.","/images/the_visitation_of_the_blessed_virgin_mary.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Visitation_(Christianity)","07-02"],"Thecla":["1st century",["virgins","theologians","protection against sexual temptation"],"Patroness of virgins and early martyrs","Saint Thecla is recognized as one of the earliest Christian martyrs and is celebrated for her unwavering commitment to virginity and faith. After hearing the teachings of Saint Paul, she rejected her earthly marriage and chose to follow him, enduring severe persecutions for her beliefs. Her legendary trials included surviving wild beasts and fire, ultimately leading to her martyrdom and recognition as a symbol of purity and devotion.","/images/saint_thecla.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thecla","09-23"],"Theodore Tyro":["4th century",["soldiers","martyrs","firefighters"],"A symbol of unwavering faith and courage.","Saint Theodore Tyro was a Christian martyr from Asia Minor who lived during the early 4th century. He was a soldier who refused to renounce his faith in the face of persecution and torture, ultimately being executed for his beliefs. His steadfastness and courage in the face of death made him a celebrated figure among early Christians and a symbol of faithfulness to God.","/images/saint_theodore_tyro.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theodore_of_Amasea","11-09"],"Theodoret":["4th century",null,"Martyrdom for faith and conviction","Saint Theodoret was a Christian martyr who lived during the reign of Emperor Julian the Apostate. He was known for his steadfast faith and was tortured for his beliefs before being executed by the sword. His courage in the face of persecution serves as an inspiration to Christians facing trials for their faith.","/images/saint_theodoret.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theodoret_(martyr)","10-23"],"Theodosius":["5th-6th century",["monks","hospitals","the sick"],"A model of charity and humility","Saint Theodosius was a prominent abbot and founder of monastic communities in Palestine during the 5th and 6th centuries. Known for his deep charity and humility, he built a large monastery and several hospitals, providing for the poor and sick. He successfully opposed heretical teachings and was revered for his piety and leadership among the religious communities of his time.","/images/saint_theodosius.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theodosius_the_Cenobiarch","01-11"],"Thomas":["1st century",["architects","builders","India","doubters"],"From doubt to faith, a powerful witness.","Thomas was one of the twelve apostles of Jesus, known for his initial doubt about Christ's resurrection. He famously declared, 'My Lord and my God!' upon seeing the risen Christ, symbolizing the journey from doubt to faith. After spreading the Gospel in various regions, he ultimately became a martyr in India, where he is revered as a foundational figure in the Christian community there.","/images/saint_thomas.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_the_Apostle","12-21"],"Thomas Aquinas":["Medieval",["students","universities","philosophers"],"Father of Scholasticism and Angelic Doctor","Thomas Aquinas was a 13th-century Dominican friar and theologian, renowned for his influential works in philosophy and theology. He is best known for his synthesis of Aristotelian philosophy with Christian doctrine, particularly in his seminal work, the 'Summa Theologica'. Recognized as a Doctor of the Church, he is celebrated for his profound insights into faith and reason, and his writings continue to shape Catholic thought today.","/images/saint_thomas_aquinas.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Aquinas","03-07"],"Thomas Becket":["12th century",["archbishops","martyrs","England"],"Defender of Church rights and martyr","Thomas Becket was the Archbishop of Canterbury and a martyr who stood against King Henry II's attempts to exert control over the Church. His unwavering commitment to ecclesiastical rights led to his assassination in 1170, making him a symbol of the struggle for religious freedom. He was canonized by Pope Alexander III in 1173, just a few years after his death, and is remembered for his defense of the Church's authority.","/images/saint_thomas_becket.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Becket","12-29"],"Thomas More":["Renaissance",["politicians","civil servants"," lawyers","martyrs"],"Defender of faith and conscience.","Thomas More was an English lawyer, philosopher, and author, best known for his book 'Utopia'. He served as Lord Chancellor of England but resigned in opposition to King Henry VIII's separation from the Catholic Church. More was executed for refusing to acknowledge the king's supremacy over the Church, and he is celebrated as a martyr and saint in the Catholic Church.","/images/saint_thomas_more.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_More","07-06"],"Thomas of Villanova":["16th century",["orphans","the poor","hospitals"],"Champion of the poor and social reformer","Thomas of Villanova was a Spanish bishop known for his deep compassion for the poor and his commitment to social justice. He transformed his home into a hospital and dedicated his wealth to charitable works, feeding hundreds of needy individuals daily. His legacy as a reformer and a humble servant of God continues to inspire many in the Catholic Church.","/images/saint_thomas_of_villanova.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_of_Villanova","09-22"],"Thérèse of Lisieux":["Modern",["missions","florists","sick people"],"A model of simplicity and love for God.","Thérèse of Lisieux, also known as the Little Flower, was a French Carmelite nun whose simple yet profound spirituality emphasized love and trust in God. She is best known for her autobiography, 'Story of a Soul,' which outlines her 'little way' of spiritual childhood and her desire to serve God through small acts of love. Canonized in 1925, she is now recognized as a Doctor of the Church and the patroness of missions.","/images/saint_therese_of_the_child_jesus.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Th%C3%A9r%C3%A8se_of_Lisieux","10-03"],"Tiburtius":["3rd century",null,"Martyrdom for unwavering faith in God","Saint Tiburtius was a Christian martyr and the son of the Roman prefect Agrestius Chromatius. He was baptized alongside his father and later ordained as a subdeacon, known for his miraculous acts, including raising a man from the dead. Tiburtius was ultimately martyred under the persecution of Diocletian for his unwavering faith in God.","/images/saint_tiburtius.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Tiburtius","08-11"],"Timothy":["1st century",["brewers","sick persons","students"],"A devoted disciple and early church leader.","Timothy was a companion and disciple of Saint Paul, who became the first Bishop of Ephesus. He is known for his dedication to spreading Christianity and for his role in guiding early Christian communities. Timothy ultimately became a martyr, killed while trying to calm a riot in honor of the goddess Diana in Ephesus.","/images/saint_timothy.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Timothy","01-24"],"Titus":["1st century",["Crete","clergy","administrators"],"Peacemaker and faithful disciple of Paul","Saint Titus was a Greek-speaking convert and a disciple of Saint Paul, accompanying him on various apostolic missions. He played a crucial role in addressing issues within the Corinthian church and was later appointed as the bishop of Crete. His legacy is marked by his administrative skills and his ability to inspire respect and joy among the faithful.","/images/saint_titus.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Titus","02-06"],"Transfiguration of Our Lord":["1st century",null,"A glimpse of divine glory revealed","The Transfiguration of Our Lord is a significant event in the New Testament where Jesus reveals His divine glory to three of His apostles: Peter, James, and John. This event occurs on a high mountain, traditionally identified as Mount Tabor, where Jesus' appearance changes, and He converses with Moses and Elijah. The voice of God affirms Jesus as His beloved Son, emphasizing His authority and the importance of listening to Him.","/images/the_transfiguration_of_our_lord.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transfiguration_of_Jesus","08-06"],"Turribius":["17th century",null,"Archbishop of Lima","Saint Turribius, Archbishop of Lima was born on the 6th of November, 1538, at Mayorga, in the kingdom of Leon in Spain. Brought up in a pious family, where devotion was hereditary, his youth was a model to all who knew him. A tender devotion to the Blessed Virgin and a love of the poor marked this boy.","/images/saint_turribius_of_lima.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Turibius_of_Mogrovejo","03-23"],"Urban V":["Medieval",null,"Pope dedicated to reform and humility","Urban V was a Pope known for his efforts to return the Papacy from Avignon to Rome. He was dedicated to reforming morals and spreading the Catholic faith. His life exemplified humility and devotion, as he lived simply and directed all glory to God.","/images/blessed_urban_v.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Urban_V","12-19"],"Ursula":["4th century",["young teachers","virgins","students"],"Martyrdom and protection of virginity","Saint Ursula was a British princess and martyr who led a group of eleven thousand virgins to their deaths rather than submit to dishonor. She is celebrated for her courage and commitment to her faith, having vowed her life to Jesus Christ. Her legacy is particularly honored in Cologne, where many churches house relics of her and her companions, attracting pilgrims seeking protection and intercession.","/images/saint_ursula_and_her_companions.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Ursula","10-21"],"Valentine":["3rd century",["lovers","beekeepers","epileptics","greeting card manufacturers"],"Patron saint of love and affection","Valentine was a priest in Rome who became a martyr during the persecution under Emperor Claudius II around the year 268. Known for his courage, he openly preached the Christian faith and performed miracles, including restoring sight to a blind girl. His legacy as a symbol of love and devotion has led to his celebration on February 14, which is now widely recognized as Valentine's Day.","/images/saint_valentine.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Valentine_of_Rome","02-14"],"Valery":["6th century",null,"Miracle worker and monastic founder","Saint Valery was a 6th-century monk and abbot known for his piety and miraculous works. He initially studied under a teacher and later joined the monastery of Saint Anthony before moving to Luxeuil to live under Saint Columban. Valery eventually established a monastic community in the wilderness of Leuconaus, where he performed miracles and became renowned for his sanctity before his death in 619.","/images/saint_valery.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Walaric","12-12"],"Venantius of Camerino":["3rd century",null,"Martyr","Saint Venantius, born at Camerino in Italy, during the persecution of Decius was taken into custody at the age of fifteen years as a Christian who was preaching Christ to others. His history is one of the most miraculous in the annals of the early martyrs. Having learned that he was about to be arrested, he presented himself to the governor of Camerino, Antiochus, at the city gates, and said to him that the lives of the gods were filled with every kind of crime, that there was only one God, whose unique Son had become a man to deliver his fellow humans from the tyranny of sin.","/images/saint_venantius_of_camerino.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Venantius_of_Camerino","05-18"],"Veronica of Milan":["15th century",["the sick","painters","spirituality"],"A model of humility and devotion","Saint Veronica of Milan was a devoted virgin and lay-sister known for her deep spirituality and commitment to her religious duties. Despite her physical sufferings, she dedicated her life to serving her community and praying for others. Her life was marked by visions and ecstasies, reflecting her profound connection to the Passion of Christ.","/images/saint_veronica_of_milan.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Veronica_of_Milan","01-13"],"Victor of Marseille":["3rd century",["martyrs","soldiers","prisoners"],"Courageous martyr and defender of faith","Victor of Marseille was a Christian officer in the Roman army who became a martyr for his faith during the reign of Emperor Maximian around the year 290. He courageously defied the emperor's orders to worship pagan gods, enduring severe torture and ultimately death for his beliefs. His legacy includes numerous miracles attributed to his intercession, particularly at his burial site, where many faithful sought his aid.","/images/saint_victor_of_marseille.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Victor_of_Marseilles","07-21"],"Victorian and Companions":["5th century",null,"Martyrs","Huneric, the Arian king of the Vandals in Africa, succeeded his father Genseric in 477. He acted at first with moderation towards the Catholics of Carthage, but in 480 began a grievous persecution of the clergy and holy virgins, which in 484 became general. Vast numbers of Catholics were put to death.","/images/saint_victorian_and_companions.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Victorian,_Frumentius_and_Companions","03-23"],"Vincent Ferrer":["Medieval",["builders","plumbers","the sick","against dying unprepared"],"Apostle of conversion and miraculous preacher","Vincent Ferrer was a Spanish Dominican missionary known for his powerful preaching and miraculous works. He dedicated his life to converting sinners and bringing the light of Christ to various communities across Europe during a time of great schism in the Church. His legacy includes the establishment of numerous religious institutions and a profound impact on the spiritual lives of thousands.","/images/saint_vincent_ferrer.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vincent_Ferrer","04-05"],"Vincent Liem de la Paz":["18th century",["Vietnam","martyrs"],"Martyrdom for faith in Vietnam","Vincent Liem de la Paz was a Dominican priest and martyr from Vietnam, born in 1732. After completing his studies in the Philippines, he returned to Vietnam to preach the Catholic faith. He was arrested and executed for his beliefs on November 7, 1773, and is venerated as a saint in the Catholic Church.","/images/saint_vincent_liem.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vincent_Liem","11-07"],"Vincent Strambi":["18th century",["clergy","spiritual formation","the poor"],"A life of prayer and service.","Vincent Strambi was a Passionist priest and bishop known for his deep commitment to prayer and service to the poor. He dedicated his life to preaching and pastoral care, emphasizing the importance of tailored instruction for different professions. Canonized in 1950, he is remembered for his humility, simplicity, and dedication to the spiritual formation of his followers.","/images/saint_vincent_strambi.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vincent_Strambi","09-25"],"Vincent de Paul":["17th century",["charity","volunteers","hospital workers"],"Champion of the poor and marginalized","Vincent de Paul was a French priest known for his deep commitment to serving the poor and marginalized. He founded the Congregation of the Mission and the Daughters of Charity, which continue to serve those in need today. His life exemplified the Christian call to charity and humility, making him a beloved figure in Catholicism.","/images/saint_vincent_de_paul.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vincent_de_Paul","07-19"],"Vincent of Saragossa":["4th century",["vine growers","winemakers","st. Vincent de Paul Society"],"Martyrdom and unwavering faith in Christ","Saint Vincent of Saragossa was a deacon and martyr who served in the early Church during the persecution of Diocletian. He was known for his unwavering faith and courage in the face of brutal torture, ultimately dying for his beliefs. His relics are preserved in Lisbon, and he is venerated as a protector of the faithful.","/images/saint_vincent_of_saragossa.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vincent_of_Saragossa","01-22"],"Vitalis of Ravenna":["2nd century",null,"Martyr","Saint Vitalis was a first century Christian citizen of Milan and the father of the twin brothers and future martyrs, Saints Gervasius and Protasius. He is the principal patron of Ravenna, where he was martyred. Divine providence had conducted him to that city, where he saw come before the tribunal there a Christian physician named Ursicinus, who had been tortured and who then was condemned to lose his head for his faith.","/images/saint_vitalis_of_ravenna.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vitalis_of_Milan","04-28"],"Vitus":["4th century",["dancers","actors","comedians","children","horses"],"Patron of dancers and actors","Saint Vitus was a young Christian martyr who lived during the early 4th century. He is known for his unwavering faith in the face of persecution, famously refusing to renounce his beliefs despite severe torture. Along with his companions Modestus and Crescentia, he is celebrated for his miraculous deeds and steadfast devotion to God.","/images/saints_vitus_modestus_and_crescentia.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Vitus","06-15"],"Wenceslas":["10th century",["Czech Republic","difficult marriages","peasants"],"Martyrdom and devotion in leadership","Wenceslas was a Christian Duke of Bohemia who became a martyr for his faith in the 10th century. Raised by his pious grandmother, he ruled with devotion and compassion, often serving the poor in secret. His reign ended tragically when he was murdered by his brother, but he is remembered as a symbol of Christian virtue and leadership.","/images/saint_wenceslas.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wenceslaus_I,_Duke_of_Bohemia","09-28"],"Wilfrid":["7th century",["York","spiritual leaders","monks"],"Champion of Roman Catholicism in England","Wilfrid was an influential Archbishop of York who played a key role in aligning the English Church with Roman practices. He founded several monasteries and was known for his strong advocacy of Roman Catholic discipline and doctrine. Despite facing exile and imprisonment, he remained steadfast in his mission to reform the Church in England until his death in 709.","/images/saint_wilfrid.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wilfrid_of_York","10-12"],"William of Bourges":["Medieval",null,"A model of piety and humility","William of Bourges was an Archbishop known for his deep piety and commitment to a life of austerity. He initially served as a canon before joining the Cistercians and later became the Archbishop of Bourges, where he focused on leading a holy life and performing penance for others. His tomb became a site of numerous miracles, leading to his canonization just nine years after his death.","/images/saint_william.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_of_Bourges","01-10"],"William of Vercelli":["12th century",["monks","pilgrims"],"Founder of the Congregation of Monte Vergine","William of Vercelli was an Italian abbot and the founder of the Congregation of Monte Vergine. After a life of pilgrimage and contemplation, he established a monastic community dedicated to austere living and devotion to the Virgin Mary. His legacy includes the continued veneration of a portrait of the Virgin at the monastery, which attracts many pilgrims.","/images/saint_william_of_vercelli.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_of_Vercelli","06-25"],"Willibrord":["7th century",["Friesland","missionaries","educators"],"Apostle of the Frisians and educator","Saint Willibrord was an English missionary who became the first Archbishop of Utrecht and played a crucial role in spreading Christianity among the Frisians in the Low Countries. He founded several churches and schools, and was known for his miracles and prophetic gifts. His dedication to the faith and tireless efforts in evangelization earned him a revered place in the history of the Church.","/images/saint_willibrord.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Willibrord","11-07"],"Wulfran":["Medieval",["missionaries","Friesland"],"A compassionate missionary and miracle worker.","Saint Wulfran was an Archbishop of Sens who renounced worldly greatness to serve God and the Church. He became a missionary in Friesland, where he baptized many and performed miracles, including saving a boy from sacrifice. His efforts helped to establish Christianity in a pagan land, and he is remembered for his compassion and dedication to the faith.","/images/saint_wulfran.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wulfram_of_Sens","03-20"],"Yves":["14th century",null,"Confessor","Saint Yves Helori, descended from a noble and virtuous family near Treguier in Brittany, was born in 1253. At fourteen years of age he went to Paris, and afterwards to Orleans to pursue his studies. His mother was accustomed to say to him often that he ought to live in such a way as became a Saint, to which his answer always was that he indeed hoped to be one.","/images/saint_yves.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ivo_of_Kermartin","05-19"],"Zachary":["8th century",null,"Pope","Saint Zachary, a native of Syria, was a Benedictine monk before being created a Cardinal-priest by Saint Gregory II. He succeeded to Pope Gregory III in 741, and was a man of singular meekness and goodness. He was elected at a time of troubles besetting all of Italy, during an invasion of Rome under the military conduct of the Lombardian king Luitprand.","/images/saint_zachary.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pope_Zachary","03-15"],"Zephyrinus":["3rd century",null,"Defender of Christ's divinity and faith","Saint Zephyrinus was a Pope and martyr who led the Church during a time of severe persecution under Emperor Septimus Severus. He is remembered for his steadfast defense of the faith against heresies and for his compassionate leadership of his flock. Zephyrinus governed the Church for nineteen years before dying as a martyr around the year 217.","/images/saint_zephyrinus.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pope_Zephyrinus","08-26"],"Zita":["Medieval",["servants","household workers","lost causes"],"Patroness of servants and workers","Saint Zita was a 13th-century virgin and servant from Lucca, Italy, known for her unwavering faith and dedication to prayer. Despite facing mistreatment from her employers and fellow servants, she remained patient and kind, ultimately gaining their respect and admiration. Her life exemplifies the virtues of hard work and devotion, leading to her recognition as a saint after her death in 1278.","/images/saint_zita.jpg","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zita_of_Lucca","04-27"]};